        <p>sea, at about the distance of half a league over our port quarter, a great sheet of pale blue fire. I can best liken it to the cascading effulgence of a vast firework, of the species known as Roman candles. It came and went in a breath, flashing out the sea and sky around La vivid but ghastly hues, with the shape of our ship, and the silent, staring figures upon her deck, as plain to the view as at noontide. And the night rolled down upon our eyes black as a wolf's throat after the dazzling glare of that astonishing outburst.</p>
        <p>Not a sound followed: the detonation which preceded the leaping up of that sheet of flame was evidently the noise of the submarine explosion, When the blinding effect of the flash had passed. and my vision was clear twain, I thought I perceived a dark smudge out in the gloom where the singular phenomenon had appeared. At first I conjectured it to be a pall of smoke, but gazing earnestly, it looked too dense a mass for this. , The better to view it, I stepped on to the bulwark rail, holding tightly by a rope to steady myself. Suddenly the vessel gave a terrific lurch as a mighty, silent, black wall of water came brimming to her sides. Over and yet over she heeled; before I could recover my poise, my feet slipped from under me, and, with a cry of horror, I felt the rope yield to my weight as I swung off upon it. Then, with a twang, it tautened, and was torn from my grasp as I soused into the water. My dive was long and deep. Happily, I was a good swimmer, and, notwithstanding the shock of my nudden immersion, my presence of mind was not paralysed. I kept my lips pursed, and waited for my head to rise again. Curiously enough, but one fear possessed 'me at the instant of going overboard: a terror that, flashed into my brain with the first chill of my plunge—the dread of sharks. The brief seconds seemed to run into eternity before I came to the surface again. I then trod the water, whilst I squeezed the brine out of my eyes and drew a deep breath. It was some little while before my vision grew clear • and, naturally, my first thought was to find the ;hip. With that swift process of reasoning which flits through a man's brain in the moment of peril, I concluded it was very unlikely that my mishap had been observed in the darkness and amid the consterna- tion caused by the frightful lurching of the vessel. Therefore, no effort would be made to pick me up. I looked around me for a moment or two, lightly breasting the phosphorescent water and noticing with dismay the increasing drag of my saturated clothes. Where was the ship ? Father of mercy! Had she foundered ? No, yonder she loomed • a solitary light twinkling aboard of her, and looking so remote that I marvelled at the distance which those few moments had put between us. To be sure, the wash Of that great volcanic wave. may have carried her along with it. I strum out for her, but I had not swum a dozen strokes when I came in. contact with.some large, heavy, floating object. I laid bold of it, and after groping it over, found it to be a hatch grating, which must have fetched away and gone overboard when the vessel heeled. I dragged myself upon it, Ind squatted, Lascar fashion: it easily bore my weight like a raft. I tried to shout, but found my voice so weak that I could raise no more noise than a husky rattling in my throat. This could only have been due to the effect of the shock, for I had not swallowed a spoonful of water in my plunge. It was at this moment that I felt a gentle fanning of air in my face. A faint breeze was ruffling the water; it came to my nostrils heavily laden with the fumes of sulphur. For a few moments the significance of this did not occur to me, as I crouched upon the grating, designing to rest before. swimming to the ship. I kept my eye upon her shadowy form, with the single feeble light glimmering aboard; and then, with the perception that her outline was growing more vague, as though a deeper shade of gloom rolled around her, it flashed upon me that this breeze was slowly but steadily bearing the vessel away. Curiously enough, on realising my perilous plight, my mood immediately changed to one of . hopefulness. My providential encounter with the grating not only rescued me from the prospect of irowning, but put me out of reach of sharks. The sea was as fiat as a mirror, and the breeze at present little more than a warm catspaw. I argued with myself that there was every chance of its expiring again, and leaving the barque motionless during the remainder of the night. But even if it ,aeld steady, providing it did not increase in weight, t knew it. would not waft the Lodmore out of the circle of my horizon before dawn: long ere this they would have discovered' that I was missing, and in sweeping the ocean around with the glass, they would, of course, perceive me. Wlnlst I thus reasoned, sitting with my chin in my hands and gazing about me, I.ny eye was suddenly taken by the big dark mass I iwie before spoken of, which seemed to follow the wonderful upheaval of flame from out of the ocead. I peered at it fixedly, and soon concluded that it was not so far distant as I had at first supposed. The gentle wind was blowing directly towards it, and I knew from the fact that the barque was slowly melting into the darkness, that she must be bearing away close-hauled. Doubtless Captain Renshaw would take advantage of this little slant to give the mysterious, shadowy heap as wide an offing as possible. Well, the night wore on, the Lodmore vanishing entirely about two hours after I had fallen over- board, as near as my fancy could measure the time. The moon rose about twelve o'clock. soaring above the sea-line like a great ruddy Chinese lantern glowing through a mist, but speedily brightening into pale gold. The breeze freshened a little about this time, and the water began to babble softly among the square apertures of the grating. I' searched tilt ocean eagerly for the barque when it stole out in silvered violet under the lunar dawn; but though I fancied I perceived her in the north- east, I could not be sure. _ _ _</p>
        <p>I ' it, j , II 4"; , L .</p>
        <p>Meanwhile the rising of the moon revealed the outline of the strange mass close by, and I saw it was a conical point of land, like the peak of a mountain. I judged it to be of a pale colour, for it shewed ashy in the moonbeams, like a snow- clad hill viewed through smoked glass. How high it was, I could not then determine in the illusive light: I put it down at about a hundred feet, but I afterwards found that it rose to thrice that altitude. The warm soft wind continued quietly to push me towards it, for which I was thankful, for such an amazing and conspicuous object rearing itself out of the ocean was bound to attract the attention of any passing vessel,- •even if the Lodmore should not return to investigate this fire- born island.</p>
        <p>Tillie passed more quickly than I should have believed to one in such a trying situ4ion, and the sickly greenish hues of dawn were beginning to spread over the heavens in the east when the grating bumped lightly against the smooth sloping surface of the island. The beach or foreground was fairly steep-to, yet not so much so but that it offered a tolerable foothold. I stepped off the grating, and, bethinking .me that I might require this again, I pulled it up high and dry. The stuff crunched and crackled under my tread, but was perfectly firm. I stooped and put my hand to the ground; it was hot, but not uncomfortably so. The light broadened rapidly. As the island grew plain to view, it assumed the appearance of a vast mass of pumice-stone lying upon the breast of the ocean: indeed,. I can liken it to nothing which it so closely resembled as the substance I have named. In colour it was about the hue of meerschaum; the Whole surface of it was as clean as an egg-shell without a grain of calcined dust, or any signs of scorim anywhere visible upon it. It was honey- combed with holes, but all very small and shallow: in short, I suppose it presented the ordinary appearance of what it undoubtedly was, namely, volcanic lava. I walked slowly and cautiously up the steep incline, for I knew not whether the crust was hollow and might yield to my weight at any step. The warmth of the mass was comforting to me, for I had been in my wet clothes all night, and was chilled to the bone despite the heat of those tropic parallels. But I had not been ashore .five minutes before may garments were steaming upon me, and in less than half-an-hour they were perfectly dry, - I was so much taken up in staring about me as I trudged up the side of this extraordinary fire-born pinnacle, whose base rested upon the bed of the ocean at least. four miles deep, that the rosy beams of the coming sunrise had shot above the horizon before it occurred to me to .look for the Lodmore. By this time I daresay I had climbed to seventy or eighty feet above the level of the sea, which flowed in a wide, blue expanse on every hand of me. As soon as I came to.a stand, and looked towards that quarter where I guessed the barque would be, I spied her at once. She looked to be dwarfed to the proportions of a mere toy, her canvas gleaming like ivory in the early mornihg 'light, but I doubt if she was more than five miles away. I reckoned that the ship's telescope would be brought to bear upon this strange upheaval by first one and then another on board, and, pullirg off my coat, I waved it and flung it into the air until my arms fairly ached, in the hope that they would perceWe me.</p>
        <p>With the coming of the sun, the gentle breeze expired, and the ocean became like a sheet of new tin in the glistening wake of the orb. The thought of thirst in the fierce beating of those dazzling rays upon the unprotected isle sent a shudder through me, and I again cast my eyes towards the Lodmore. But she was too far distant for me to perceive any !iglu of movement on board of her. I saw that iver head still bore away towards the north-east; it the fact that she did not change-her course was no indication that they did not see me, since she was now becalmed, and without steerage way. Whilst I stood watching her, the island suddenly began to tremble under my feet. The sensation was very similar to what we had experienced upon the deck of the Lodmore on the preceding night; the feeling it imparted was for all the world like the tottering of a man about to fall in a swoon. It might have lasted for twenty seconds: I cannot say; but this much I know, that it created a terror In me of such another upheaval of flame as that in the heart of which this wonderful creation had been borne above the face of the deep. I do not believe I am naturally a very timid man, but that notion took hold of my fancy, and threw me into a very frenzy of apprehension. Heedless of the risk of crashing through the outer crust, into some terrible abyss beneath, I broke into a run down the slope, crazy to leap upon the grating, and push off clear of the island. A little reflection would have told me that I could be no better off there, should there follow such another terrific eruption as that of the night. But I was too panic- stricken to reason: I raced down the crunching steep as hard as I could, then suddenly came to a halt with a shout of dismay. The grating was afloat. The square of it lay stirless upon the calm water, about six yards out, from where it lapped the pumice-stone beach. This would have been nothing to me as a good swimmer; but close alongside, hovering in the transparent brine, was the hideous shape of a great cuttle fish, or octopus; its long suckers or arms writhing slowly in sinuous folds all about its body. I stood watching, and breathing hard. Trfere was something fascinating in the spectacle of that horrible marine monster. I could plainly see its large globular eyes, apparently fixed full upon me; and, at last, unable to stand it, I kicked off a big houlder of the lava and hurled it at the creature. The sea was lashed into an eddy of foam, and when the seething had settled down into clear water again, the cuttle-fish was gone. But after such an apparition, I was not going to take to the water again. How had the grating got adrift, I wondered? I had pulled it up well clear of the sea, and there could be no rising tide out in the breast of the Indian Ocean to carry it off. Then another startling thought occurred to me: the trembling of the island had been accompanied by a subsidence of the whole body of it, and my grating had been left afloat by the ground settling from beneath it. I looked earnestly towards the raft. Yes, there was no question about it: through the glass-clear water between it and the shore, I could plainly discern the marks of my footprints upon the crumbling white lava, where I had just trodden. The sudden panic which had seized me now gave place to a sort of dull, dogged sense of despair. At any minute the island might plunge back into the depths from which it had emerged, and the vision of that frightful cuttle-fish became the central horror of this appalling possibility. I retraced my steps up the slope again to look towards the Lodmore, and see whether there appeared any prospect of succour coming to me. I had not walked far when I stopped to look backwards, and then I uttered a shout of joy. For, from the elevation I had gained, I saw a boat pulling towards the island, the water winking in myriads of sparks to the rise and fall of the oars. She was a long way off, but the sight of her gave me back all my stoutness of heart, and, folding my arms, I sat me quietly down upon a little hummock of the island to await her coming. The sun had now mounted well above the horizon, and -the glare of the white lava drew tears from my eyes. My nerves were strained to a fearful tension by the momentary expectation of feeling a recurrence of the trembling sensation, accompanied, perhaps, by a sudden collapse, which would leave me with a vortex of waters seething about my ears in the space of a single heart-beat. But all remained silent and stirless as the grave. A huge and stately sea-bird, after hovering above my head for several minutes, dropped gently upon its out- stretched wings, and perched within a dozen yards of me. I called to it and extended my hand; it eyed me attentively, but did not offer to move. I then rose and walked towards it: so tame was the magnificent creature that it allowed me to come and stroke its downy feathers. Probably it had never seen a man before.</p>
        <p>_ It was now grown intolerably hot, and I was beginning to feel parched in the throat. But the boat was steadily approaching: she was now so near that I could make out the figures of her four oarsmen, with the chief mate in the stern sheets. Again I flourished my jacket, and the mate waved his cap in response. I made my way down to the water's edge, the big sea-bird actually waddling down the slope after me. A few minutes later the stem of the boat grated upon the lava, and Mr. Matthews jumped out. "Well, well !" he exclaimed, staring about him open-mouthed. "Who ever saw the like of this before? Apparently the creation isn't finished yet. A nice sort of object this would be to heave up right under the keel of a ship! I'm glad to see you're all right, sir. We never reckoned upon seeing you any more when we discovered you were missing, which wasn't for about an hour after that confounded tidal wave had rolled away." I clambered into the boat.</p>
        <p>"Stop you as long as you like on this island," said I. "For my part, I've had enough of it. The confounded thing may founder at any moment, and carry us along with it. Why, it's settled down several feet since I've been on it."</p>
        <p>"Is that so ? " said he, scrambling inboards over the gunwale. "Then we'll clear out as soon as we can. Shove off, my lads! Stop and pick up that grating as we go, and then we'll make ail haste to give this confounded rock a wide berth." In due course we returned to the Lodmore.</p>
        <p>Captain Henshaw told me that through the spy- glass he had plainly seen me waving my jacket, soon after daylight. "We've all been cursing this calm weather," said he, "but it's been the means of saving your life on this occasion. Had there been anything of a breeze during the night, ww should have put yonder rock below the horizon before the dawn came along." I stepped below for something to eat, and then went to my berth to turn in, not having closed my oyes throughout the whole preceding night. I slept until sundown, for, indeed, I was more exhausted by my singular experience than I was perhaps conscious of. When I came on deck again, I found the calm as breathless as ever. Away in the south hung the island, shewing purple in the glow of the sunset. Well, throughout the night that interminable rahn lasted. The skipper was very uneasy, and kept coming and going throughout the hours of darkness, fearing, I suppose, a repetition of the astonishing experience of flame and volcanic wave. But never a sound or movement broke the dead hush. I did not turn out till the sun had risen to twice a man's height above the horizon. Captain Henshaw was standing close to the companion-hatch as I stepped up on deck. "The island's gone!" said he, on catching sight of me. "Gone?" I exclaimed. "Aye," he answered. "flow or when nobody aboard this vessel can tell. 'We've not stirred by so much as our own length all night long. You can see that by looking at those spud peelings along- side, which the cook threw overboard yesterday. There was nothing in sight at dawn this morning. Blessed if this traverse doesn't clean beat all my going a-fishing ! Tell you what, sir: you're a bit of a fist at writing yarns, aren't you ? Well, sit down and tell 'em the true story of that fire-tossed island. No need to dramatise it, as I believe you literary gentlemen term it: I allow the naked facts are queer enough in themselves. There's only one thing about it, perhaps," he added, fishing in his pockets for his pipe. "Nobody short of a sailor- man would ever believe it." rTHE END. 1</p>
        <p>SUICIDE OF A ST. HELENS PUBMCAN</p>
        <p>Mr. Ga—skell, licensee of the Lord Nelson Arms, Parr Stock-road, St. Helens, hanged hinisblf in the wash- house on Saturday.</p>
        <p>AGENTS Josepli Beswick, Horsemarket St., WARRINGTON. W. SHAW Jun. Top Locke, Runcorn. S. JEAVONS. 21. George St., St. Ifeien,. CULSHAW, 38 Bur tcougb St., Ortnekirk. AND OF ALL TOBACCONIDT3.</p>
        <p>Feis-Naptha</p>
        <p>Nobody takes the money. Nobody wants the money. Nobo.ly takes the money. Nobody wants the money.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha 39 Wilson street London E C</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>FIRE ISLAND.</head>
        <p>THE FIRE ISLAND.</p>
        <p>BY HERBERT RUSSELL,</p>
        <p>Author of "Elmira," "True Blue," "The • Piccaroon," "The Burning Sea," "The Treasure of the Temple," "The Mystery of Whitelady Island," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Our passage from the Thames to the Cape al Good Hope had been an uncommonly good one. Few and brief indeed were the occasions upon whieb our nimble little clipper hung "like a painted ship apon a painted ocean," and day after day the course prickincrs on the chart regularly shewed long stretches of 'blue brine left astern. And oh the exhilarating effect of those salt and piping winds upon my emaciated frame, worn to a mere shadow by many weary weeks of feverish tossing upon a siCk bed. What is there in this wide world to gladden the spirit and send the joy of new life thrilling through the veins if it be not the humming sea-breeze, breaking into a thousand shrill, hoarse songs as it pours through the rigging of the leaning ship ?_ _ _</p>
        <p>But once I:ouna Agulhas our luck seemed to desert us. The wind grew shy and fickle, and our progress became little more than a stealthy sneaking over the scarcely blurred circle of blue. The ruddy face of the skipper grew long, as morning after morning he came on deck and found that this baffling weather was fast destroying all the hopes of a swift passage which the earlier portion of our voyage had raised. For my own part, I viewed these halcyon days with indifference as to the delay they were causing. To be sure the unbroken scene of silver shining water, brimming to the dazzling sky, grew a trifle tedious at times. But my sole mission was the quest of health, and with ample leisure it mattered not to me whether our run in the twenty-four hours was two hundred miles or whether it was only twenty. Our vessel was the Lodmore, a trim little barque of seven hundred and eighty tons. We were bound from London to Rangoon. I was the only passenger on board, viewed, apparently, as a great natural curiosity by the Jacks forward, who could never quite reconcile the Idea of my perfect sanity with my choice of a sailing ship in preference to one of the fine steamers regularly plying upon the route. "I have told them you are going out for your health," said the second mate to me with a laugh; "and the bo'sun said so he should reckon, at the same time touching his forehead with an air of pity and contempt. Well, by dint of boxhauling our yards to every catspaw, we crept stealthily into the parallel of Madagascar. I cannot give you the exact latitude and longitude of our position at the date of my story, for although I had been a sailor in my time I was not sufficiently concerned in the navigation of our barque to carry her daily reckonings in my head. It was December 14th, 1892, the period of the year when the limit of the southern sun-swing threw very little shadow upon our decks at nooni The memory of that patticular day lingers with cameo-like clearness in my brain. I know not why it should either, for it was merely a repetition.of the hot and calm days which had preceded it, except that perhaps nature was more hushed and stifling than she had yet been. Still, every trifling incident, every scrap of conversation with the captain and mates, recurs to me with extraordinary vividness when I recall that 14th of December nine years ago. The Lodruore lay stirless all day in the heart of the breathless stillness, her tall, shining spires of canvas seeming to writhe sunwards through the invisible vapours drawn up by the heat. Captain Henshaw, in his shirt-sleeves and Panama hat, paced the deck irritably throughout the 'day, and I remember his giving vent to a vigorous appreciation of Vanderdecken, saying that if a sailor was to be cursed for blaspheming at adverse weather the whole ocean ought to be alive with Flying Dutch- men. I have witnessed some remarkable effects at sea in my time, as any man who follows the life necessarily must; but never can I recall anything to equal the sunset which brought this day to a close. There was a something in this piling up of scarlet grandeur to the very zenith almost which held every man of us on board silent and staring. Figure the western heavens alslaze with gushinas of hot purple—arrested whirl- pools of crimson 'haze is the image which best describes the marvellous effect—with the rayless, palpitating orb .dipping into a sea of blood, and flushing the sky down to the far east in hues of gold and orange, till the radiance fainted out in pale amber. When the upper limb of the sun had vanished, however, the gorgeous. effulgence died out of the sky with the swiftness peculiar to tropical climes. The night was gloriously clear. I never remember such a myriad of stars as twinkled in the deep indigo, with the familiar galaxy of the Southern Cross nearly over our mastheads. Yet it was very dark, despite the almost ceaseless play of violet sheet-lightning away in the north-west. When the blackness had settled down the dead hush grew oppressive. A vague sense of expectation took possession of my mood—an indefinable apprehen- sion of I know not what, helped as the fancy was in this way by the silence and mystery of the night, as though Nature were holding her breath prior to some and the chief mate came on Eigillutigbheltyls display.st rucl deck to keep the watch. He stepped across te where I overhung the bulwarks, watching the dim clouds of phosphorescence wreathing like smoke through the stirless water. _</p>
        <p>"old Stormy he is dead and gone' with a vengeance," said he, quoting the opening words of a well-known sea chant. "If this sort of thing goes on much longer, there'll be no standing the old man. He's well nigh intolerable as it is." "Oh, well, I suppose the obligation of prompt despatch is the bogey of the modern shipmaster. Owners make no allowance for scanty winds." "I reckon that probably you're the one man aboard who doesn't caie a fig how long these calms may last," continued the mate. "Blow me tight if ever I saw such a change in a man's appearance as there is between you now and when you first came aboard!" _ _</p>
        <p>I was about to answer him when I became sensible of a most extraordinary sensation. The deck seemed to tremUle under my feet, as though the whole shape of the motionless ship were vibrat- ing from the effect of a violent shock. For the moment I believed it was a sudden unaccountable thrilling of my nerves till the mate burst out: "Strike me lucky! Are my legs• getting paralysed?" , The quivering lasted whilst you might have counted fifty, no longer; the ship then gave a faint heave, causing her sails to swing inwards with a sullen flop, and all was quiet again. "What in the name of all the Saints is the matter?" exclaimed the deep voice of the skipper, his shadowy form emerging through the companion. hatch. "Felt to me as though I were sitting in my little parlour at home with a heavy waggon rumbling past the front door." "The vessel certainly trembled, sir, replied the mate. "Seemed to me just as though her keel had picked up the ground, and yet a score of deep-sea lead-lines lashed together couldn't plumb the soundings hereabouts." He had scarcely finished speaking when the same peculiar sensation again ran through the ship. Two or three cries were raised this time by the men of the watch forward. The sense of shuddering was far more acute on, this occasion. Indeed, it felt for all the world as though the vessel were scraping over a reef, which, of course, was quite impossible seeing that she lay without headway. Again she heaved lightly, as though upborne upon a fold of swell, as the queer thrilling died away. • "What can it possibly be ?" I exclaimed, my wonderment not wholly unmixed with a startled feeling at the extraordinary. occurrence. "Bloomed if it don't feel like the shock of an earthquake to me!" ejaculated the mate.' • "Or a smothering great whale tickling his bacl,: up against our bilge," added the captain. "Better get a cast of the lead, Mr. Matthews." The mate strode forward, bawling out the neces- sary orders. A momett, later I heard the splash of the plummet dropped into the water; but as we had conjectured, no bottom was found. "Do you smell sulphur, captain, or is it only my fancy ?'" said I. He sniffed violently for a moment and then replied: "Yes. There's certainly a taste of it in the air. Pretty well explains the thing, I reckon. , You may take it that one of those disturbances has taken place somewhere in the bed of the ocean which happen when the sea comes in contact with the internal fires of the globe. Ha What's that,! " What now happened was one of those amazing and incredible experiences which it is seldom the lot of men to encounter, even at sea. As the skipper's cry of astonishment rang out there wal a dull, thunderous blast, which smote the ear in such a deep note of concussion as to produce a temporary sense of being stunned. Then, a moment after- wards. there leaned from off the face of the black</p>
      </div>
