      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>At this dull season, so far as atmospherical conditions are concerned, there is something peculiarly pleasing in the warm shades of red which predominasle annually in both the world of dress and millinery. The richness of the tones which are this year particularly charming never fail to give a becoming tinge of warm colour to the complexion of the wearer, while they contrast favourably with the greyness of a November afternoon and strike a note of cheerfulness in the most dismal surroundings. To both , blonde :and brunette rest is undeniably becoming and a soft deep shade cannot . be considered toc</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>BLOUSE,</head>
        <p>BLOUSE,</p>
        <p>striking by even the quietest dressers. Worn with dark furs, a crimson toilette looks not only ladylike, but also very smart . and does not require much trimming as a good cut and fit will ensure success. I have recently. seen some very attractive hats in Wine coloured .velvet trimmed with some handsome shaded ostrich feathers of the same colour or with a wreath of shaded leaves interspersed with red berries. A French sailor hat worn tilted over the face looked well in conjunction with a plain black tailor-made costume. The hat was of a bright rich red straw with the crown swathed with red silk while some red berries and leaves covered the bandeau at the back. The pretty blouse depicted is made of bright crimson silk with a yoke and cuffs of creamy lace. The full undersleeves are also of lace, while the outlines of the shoulders are defined in the fashion so much in vogue by a series of drawings eliding in a draped and frilled fichu. The bodice part is tucked for a few inches and then allowed to fall freely to the waist, where a swathed silk 'band finishing in a bow confines the fulness. The sleeves from the elbows fall in a full frill over the undersleeves of lace. The neck is collarless, although a twist of chiffon is employed to give a finish. A band of black velvet could be sub- stituted for the chiffon and would look well with a few ornamental brooches 'caught in it.</p>
        <p>A WINTER HAT.</p>
        <p>A pretty throat, however, requires no cpnpeal- ment and . these adjuncts are only necessary when there is some defect to conceal. There are some, very, pretty soft silks for blouses on view in the shops in various colours. These have a rather large raised silk spot dotted over the surface and make up into very useful and smart blouses for either morning or afternoon wear according to the amount of trimming used on them. Wine-coloured velvet is used to make the stylish winter hat sketched, which is trimmed by a long shaded Ostrich feather which springs from a chou of chiffon and curls over the hat on to the hair behind. The shape is of the latest mode and can be recommended as quite the latest thing in the realms of millinery. The crown is rather higher than those we have been accustomed to as the signs are not want- ing that the plateau hat is waining in popu- larity, and that as the season advances so also will the growth of the crowns of our hats. It is to be hoped that Madame la Mode wi'l not carry this, her latest whim, to extremes as on previous occasions, for the damsel of to-day is such a stalwart personage that she needs no aid to emphasise the goodly number of inches which are apt to cause he to overshadow the</p>
