      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>COSY DRESSING GOWN</head>
        <p>A COSY DRESSING GOWN</p>
        <p>mere Man. We have recollections of the tremendous height of hats 20 years ago as set forth in the 'fashion plates of that period which will surely counteract any leanings towards very high crowns, and I trust that such an exaggeration together with' that monstrosity, the crinoline, will never displace the toilettes of the smart girl of the period. A red hat can always be worn successfuly with a black or very dark blue toilette, but with no other unless one of its own colour. _ .</p>
        <p>The cosy dressing gown illustrated is made of warm flannel of so thick a make that a lining was not necessary, although it could be used for the upper part if desired. The skirt has a shaped flounce and a soft silk fichu edged with a frill to match is draped across the figure down to the waist where it is caught by an orripmetal clasp, the long ends depending down the front of the skirt almost to the feet of the wearer. The sleeves which are very full are caught into a band of silk from which a soft silk frill falls over the hands. The gown hangs loose from a yoke and is therefore eusily slipped on. The very elaborate styles of dressing as tea gowns one often sees are often made tight fitting at the sides or back, but this, of course, necessitates the wearing of corsets underneath by which the ease and tortffort of the garment is altogether spoilt. The fichu on this. gown might be made of cream coloured washing silk or net if preferred thus enhancing and smartening up the gown. There are some very cosy fabrics made speci- ally for gowns of this kind with a soft woolly surface and a zig-zag pattern in colours. These make very charming dressing gowns. COQUartif.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Queen's Brother.—The Crown Prince of Denmark intends making a tour of Europe, and will pay a visit* to the King and Queen at Sandringham towards the ' end of the month. His Royal Highness, who has seen I less of this country than might have been expected from his long and intimate relationship with our Royal , Family, will be present at the celebration of Queen Alexandra's birthday on December Ist. He is the oldest of the Crown Princes of Europe. The title does not usually convey the impression of a Royal person- age very far advanced in life, but the Crown Prince of Denmark is a grandfather of some years standing. King Edward was heir to a throne up to the time his Majesty was close upon sixty, but the Crown Prince of Den- I mark has passed that age, and he has enjoyed his present , title for just about forty years. Attended by Police.—The Duke and Duchess of Roxburghe have made early arrangements to take up their residence at Floors Castle, where a great welcome is to be prepared for them. Meanwhile the Duke and Duchess we are told have been spending their honey- moon, quietly, attended by two detectives—That is one of the drawbacks of splendour and riches, and ordinary folks may find therein comfort for their insignificance. To be haunted by policemen on such an occasion is so much out of keeping that one would have thought the Ducal pair would have preferred a quiet and simple wedding abroad. A great display only invites the at- tention of all sorts of scoundrelism, and it is not only jewels that have to be guarded, but their owners also for at the present moment vast rewards are offered for a millionaire who is missing within a hundred miles of New York. Silver Weddings of the two rArchbishops.—The Arch- bishop of Canterbury and Mrs. Davidson had a special reason for celebrating their silver wedding at Lambeth Palace, for it was there that they were married, when Dr. Davidson was Chaplain to Archbishop Tait. Mrs. Davidson was Miss Edith Tait, the second daughter of Archbishop Tait, and her great experience in the num- berless matters that have to be considered by the Primate, enables her to give much assistance to her husband. By a coincidence that is remarkable, the Archbishop of York and Mrs.Maclagan, celebrated their silver wedding on the same day. Dr. Maclagan is how- ever more than 20 years older than Dr. Davidson, he being 77, whilst the Archbishop of Canterbury is but 55. Dr. and Mrs. Maclagan celebrated their silver wedding in the South of France, where they have been staying for some time. _ .</p>
        <p>Dining and Discomfort.—A progressive dinner party is an innovation, introduced by some hostesses, which has little to recommend it. On such an occasion each gentleman has to move down two places at each course, so that he talks to each, or as many ladies as possible, in turn. Even thoughhe might be a brilliant conversa- tionalist, no sooner would he be able to discover a mutually interesting topic, than he would be off. Then 1 a man usually regardshis:dinner as an important matter 1 in the day's business, and the constant disturbance would I inevitably make him more morose and taciturn at each move, and more doubtful as to whether he might not be going on with somebody else's bread, or drinking out of somebody else's glass. Moreover there is, as a rule, quite as much disturbance as is desirable at a change of the courses, even when the waiting-at-table is of the most ample and perfect kind, and more often than not such changing of seats would only lead to disastrous confusion. The idea h therefore altogether one which sensible hostesses will not entertain. The Christmas Turkey.—The Christmas turkey is likely to be both scarce and dear this year. That is as regards the home-bred bird, which has suffered from the damp and sodden earth caused by the abnormal rains. The deficient supply will no doubt be made up with foreign turkeys, which are brought over in immense quantities from Russia and other countries, but at home the farmers' wives generally, are complaining of heavy losses amongst the turkey chicks. They are difficult to rear at all times, and with unfavorable conditions they are only too ready to give up the effort to live. There are many people who regard the turkey as a much over rated bird ;but it still holdslits pre-cminence as an item of Christmas fare, though this year, probably a greater number than ever will be imported from abroad Wearing Other People's Hair.—" John Strange Winter" has called down much-wrath upon her devoted head by the assertion"that ninety-nine women out of a hundred wear wigs." No doubt Mrs. 'Stannitrd refers more to "fringes "or "transformations" than to wigs, but even if all kinds of falsetresses are included, the statement is denounced by many as a gross exaggera- tion. But evidently some of these people have no idea of the general' extent to which "transformations" are worn, even by women who have ample hair of their own They save a good deal of trouble as regards dressing,and obviate the injury to one's own hair by the use of curl- ing or waving irons. Moreover they are'easy and cora- fortakle to wear, and so far from any stigma attaching to their use the putting on of the "transformation" is looked upon new much in the same light as putting on the hat. It is a good deal to say that as many as ninety- nine women out of a hundred wear other people's hair in addition to their own, but there is no more disparage- ment insuch an accusation nowadays than in saying the majority of women and girls wear false teeth. Bridal Modes.—The fashion set by the new Duchess of Roxburghe, of wearing her wreath over her veil, is already. being followed bfbrides on this side of the Atlantic. And a very becoming fashion it is, for when the veil is thrown back over the flowers, at the conclusion of the ceremony, it forms a delightful frame to the face, a kind of misty halo. But to secure this effect, the veil must of course be of simple tulle, so much more desirable in every way, than the heavy lace heirlooms under which so many brides have been weighted down. The bridesmaids at two or three recent fashionable weddings have also appeared in wreaths over tulle veils, the latter turned back over the flowers, and flowing almost to the edge of the gown. But when garbed in white, as was the case in one instance, there a was too little difference between the bride and her " maids " and too much sameness about the "proces- sion." A touch of colour would have been welcome.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <p>'</p>
        <p>, O4 tk.</p>
        <p>It must further be confessed that hats undoubtedly give a smarter appearance to a bevy of " maids " than do wreaths and veils. _ _</p>
        <p>A Dainty Accessory.—A modish little neck wrap may be run up easily by the clever home worker, at the smallest possible expense. Some four or five inch wide velvet or silk.and a little old fur that can be cut up into finger strips, is all that is necessary wherewith to build this accessory. The silk or velvet—about three- quarters of a yard in length—is edged with the fur, and worn wrapped round the neck, the ends crossing in front, where they fasten with a pin or brooch. If the fur is not forthcoming gold braid may be substituted, a touch of the same appearing in the bat or toque. Considering the fact that we are enjoying a time of comparative peace, the prevalence of gold lace and braid, and other military and naval decorations is,to say the least, curious</p>
        <p>ATADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>SALE "A" v. STOCKTON HEATH "A" Played at Sale on Saturday. An even game resulted in a draw of one goal each. Howard scored for Stock- ton Heath. E. Fair°lough and P. B. Hawtin played the best game for the visitors. GRASSEIsTDALE V. Sr. CLEMENT'S. On the former's ground on Saturday. Final :—St. Clement's; four goals; Grassendale, nil. FAIRFIELD V. LOVELY-LANE. -</p>
        <p>At Fairfield. Final :—Fairfield, two goals; Lovely- lane, one goal. FROGRALL ALBIONS v. ERASMIC. Played on the ground of the former on Saturday. Final :—Froghall Albions, three goals; Erasmic, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>BILIOUSNESS.</head>
        <p>BILIOUSNESS.</p>
        <p>Life to the bilious person is a burden. No day is free from the possibility of an attack. When it does come the sufferer is prostrated. Sickness, nausea, and headache, are some of the minor symptoms. Acute attacks of biliousness are often accompanied with violent vomiting, intense weakness and a sense of oppression. It all arises from a sluggish liver. This does not allow the bile to flow properly. Even in the most chronic cases of Biliousness, Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills will effect a cure. Mr. Frederick Vickers, 215, 'Mill-street, Manchester, had long suffered from biliousness, caused by a sluggish liver. ""My bile would not flow properly," he says, "and my blood became weak and lifeless. Stomach troubles added to my misery. Sick, heavy, and dull, each day was a burden to me. Two doctors attended me but without avail. Had it not been for Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills, I should still have been ill. They cured me positively and permanently." They are a perfect blood ! purifier, cure liver and kidney complaints, biliousness, indigestion, constipation, piles, dyspepsia, female ail- ' ments, eczema, pimples, and blotches. Sold by leading ' chemists and stores, price Is. lid. per bottle, or on receipt of price from W. H. Comstock Co.' Ltd., 21, Farringdon Avenue, London E.C. FREE SAMPLE. I Mention this paper and enclose id. stamp for postage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD v. FLIXTO'N.</p>
        <p>Latchford visited Flixton, and although short of five of the regular team, they were too good for the home team. The defence of the visitors was very sound, while in the forwards, Callon 4proved himself a very good substitute at centre, scoring three goals. Cliffe scored two and linsworth one. Result :—Latchford, six ; Flixton, one. LATCHFORD 2ND V. ORFORD BARRACKS.</p>
        <p>At Latchford. In consequence of several of the regular team having to assist the first eleven the home team was considerably weakened. Despite this a very good game ensued. For the home team, Grounds and Spencer scored one each, but the defence was not up to the usual standard with the exception of Rathbonc at centre half and Unsworth in goal. Result :—Orford Barracks, three; Latchford second, two.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Alderman J. Massey, Mayor of St. Helens, attended service at the Parish Church on Sunday morning, accompanied by a large number of members of the Council, magistrates, and officials. The procession from the Town Hall was headed by a detachment of the borough force under Chief Constable Wood, and the members of the Corporation Fire Brigade in uniform, under Superintendent Lyon, were also in attendance. The Rev. Canon Willink preached an earnest and interesting sermon, and appealed on behalf of the St. Helens Hospital. . In the evening the Mayor attended service at St. John's Church, Ravenhead, when the object of the offertory was to assist the Mission Nurse's' Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>PRISON FOR A RESISTER.</head>
        <p>PRISON FOR A RESISTER.</p>
        <p>Some excitement was caused at Eckington Police Court, in Derbyshire, on Monday, by an application on. the part of the overseers of Clowne, near Worksop, to commit the Rev. William Sharman, a Nonconformist passive resister, for non-payment of rates.—For a month the overseers had ; been unable to levy a distraint, although the amount was only 55., Mr. Sharman having declared that he would not pay. It was now said that Mr. Sharman wished to go to prison.—" Let him go there seven days,"said the chairman of the magistrates.</p>
        <p>Lord Kitchener, while ridinr, near Simla, collided with the wall of a tunnel and bloke his leg above the ankle. The doctors are hopeful of a speedy recovery. A fatal duel was fought in Paris, two young men of Toulouse taking swords to settle a dispute about a woman.</p>
      </div>
