      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>EVENTS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>A CLAIM OF 11,41)0,000.</p>
        <p>London is likely ere long to be the scene of a very sensational action, not entirely un- political in all its bearings. The plaintiff in this action is the Princess Catherine Radzi- 'will, and the defendants are the executors of Mr. Cecil Rhodes—namely, the Earl of Rose- bery, Earl Grey. Mr. Alfred Beit, Dr. Jameson, Sir Louis Mich- ael, and Mr. Bourchier Hawksley. Lord Milner, on ac- count of his official position, has taken no part in the ad- ministration of the estate, and is, therefore, ex- cluded from the action. The Princess claims</p>
        <p>the sum of PRINCESS RADZIWILL, WHO • I , 4 0 0,0 o o MAY CLAIM 4-1,400,000 FROM under an agree- MR. CECIL RHODES' EX- ment ,dated on ECUTORS. or about June 20th, 1899; -while the defence is that the signature is not that of the Colossus, but a forgery. Mr. Rhodes denied its authenticity, and Princess Radziwill was convicted of forgery at the Cape, and was imprisoned there for a time. Thus, apart from the financial aspect of the case, success in the present action would ex- onerate her and clear her name. She has retained as counsel on her behalf Mr. Rufus Isaacs, K C., Mr. Eldon Bankes' K.C., and Mr. G. J. Matthew, and sensational developments are promised. The Princess has lost no time in putting the matter in train since her recent arrival in London. Previous to her convic- tion on the charge of having forged Mr. Cecil Rhodes' name, which she vigorously denies and declares she will prove to have been a false accusation, Princess Catherine Radzi- will was a well-known figure in Cape Town. 'She went to the Cape in 1899, and edited a weekly paper which bore the name of "Great- er Britain." The development .of this law- -suit is being awaited with great interest on all sides.</p>
        <p>. 4,1? - . - • - _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>140 MILES PER HOUR.</head>
        <p>140 MILES PER HOUR.</p>
        <p>" Dr. Reichel is the hero of the hour, not 'only in his own country, but throughout the world of engineers. This is owing to the -successful and sensational experiments on the Berlin-Zossen Railway, of which he was the inventor and designer. This railway, some ten or twelve miles in length, running from Marienfelde to Zossen, has been built on his lines during the last three years by Messrs. Siemons and Halse at the instance and expense of a body of electrical experts; and that it is thoroughly complete, it is giving</p>
        <p>REICHEL'S ELECTRIC CAR, THE FASTEST</p>
        <p>IN THE WORLD.</p>
        <p>'wonderful and astounding results. . Indeed, over a year ago the rate of 90 and too miles an hour had been reached; but it was found that' it was too much for the track, which had to be re-laid with 961 b. rails, bolted to rock-embedded sleepers. Now the rate has been increased to 140 miles an hour for the short stretch; and the track shows no sign of wear. The power is introduced by three overhead wires. So great is the succ.,ss that there is already talk of fresh railways over longer stretches ; and it is quite possible that within a decade we may be whirling .about this country at the rate of 150 miles .an hour!</p>
