      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>"DAVID GARRICK " AT WINDSOR CASTLE.</head>
        <p>"DAVID GARRICK " AT WINDSOR CASTLE.</p>
        <p>Upon the the occasion of the King of Italy's state visit to Windsor this week, Sir Charles Wyndham has been honoured by a command to the Castle, .to play " David Garrick." No better selection could have been made or more characteristically English piece chosen for the • entertainment .of the Italian Sovereign. "David Garrick" is a great play, and never are Sir. Charles Wyndham and Viss Mary Moore seen to greater advantage than in it. The only pity</p>
        <p>•SIR CHARLES WY.NDHAN.</p>
        <p>MISS MARY MOORE</p>
        <p>seems to be that</p>
        <p>Thursday night was</p>
        <p>chosen, owing to Thursday being the heaviest 'day for the King and -Queen of Italy. On .that day they visited London, when they received the Italian Colony at the Embassy? in Grosvenor Square, lunched at the Guild- hall, returned to Windsor and attended a state banquet, ending up with the theatre at i i p.m. It was •rather too. much for one day ; and Sir Ch,rles Wyndham had a tired audience to face. All the properties were sent down to Windsor as usual, and a stage that met all requirements was fitted up in the Waterlow Gallery. The cast included apart from Sir Charles Wyndham and Miss Mary Moore, Mr. Alfred Bishop, Mr. Sidney Broug,h, Mr Harry Paulton, Mr. Frank Atherley, Mr. Leslie Faber, Miss Emily Vining and Miss Ethel . Marryat. The New Theatre - was of 'course closed for the evening.</p>
