      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>LORD WOLSELEY ON HIMSELF.</head>
        <p>LORD WOLSELEY ON HIMSELF.</p>
        <p>A very welcome contribution to the week'S literature is Lord Wolseley's "Story of a Soldier's Life," full, of genuine adventure and human interest. Lord Wolseley sets forth in his brief preface the aim and object he had !before liim in writing these two volumes. He has tried, as he tells us, to re- cord the noble actions he has witnessed, and describe the men he has been associated with. "Ihave set down nought in mal- ice, and there- fore beg my readers to for- give what may be my preju- dices." Natur- ally, he is him- self the chief and principal actor, the centre and around</p>
        <p>pivot</p>
        <p>Which the whole story - revolves,</p>
        <p>LORD WOLSELE Y.</p>
        <p>and</p>
        <p>his own</p>
        <p>personality. de- spite his modest disclaimer, Inspires and •izoinins au that it contains. Uniortunately, ataeons Wbich be felt cogent, and which 'al'e laud admit to ix &amp;maw* cansciviid, bawl)</p>
        <p>!weighed with him in avoiding the latest epi- sodes of his most eventful and useful life, and, much as we may miss his own version ol well-known controversies, his own account oi the part he played in recent affairs, and 01 the treatment accorded him, we cannot but commend his reticence. It is not possible here to do justice to such a work as this, but the fulness of interesting incident will be realised when it is stated that not only are the laitherto-recorded vicissitudes of this great warrior's life included, hut many stirring episodes that have never More been heard in club or seen in print. It is an ideal work to put into a boy's hands, especially the boy of military bent. The public recep:ion accoracl "The Story of a Soldier's Life' has lx:en as phenomenal as it deserved.</p>
        <p>I • ; 1 . $ A • /CA • • -„-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>NEW ADMIRALTY YACHT.</head>
        <p>THE NEW ADMIRALTY YACHT.</p>
        <p>The new Admiralty yacht, the Enchantress, Launched at Belfast, is expected to be put in commission by the end of the financial year. She is a tine vessel of 3.60 p tons displacement, and has been very favourably criticised. She has been built by Messrs. Ilerland &amp; the famous Belfast shipbuilders, and was launched by Mrs. Arnold-Forster, the wife of the new Secretary of State for War. Her con- nection with Belfast is, of course, the fact that her husband is member for the division. It is, indeed, only two or three weeks since</p>
        <p>ANTRESS.</p>
        <p>they re-elected him upon his promotion to Cabinet rank, although, owing to an accident, which laid him up, he was unable to go and fight the •election in person. He is, in fact, not quite convalescent yet, and much of the War Office business is transacted at his pri- vate house.in Evelyn Gardens. So, with Lord Roberts' greatly regretted illness, both the heads of the unpopular Pall Mall department are away.</p>
