        <p>private life, and</p>
        <p>her undoubted Aims HELENA MILLAIS, and sudden suc- MR. GEORGE ALEXANDER'S cess gains addi- NEW PROTEGEE. tional interest</p>
        <p>from the fact that until she joined the "Old Heidelberg" company last August she had never played either in a professional or an amateur dralnatic company. " I was fond," she says, "of 'dressing `up' and acting by myself, even when I was a little tot and nothing gave me greater pleasure than to shut myself up in the big attic at the top of the house and act and recite all alone. Since I've been older I have recited at concerts and baiaars— Shakespeare for choice— but that's all the experience of the stage I pos- sessed When I finally persuaded my father to take me to see Mr. George Alexander. I like sympathetic parts best, you know; long ago I wanted to play 'Jo' or 'Little Nell,' or one of the 'Two Little Vagabonds' ; in fact, I did play 'Jo' and 'Nell' by myself in the big attic. And here, in this part of Kathie, it is in .the concluding scenes that I lam happiest. feel that there.l am more thoroughly in touch with the audience." Miss Millais not only acts, but sings and dances well; she is also a. Clever artist and musi- cian, and cycles and swims for recreation.</p>
        <p>, . --- •...,0../.41• „,„,.... ..,, :.... • • ,I# _. Il /I V ii 6 'Lc\ .. - li-si-FA ) 11</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>COMMITTEE OF THREE.</head>
        <p>THE COMMITTEE OF THREE.</p>
        <p>Very general • satisfaction has been ex- pressed at the appointment of Viscount Esher, K.C.8., Admiral Sir John Arbuthnot Fisher, G.C.8., and Col. Sir George Sydenham Clarke, R.E., K.C.M.G., Governor of. Victoria, to form a Committee to advise as to the creation of a board for the administrative business of the War Office and as to the cdnsequential changes thereby involved. The model which the Committee will consider will be that existing at the Admiralty. The committee will be. constituted at once, and its chief sittings will extend from Janu- ary to about. June. The recommendations of the committee will not, it is stated, be put into force until 1905. which is about the earlist date by which a new board can be put into working order. The creation of a board imperils the continuance of the post of *Commander-in-Chief. The chairman of the committee is Visrount Esher, who is the son of the distinguished judge. foriverly SO well known as Master of the Rolls.</p>
        <p>Born in June, 1852, he was educated at Eton and at Trinity College, Cambridge. In 1878 he became private secretary to the Marquis of 11 artingtcn, now Duke of Devonshire, acting in that capacity unti; 885. After an Lneventful Parliamentary career he .-.Lted as Secretary to* the Offic€ of Worhs from 1895 to 1902. Later on hil Lordship servc d rron the Royal Commision on the South Ali an War. He owes his selection on the in( scnt occasion to the prominent part AN, 11 ich he took in that important investigation. Admiral Sir John Fisher is foremost among British naval ,:crs, the Comm and er-in.Chief at Ports- mouth. Since his thirteenth birthday he has devoted his lifiz! to . the sea service. As a young officer he gained distinction in the Crimean campaign, and in the China war of 1859-60. He took part in the Egyptian war of 1882. At the bombard- ment of Alexandria he was in command of the Inflexible. In 1906 he will have to retire under the age cfause. Sir George Clarke, who has been Governor of Victoria 6ince toot, is a distinguished Engineer afficer who has made a special study of efence problems.</p>
