      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, November 21, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, November 21, 1903.</p>
        <p>THE DISTRICT NURSES.</p>
        <p>WHAT THEY ARE DOING IN WARRINGTON. An Appeal to the Churches</p>
        <p>Warrington possesses an efficient band of district nurses, the value of whose work,especiallyin the homes of the poor, it would be difficult to over-estimate. Sgme interesting details of.the past year's operations 'Were nikfolded at the annual meeting of the District Nursing Association which was held in the Co-operative Hall, Cairo street, on Tuesday evening under the presidency of Mr. F. Monks, treasurer and founder of the organiza-. tion. There was a fair attendance, including tke Rev C. Harvey Cook, Miss Whitfield (superintendent nurse), Miss Gillie, of Liverpool, Councillor J. Roberts, .Mr. G. Thompson, Mr. John Fairclough, and Mr. J. D. Fairclough (secretary). The SECRETARY read a letter of apology for non- attendance from Mrs. Miller and said the Revs. Canon Willis, John Yonge and E. C. E. Carleton and Mr. John White had also expressed regret at their inability to attend.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>YEAR'S WORK.</head>
        <p>THE YEAR'S WORK.</p>
        <p>The sixth annual report was presented as follows : The committee have much pleasure in submitting their report for the sixth rear's work, which continues to be very satisfactory. the thanks of the committee are due to all the subscribers and those public bodies whose contributions have made so welcome and substantial an address to the funds. For details of work done the committee refer subscribers and others to the superin- tendent's report, which speaks for itself and indicates the number and variety of the cases visited. A refer- ence to the treasurer's report will show the necessity for new and increased financial support if the Society is to continue a work commensurate with the needs of the town. There are now six regular nurses on the staff, and the report of the inspector after her annual visit is gratifying. She expresses her great satisfaction both with the nursing and the home management. To the superintendent and the nurses our warmest thanks are due for their untiring devotion to their duties and the efficient manner in which they are discharged</p>
        <p>In her report the SUPERINTENDENT s-4,1d that the fact that the statistics showed a decrease in the number of patients attended, and in the number of visits paid, did not by any means imply retrogression, but was rather due to the comparatively healthy state of the town a condition of things which inquiry showed to be fairly general throughout the country. The work of the Association was more highly appreciated than ever by the poor of the town and they had come to know and recognize it as peculiarly their own, and to speak of the staff as "Our Nurses." The health of the nurses throughout the year had been good. There had been two changes on the staff. Nurses Harrison and Jessop, both from "Queen's" Training Houses in London, were elected to fill the vacancies. All the nurses at present, therefore, were on the "Queen's Roll." Although the endeavour was to be "nurse, not almoner," it was of incalculable help to them to be able to relieve some of the pitiful and deserving cases of poverty which came under their notice,and to those who had enabled them to do this, and whose kindness was acknowledged else- where, they owed an unspeakable debt of gratitude. Gifts of old linen and clothing were still urgently needed, and blankets, old or new, for lending, woulds be gratefully received. A summary Of the work showed that 492 cases had been under notice, 51 b3lng cases transferred from last year, whilst 441 were new cases. There were 107 cases visited once or twice but not entered. The number of visits paid was 17,567. The results in the cases nursed were as follows :—convales- cent or relieved, 262; transferred to Hospital or Infirm- ary, 25; died, 98; removed from books for other causes, 11; remaining on books, 45. The cases were reported to the Association as follows :—By medical men, 268. by medical men requested by parents and' friends, 52; by clerg,y, ministers, and visitors, 23; by. patients and friendf.s. 72; by nurses, 16;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>TREASURER'S REFOR T.</head>
        <p>TREASURER'S REFOR T.</p>
        <p>In presenting the statement of accounts, the TREASURER called attention to the need for new and increased subscriptions. Owing to death and removals the subscriptions were less,and these would be felt more particularly in the current year. The handsome dona- tions by the Cycle Club and St. Nlary's Dramatic Society had helped very much to keep the Association free from debt and difficulty. The very handsome gifts towards the purchase of the Nurses' Home would help permanently by reducing the amount paid for rent, when they were able to pay off the balance due the bank for this purchase. It was a very gratifying feature that, most of the donations and special gifts were spon- taneous, indicating that the work of the Association was appreciated by those best able to judge of the ser- vices rendered to the sick poor of the town. The balance sheet showed that the income for the year, including a balance brought forward of £ll 175., amounted to £660 2s. 11d. After paying expenses there was a credit balance of £4.4 us. 7d. On the property account there was a balance due to the bank of ;£236 16s. 6d. The expenditure of I'o6l 16s. 6d. had been incurred in the purchase of the Nurses Home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>SPEECH BY MR. MONKS.</head>
        <p>SPEECH BY MR. MONKS.</p>
        <p>The CFLAIRNI AN said that from letters which he had received and expressions of opinion which he had heard, he was, quite certain that the work of the Association was very much appreciated by the people of Warrington and they showed substantial appreciation by subscriptions to the funds. Only a few days ago they received a contribution of £26 from local anglers and he heard a sort of whisper that there was a further sum to come. Last week they had another illustration of the appreciation of the work, in the performances of the St. Mary's Dramatic Society. They all felt very much indebted to the Society and particularly to the secretaries—Messrs. Hearne and Vernon—for the very considerable amount of trouble they took in arranging the entertainments. He was sorry some supporters of the Association were unable to attend the meeting, hut when they were building a cathedral it was a rather big job, and he understood a great many of their Church of England friends were busy looking at the plans of the magnificent cathedral which it was proposed to build at Liverpool. Mr. Monks proceeded to contrast the annual report with that presented after the first year's working. The number of CMOs reported during the first year was 193 and the visits paid totalled 6078: This year there had been 441 cases and 17.567 visits. That showed a great amount of devotion on the part of the nurses,and although, fortun- ately, he had not needed their help he knew many who had the advantage of their attention and they were very grateful for it. The first year's expenses amounted to £261 2s, ' sd. whereas during the past year they had spent £5BB ss. 4d. Mr. Monks expressed the gratitude of the Association for the donations of £2OO each from Messrs. Vere B. Davies, Edward G Davies, and Leonard G. Davies, as a memorial to their late father, £5O from Councillor H. Thornton, £5O from Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Swansea, and £5 from Alderman Smet- hurst. These amounts had been devoted to the fund for the purchase of the Nurses Home, on which there was a deficit of £256 16s 6d. rt had been suggested that'll bazaar should be held with the object of wiping off the debt,but his experience of bazaars was such that he would rather pay down what he intended to spend than have the trouble of promoting a bazaar. The snbscriptions on the current account were not as large as last year's but the difference was more than covered by the donations from the St. Mary's Dramatic Society and the Cycle Parade Committee. He con- gratulated the Association upon the growth of the work and expressed the opinion that steady progress was better than growth which was forced like a hot- house plant because it appealed to the people and they would support it. Every penny they had received, as far as he knew had been cheerfully and Willingly subscribed and was the spontaneous gift of those who appreciated the work of the Association. He had pleasure in moving the adoption of the reports and balance ,theet.</p>
        <p>Mr JOHN; P-AIRCLOUGII seconded and said it was very vratifying to see the Association in such a prosperous The proposition was carried. THANKING THE NURSES AND THE ST. MARY'S DRAMATIC SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. TitomPsos m.wed.a vote of thanks to the superintendent (Miss Whitfield) and the nursing staff, and to the St. Mary's Dramatic Society for theirefforts on behalf .of the Institution. He had, he said, in moving about amongst workingmen heard reports that led him to believe that the services of the nurses were very much appreciated.,Theycould not go very far with- out meeting a nurse going onher rounds and he was very pleased that the Tramways Committee had granted the free use of the cars to the nurses. (Applause.) found that this year tkere had been a!) increase of three collections on behalf of the Association in.churches and! chapels in the town. For the Infirmary there were collections in about 50 places of worship, but for the Nursing Association collections were made in only 14 of them. He knew the clergy had a. grelt deal to do with the money raised by the congregations, but if they would give the people a chance to contribute, about £5O or £6O might be handed over to the institution. Mr. Thom]) son con- cluded by paying a tribute to the work of the St. Mary's Dramatic Society. Councillor JOHN Ronmrs sl.conded and said he could</p>
      </div>
