      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>speak from his own experience of the value o f work of the nurses,_He expressed the indebtedness _ _ .</p>
        <p>the townspeople to Mr. Monks for founding the Associa- tion which had performed an admirable work. He _ _ _</p>
        <p>hoped Mr. Monks. would. be spared for many years to continue the -work of which he was the founder. Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. (Applausa.) At a ma 3ting of this society on Snarlay,the chair was Mr. WILLI.v.m. BIBBY said that on behalf of the occupied by Mr. George Oolley who spoke upon a friendly societies he wished to ton.der sincere thanks to Christian's duty to his fellows, and gave an instance the nurses for theirethcient i arvica 4. 1-b xis surprised • drawn from the Foreign Mission field.—Mr. T. W. that the varions_places of worsaip did not take the Hunter, of Manchester, delivered an address based on matter up and sead in largor con".ributions. The Naboth's vineyard, which he referred to as a Bible workingmen of t!i a tow .1 gave vary Lan Iso_nely to the drama of conscience. He graphically described the in- Infirmary but ha considered the nurses did an even cident, and pictured a scene of Ahab, Jezebel and nobler work tn in th it in ;IA tat o 1 --fsapolattse) —in the Elijah. In drawing alesson, he pointed out that real sense that t• rev intradno I into tha ho:nes of the paople pleasure was to be obtained in the pursuit of good and habits of c4,triliness,thrift and evei-ything that was good not in the pursuit of sinful lusts.—Master Pierce very The:lnfirmary:lid a noWe wir.: in tra Ltiag.patients who effaotively played an organ solo entitled "Nazareth." had met with acniciants, ton he e viiide--od that it</p>
        <p>received more that it; fair 'prop )rtioa of sup pbrt. Nfr Bibby referred to the necessity for more nurses to cope with the great amount of work. Tae Ciiiui ailed his testimony to the good work of the stiff. in raferenc?., to ?Er. Thompsou's re- marks about pu-sons, some of theni ware a bad lot— (langhte:•)—but some were v3ry goal and had helped th 3 Ass )ciation as mach as lay in their power. There were oz;hers, h)k \war, w.io did alt give the slightest att to aly re iirst, far snp?nrt. As business men they w.!re proverbially oat of it altogother—(laughter) --but he wished they could stir them up a bit, and if anyone could suggest a means by which they could be stirred up to a greater sense of their duty to the Asso- ciation, he should be very glad. One very large denom- in ttion iiiVirringtou had not contributed a penny from the day the Asio.eiation was founded. It was a great shame and he thought as some years had gone by he should feel it inc.auubent upon him to send a very strong protest to that body and SB3 if they could not do s mething to help the institution. The motion was heartily carried. In responding, :%Liss WatTFIELD said the vote of thanks was quite unneceSSary. The nurses found quite . . . .</p>
        <p>sufficient thanks in the feelin7 that they were able to help some of their p3nrei• aacl less fortunate brethren. Several years ago when they were invited to Mart- borough House, Queen Alexandra gave them a nice little addresi. and in a few of h3r words exactly ex- pressed the feeling of the tuar.3ei. She said: "I can, indeed, imagine no higher or holier. calling than that of being able to tend the pr and staff ering in the. hour Of their greatest need." (Applause). Mr. M. G. Flr.a.rtxa returned thanks on behalf of the St. Mary's Dr in atic Society and said it wai pleasure for them to be able to help the Association. ADDRESS BY MISS MIME.</p>
        <p>Miss GELLEE. of Liverpiol. then delivered an address in the course of which she congratulated the Associa- tion upon the excellent report which showed a very good year of work. &amp;-fe alluded with gratification to the opening of i "IA l t-!-rt i•I Warrington, to the fact that the Association had won the hearts of the Tramways Committea—(laughter)—and to the support which it received from various organizations in the town.. She gave some interesting details of the work in Llverpool, and Painted, out thit etch lady or a certain nuinber of ladies connected with the Association had to superintend the work in a particular district. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Baptist Church, Colborne Street. The Rev. H. Dannico preached on Sunday evening last at the above Church on the subject," Why we are Nonconformists." After speaking about the reform- ation and its effects on the religious life of England, the beginning of Nonconformity, and the position of Nonconformists, the preacher stated his intention of using the word Free Churchmen, which included the three terms Protesta,ntism,Nonconformity and Dissent. "We are Protestants," he stated, because we protest against any man coming between the human soul and God, we are Nonconformists because we refuse to con- form to certain unscriptuml doctrines which are taught in the Church of England Prayer Book, such as Baptismal Regeneration, and we are Dissenters because we dissent altogether from the principle that the Church and State should be united?' Nonconformists contend that the Church should not be under the con- trol of the Government or State, but that as a spiritual body it should be subject to no authority save Christ himself. Choir Festival at Bold-street Church.</p>
        <p>That systeru had had excellent results. Miss Gillie paid a tribute to the late Mr. Rathbone _</p>
        <p>ivho founded the work in Liverpool. Continuing she said no woman would become a district nurse unless she had a love for the people, a, love for God, and a love for those who were poor, weak and suffering. One of thotifficulties which the nurses experienced Was to teach people without seeming to teach them, and t,s go into homes without appearing to change things &amp;Int once. Miss Gillie alluded to the good work which the nurses performed in teaching habits of cleanliness, and readlthe tribute paid by a distinguished medic ii officer who said a district nursing staff was a valuable accessory to public he tlth work. (Applause). A morning's work of one ef the nurses in Liverpeol WAS interestingly described, and in conclusion the speaker pointed out how sympathisers might supplement the work of the nurses in Warrington by visiting patients, reading to them, and rennering other little services. She heartily congratulated the Association upon all that had been done during the past six years. (Ap- plause.)</p>
        <p>There were large congregations at Bold street Wes- leyan Church on Sunday when the annual choir festival services were held. In the morning the Rev. R. Wilkins Rees preached from the text : "Instruments of God." The anthem" Send out Thy light," was rendered by the choir, and Miss Mabel Henshall sang" Entreat me not to leave thee:" Mr. E. Heywood, A.R.C.O. gave an organ recital which comprised the prelude and Fugue in D major (Bach) and Communion in D (Hollins); and the hymn of praise" All that ha.h life and breath."—At the evening service the Rev. G. H. Carnson preached from the words : "Joy in Service." The choir gave the anthem "Hear my Prayer," the solo being taken by. Mrs. . _ . . .</p>
        <p>A. Booth, while .Mr. Arthur Griffiths Contributed "If with all your heart." The organ selections were "Jerusalem the Golden," and the sonata No. 3. Mendelssohn.—On Monday night, the cantata "Ruth," was rendered by the choir in highly creditable style. Mr. R. A. Naylor, who had promised to preside, was un- able to be present. The programme opened with "The Lord worketh wonders," by Mr. E. H. Pearce. The duet, "Power Eternal," was feelingly interpreted by Mrs. A. Booth and Miss N. Anderton. Mr. E. Heywood gave two organ solos—" Reverie " (Copoeci) and the concert overture in C Major (Hollins).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>DEFORMED CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>DEFORMED CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The CfIXIgII.IN called attention to the ex.cellent %cork that was being done by the society known as Mrs. Harrison's Aid Society which supplied a grett deal of food for p ttients. He was also glad to say they had received a great deal of encouragement from the cookery school at the Technical institute. (Applause.) He advised the ladies of Warrington to call at the school and listen to the lectures given, andlhe was quite sure they would be delighted. Con- tinuing he said that during the year the Association had dealt with 32 cases of deformed children. They knew what a sad thing it was for anyone who grew up knock-kneed or bow-legged, and if any lady or gentle- man would let him know of cases of children suffering from sqch deformities he would put them in thc way of having them made right. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>SPEECH BY THE REV. C. H. COOK.</head>
        <p>SPEECH BY THE REV. C. H. COOK.</p>
        <p>The Rev. C. FaRVEY CDIK, the newly-appointed pastor of Cairo-straat Chtp3l, mwel ahe trty vota of thAnks to Nliss Gillie. Fla hopad,h3 s tid, to come into close relationship with the district nurses of the town for they ware doing a coornon work. They could together help to do somathing not only for the redeem- ing of the bodies but for tha.redeamin.g of the souls of those who needed their help. Nurses needed help and sympathy of the most sincere kind. in regard to the collections at places of worship, the speaker referred to the difficulty which he knew. -Free Church ministers experienced in persuading the Church officials to devote offertories to such an organisation. Happily, that did not apply to the Unitarians, who, if asked to help anything which meant the redeeming of nutnkind, would respond liberally. (Applause). The resolution was heartily carried. The Chairman's services were cordially acknowledged and the proceedings concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>KILLED COMING FROM WORK.</head>
        <p>KILLED COMING FROM WORK.</p>
        <p>Many workers receive their death-blow just as they leave work. Tairoresses, leaving a hot factory,step out into a keen, biting wind, and catch a cold Which may lead to pneumonia. Miners come up from the workings bathed in perspiration, and contract liver chill, which leads to acute liver complaint, rheumatism, or some other .and evally serious ailment. In Charles Forde's bile beans will be found a remedy which will fortify the system against such dangers. John Hughes a miner living in Herbert street, Abercynon, says :—" One thy, after working a hard shift, I came up wet with perspiration, and stood out in the open air for a time. The result .was that I caught a severe cold, which affected my live.-,disordered my whole systpm, and brought on rheumatism. I was seized at intervals with violent pains in the back, and so unbearable did they beddine that I was compelled to leave work. After this my appetite left me, and I could not divest what little I did make myself eat. Very little food would remain on my stomach.</p>
        <p>The pain about my loins grew more and more intense, and ngthing I took gave me any relief. "Just when at my worst I read an account of the marvellous power of Charles Forde's bile beans. Hop- ing that they would do me good, I ordered a box, and began a.course. •</p>
        <p>"By the first boa was done the pains were nothing like so violent,and I began to enjoy my food.So I persevered with the use of this wonderfnl medicine until I became completely cured—had got rid of every bit of pain, arld was eating downright heartily. I have had no return of either indigestion or rheumatism, so I know the cure was permanent." The vegetable remedy which, to quote Mr. Hughes' words, "acts like a charm" in all cases of colds, chill, rheumatism, or liver and stomach troubles, may be had from all chemists at one-and-three-half-. pence or two-and-nine per box.</p>
        <p>COLONEL P LK X, •\ . P., AT tiAll LESTO</p>
        <p>On Monday night a meet ing of Cemservatives and Unionists Twas held at Earlestown for the purpose Of hearing Colonel Pilkington's views on the fiscal question. Mr. C. B. F. Borron support- ing a resolution Confirming Mr. Chamberlain's policy, Colonel Pilkington stated that the years from 1846 to 1872—allnded to by the - supporters of Free Trade— foreign countries were at war with each other, and, owing to that fact, trade in this country pro- gressed enormously.. But since then the tide of commerce had changed, our imports had decreased in respect of foreign countries, and had it not been for the growth of *Colonial trade this, country would have been in a nitich worse position that .it was. It was for the working men of England to do all they could to develop that trade. It was abstird for oppon- ents of Mr. Chamberlain's policy to stater' that a 20 per cent. duty on corn would result in a little loaf. AlO per 'cent, duty on manufactured goods in/potted would only tend to improve the position of the working man. He paid a lengthy tribute to Mr. Chamberlain on his policy and self-sacrifice, - and concluded by asking all working men to support policy.—The motion was pissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>158 MILES TN 158 MINUTES.</head>
        <p>158 MILES TN 158 MINUTES.</p>
        <p>The London and North Western Railway Company have this week established a record railway run. The American boat express, which usually runs from Liver- pool to Euston without stop, was pulled up at Crewe. The journey from Crewe to London, 15SA miles, was accomplished in exactly as many minutes, notwith- standing that on two oc,asions speel was reduced al- most to walking pace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>LUNG DISEASES.</head>
        <p>LUNG DISEASES.</p>
        <p>Tbc Rev. W. DACRE, 5, College Villa Road, S. Hampstead, London, writes, June Nth I have a high opinion of VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. have used it with much benefit to myself and have recommended it to others."</p>
        <p>VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE is the purest and most efficient remedy procurable for coughs, colds bronchitis, asthma, catarrh and children's ci)ughs Price 9!d., ls. 1.W., and 2s. Dd., at chemists and drug stores.</p>
