      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Rev. James Flanaghan's Mission.</head>
        <p>The Rev. James Flanaghan's Mission.</p>
        <p>On Sunday and Monday successful mission services were conducted in the Parr Hall by the Rev. James Flanagha,n, of the South East London Mission. Last week services were held in the Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church, and on Sunday evening Parr Hall was crowded. The Rev. J. G. Reed presided, and Mr. Flanaghan gave stirring addresses, describing his work in the London slums. The singing was led by a united choir, conducted by Mr. Albert Spruce, and Mr. Fred Podmore presided at the organ. On Monday evening a large andießce assembled to hear Mr. Flanaghan's lecture, "A walk round my parish." Councillor Dodds presided, and expressed the pleasure it gave him to take part in anything connected with the Primitive Metho- dist Church, knowing, as he did, their history and politics. He was anxious to see Primitive Methodism take the place it ought to in Warrington and for them to have a new and larger place of worship. Mr. Flana.ghan's lecture proved most interesting. As the result of the services £5O will be handed over for the aid of the rev. gentleman's London mission.</p>
        <p>Foreign Missio is. Anniversary Services at Wycliffe. The foreign missionary annivqrsary in connection with Wycliffe Congregational Church took place on Sunday and the following Tuesday evening. The cause of missions was advocated by three foreign missionaries of the London Missionary Society. At, the, service on Sunday morning, the pulpit was occu- pied by the Rev. Arthur A. Dignum, who is a son of Mr. John Dignum of Great Sankey, for many years deacon and Sunday school teacher at Wycliffe. The Rev. Arthur Dignum went out to India in 1881 from Lancashire College and was ordained in Wycliffe Church. His furlough home on this occasion has been saddened by the deat'i of his wife. lin speaking of his work in India, Mr. Dignum referred to the great intelligence of the Indian people and to the beauty and similarity of the Brahmin religion to that oflthe Christian religion. The Christian missionary had to put forth his best efforts to prove the existence of the Saviour Jesus Christ. Despite innumerable difficulties, the Christian religion was acting for good upon the lives of the people, and the number of con- verts was steadily increasing.—ln the afternoon the Rev. F. NV. Walker, from New Guinea, delivered an address to the scholars in the Sunday school and greatly interested them by his narration of typical incidents in the life of the young people of New Guinea. In the evening he based his remarks on the text "That if any man would not work neither should he eat."(llThess.3c. 10v.). He referred to the tricks which were played upon the natives of New Guinea by traders, and which had led them to be lazy and dilatory. He pointed out how necessary it was that the natives should be pro- tected from these un-Christian practises, if the gospel was to make any progress amongst them. At the morning service, the choir sang the anthem "Arise, shine!" and in the evening, "How lovely are the messengers." A missionary meeting was held on Tuesday evening in the Memorial Building. The Rev. John Yonge pre- sided and addresses were delivered by the Rev. F. NV. Walker, from New Guinea, and the Rev. A. Bonsey, from Hankow, China. The CHAIRMAN expressed the hope that the two gentlemen, who had 4evoted the whole of their lives to foreign missionary work, would gather some en- couragement from that large meeting. He was con- vinced that the audience would gather not only information which would be interesting and instructive but they would gather a new measure of enthusiasm in that great part of Christian service. From the state- ment as to the financial condition of the Warrington auxiliary of the society, which includes Lvmm and Widnes,it appeared that the Churches in the Warrington auxiliary had contributed £lO3 14s. 11d., £7B 2s. 6d. of which had been contributed by Wycliffe Church. (Applause.) He regretted that that sum was not so large as in past years. He considered that in pro- portion as Wycliffe Church • rose to the level of generosity for the cause of Christ in other parts of the world the better it would be for the Wycliffe Churchat home. (Hear, hear.) Mr. WALKER, in the course of his address, gave an example, showing that the natives of New Guinea,when treated fairly, were honest and just. Mr.BoxsEv spoke of his work in Hankow and district and referred to the hollowness of the old religions of China, and to the evil practices of the priesthood. Additional interest was lent to the proceedings by two appropriate recitations by Miss Ruby Witts and Nlaster. Herbert Eccles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
