        <p>LONDON AND N ORTH-WEb"rERN RAILW % . ST. HELENS</p>
        <p>_ CABLE Co. These teams met at Arpley on Saturday last. Cable kicked off, Loco's pressed and from a mark Taylor anded a goal in the first minute. Again the railway men got going and from a rbund of passing Bouchier scored a capital try. This rapid scoring nonplussed the Cable men who seemed unable to withstand the onslaught made on their lines. The railway continued to have the best of matters, several tries being obtained in quick succession. Half-time, score L. and N.-W. Railway, 2 goals, 5 tries; Cable nil. The second half was again all in favour of the Loco's who shewing surprising form completely outclassed their opponents and at the end the game stood L. and N.-W. Railway, 4 goals 11 tries (41 points), to Cable nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>ST. MARY'S MISSION INSTITUTE.—The inaugural meeting of the above was held in the infants school- room on Monday night last. The meeting took the form of a reception by the ladies of the committee, and the room was prettily decorated for the occasion. There was large muster present. The following ladies Iwere1were in command, assisted by a bevy of willing helpers: —Mrs. Aitkin, Miss Tinsley, Mrs. Owen, and Miss Standish. The president, Mr. J. B. Aitkin, made the opening speech, and welcomed the members. He gave those present some sound advice, and his bright and interesting speech was appreciatively listened to. Re- freshments were then served and were done full justice to. Daring this time digestion was aided by selections on the 'tyramaphone by Mr. Sidney Tun- stall, and these were afavourite item in the evening's programme. The Rev. J. R. Jones, vicar, and Mr. W. Hodgkinson, the chairman, also addressed the gathering and the objects of the society were explained. A short programme was also gone through, which was of a very enjoyable nature. A banjo and pianoforte duet by Misses R. and M. White, "Yankee Doodle," was well rendered, and Miss A. Daunt gave a fine rendering of " Vainka's song" ; Miss Belle Davies in "Needles and pins," was very successful, and Mr. Harry Mainley's humorous sketch caused much merri- ment. Miss M. Standish accompanied the vocalists on the pianoforte. The absence of Mr. .1. Hill through indisposition was much regretted, as he had done much to bring the society to its present stage. The inde- fatigable scribe, Mr. T. Pennington, was well rewarded by the success of the gathering, which dispersed at 10 p.m., the National Anthem being the closing item. We understand the institute will meet each Monday evening during the session, and it is to be hoped that the en- thusiasm displayed on Monday night will be of a lasting nature.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>WRITER OF OUR 'SPECIAL."</head>
        <p>THE WRITER OF OUR 'SPECIAL."</p>
        <p>Mr. G. P. Gooch, who contributes a spe cia article to our columns this week On" Protection in the United States," is one of the young Cambridge men who contributed to the series of articles called" The Heart of the Empire." Mr. Gooch was a student under Lord Acton at Cambridge, and is engaged with others on completing the great history which Lord Acton was writing when he died. Mr. Gooch is an able lecturer, and often appears on Liberal platforms for the Eighty Club and other bodies. Some six weeks ago the Liberals of Bath recommended him as their second candidate, and his friends hope that. so able an exponent of Liberal policy will soon find a seat in Parliament.</p>
        <p>_ [Book copyright of the above article, which forms one of a series contributed by leading members of the Eighty Club, is strictly reserved. Next week Lieuten- ant Gtrlyon Bellairs, It.N., will write on "Our Naval Supremacy as Affected by Protection"; and in the following week Mr. Sydney Buxton, M.P., Under- Secretary of State for the Colonies in the last Liberal</p>
        <p>Administration, will contribute an important article on "Retaliation as a Policy.";</p>
        <p>FAILURES IN ENGLAND AND WALES.—According to "Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, Nov. 13th, was 143, viz.:—New Bank- ruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 70; deeds of arrangement registered, 73. The res- pective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were :—Bankruptcies, 72; deeds. of arrangement, 79; total 151 ; beinga net decrease in bankruptcies of 15, and a net increase in deeds of arrangement in 1903, to date, of 343.</p>
