      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>THE COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>LANCASEHRE v. YORKSIIIRE.</p>
        <p>This match was played at Leeds before 8,003 people on Saturday Lancashire winning a fine game by 8 points to nil. There was no score at the interval, but later Hogg got in twice,.and Lomas placed a goal. Dickenson also got over but the try was disallowed. Final Lancashire 1 goal, 2 tries (8 pdtnts), Yorkshire nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCH.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. SEATON.</p>
        <p>A friendly match with the Cumberland organization, Seaton, took place at the Wilderspool enclosure on Saturday. With Dickenson away assisting the county. and Fish suspended opportunity was taken of giving a trial to Kenyon, who made such a capital debut with the "A" team a fortnight ago. Hockenhall appeared in the centre. The opening phases of the game were of an interesting natuce, and contrary to expectations the Cumberland men bustled the Warrington defence and Hallam wasrepeatedly called upon to clear. Eventually the home half-backs combined and Mahoney and Davies took the oval well inside the Seaton quarters, but the final transfer to Harris was ill-timed, and Petrie, who played a good game throughout, had not much difficulty in forcing the wirepallers back to neutral territory. Sucitssive efforts by Fletcher and Nicholson were checked by Isherwood and Kenyon. This player kicked finely across to Harris, who unfor- tunately overran the ball and a glorious opening was thus missed. Both Harris and Hockenhall made futile efforts to break through, so accurate WILS the tackling of the Cumberland mm. Near the interval Taylor got away from a pack at half-way, and defeating several of the visiting backs, served Harris at the opportune moment, and that player crossed, this being the only tangible record of the first half. Turning, Seaton pressed and their efforts were rewarded with an equaliz- ing try a few minutes after the resumption. Warring- ton made strenuous efforts after this, and Harris and Kenyon were applauded for brilliant individual efforts. Hallam and several others tried ineffectual shots at goal, and it was not until this half had been well spent, that Cooke tried a shot from close range, and this being successful Warrington thus woo by five points to three.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>FIRST DIVISION.</head>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>WIGAN v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Wigan, before 7,000 spectators. lath teams were weakened by absentees. Runcorn had the advantage in the first half, Farmer kicking a penalty goal. When Wigan attacked, the Runcorn defence was very sound. Wigan strove hard, and Mason equalised with a penalty goal. Runcorn were still forced to defend, which they did brilliantly. Rees then scored for Wigan. Final : one goal and one try (five paints); Runcorn.one goal (two points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>WIDNES v. HUNSLET.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v. HUNSLET.</p>
        <p>At Widne's, before 2,000 spectators. Both teams were as selected. Playing against the wind in the first half, Hunslet had the best of matters, and Walter Goldthorpe, Whiteley. and Hannah got over for Hunslet, and Tilley scored a try and kicked a goal for Widnes. Final:— Hunslet, three tries (nine points) ; Widnes, one goal and one try (five points). BATLEY v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>This match at littley, provei rather uninteresting. The teams were extremely well matched, and though the play was frequently located in dangerous proximity to the goal line neither side got.over. After Ellis had restarted Batley commenced rushing tactics. Wolsten- Wine dribbled down the field, and then picked up and passed to Rodgers, who was tackled just short of the line. P. Thomas. by grand play spoiled Batley's attack and was the qhief means of carrying the game away to the other end. The defence, howev%, prevailed. Old- ham broke away, and a try was 11:mostly averted. Thl game ended in a pointless draw. - •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>HUDDI4.:EBTIFII4O v. Lgial-t.</head>
        <p>HUDDI4.:EBTIFII4O v. Lgial-t.</p>
        <p>There were 5,000 roople pr2isertt at this match at Huddersfield. Inithe first half Moseley and Muxworth scored triel, Holroyd kicking a goal. Half-time score: —Huddersfield eight points; Leigh nothing. On chewy- ing ends Molyneux dropped a goal for Leigh, who afterwards fell away. Huddersfield scored five tries, by Deere (two), Moseley (one), and Sutcliffe (one), and Holroyd kicked a goal. Leigh were outplayed at all points of the game. Result :—Hudderstteld,.„two goals and 'seven tries (25 poitits) ; Leigh, one goal (two points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>HULL KINGSTON ROVEItS v. KEIGHLEV.</head>
        <p>HULL KINGSTON ROVEItS v. KEIGHLEV.</p>
        <p>The Rovers scored within five minutes of the start, of this match at Hull. Gordtm adding the goal. Keighley then gave a good account of themselves,but they found the Rovers too strong. Phipps got over at the corner, Gordon placing a fine goal. Fletcher added a try before the in‘ervol thus making the score read :—Htill Kingston Rovers, 13 points ; Keighley, nothing. The game was resumed in a heavy rainstorm, and the play was of ascrambling order. keighley were outclassed, but offered a stubborn resistance. Jagger scored a brilliant try for Keighley. Result :—Rovers, two goals and three tries (13 points) ; Keighley, one try (three points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>WARRINGTON INTER-WORKS LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON INTER-WORKS LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>NAYLOR'S v. WALKERS.</p>
        <p>This match was played on Saturday and resulted in a win for Naylor's by 19 points to nil.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS BROS, v. CROSFIELDS. On the ground of the former on Saturday, • a ve'ry. interesting game being witnessed. The "Wirepalers" forced the pace, and drew first blood through the medium of Tynan, and Morris converted. Shortly aftet a magnificent effort by Morris resulted in his running half the length of the field, and planting the ball undr. the stumps, the same player easily converting. Half-- time score was 10 points to nil in favour of Rylandg!, In the second half the leaders attacked strongly and added three further trios, all being scored by Phillips. , One was converted by Morris. The final 'score was: Rylands, three goals five tries (21 points) ; Crosfields,, i I.</p>
      </div>
