        <p>pre- VI gencrAl 'servent GTRT, from the country pre- ferred.—Apply S. If CCLFSTON PTRET,T, Viligen•</p>
        <p>Miss Robinson, of Liverpool, has replied to the letter from the Secretary of the National Service League, published in our last issue and criticising her remarks at the meeting of the Warrington Women's Liberal Association. It was our inten- tion to close the correspondence on compulsory military service last week,and we:publishea a notice to that effect, but we feel that we can hardly deny the right of a lady to the "last word,' and Miss Robinson is therefore afforded space for her admirable reply. She again confines herself to the humanitarian aspect of the question and ad- vances arguments against any form of conscription in this country, which are, we believe, unanswer- able. In our own view, already frequently ex- pressed in these columns, the National Service League proposals are indefensible even on military grounds. The standpoint from which we have. argued is that the condition of affairs in this country has not changed to any extent which justifies compulsory service for home defence.. In conclusion, we would emphasize one comment by Miss Robinson, which appears to have local appli- cation, viz : "If only the earnestness, energy and self-sacrifice of the younger members of the National Service League were turned in the direction of fulfilling the higher law of nations, how much they might accomplish for a world weary of this old barbaric yoke of militarism."</p>
