        <p>Most of the speaking at the meeting of Con- servatives and Unionists at Stockton Heath on Thursday evening was left to aspirants for Parliamentary honours. No fewer than three probable or possible candidates for neighbouring divisions unburdened themselves on the fiscal question and the trio uniformly sang the praises of Mr. Chamberlain and declared themselves followers of his policy. This much can at least be said in their behalf. They had Oa courage to daclare their convictions, and in this respect contrasted favourably with some Conser- vative candidates at recent elections who have studiously fought shy at expressing themselves in favour of a tax on food. There was no shyness of this kind on Thursday. They tried to whittle down the proposed bread tax to a very small thing but they were all in favour of it and prepared to go the whole hog with Mr. Chamberlain. The most fluent speaker was Mr. F. E. Smith, barrister-at-law, who is the candidate chosen to oppose the popular " Tay Pay" O'Connor in the Scotland division of Liver- pool. Mr. Smith is known to the Warrington public as a lecturer on history, and for ourselves we prefer to hear him in that capacity rather than when "on the stump." In venturing to assure his audience on Thursday,that England was enjoying a period of prosperity when the Corn laws were abolished he threw teachings of history to the winds. His declaration was juSt a little i‘it too much for even a Tory audience and we are not surprised that the statement was challenged. It is not necessary to rely on histori- call records for a picture of England under Pro- tection. Our fathers and grandfathers still living can and do truthfully attest that the period which immediately pre2eded the dawn of Free Trade was one of the dArkest of last century. There is certainly no wish on their part to see a return to like conditions.</p>
