        <p>Congratulation was the dominant note at the annual meeting of the Warrington District Nursing Association on Tuesday evening, and with just cause, for a credit balance of over £44 on the year's working certainly indicates sound progress. It must not, however, he thought that the need for liberal support is riot just as urgent. The purchase of the new Nurses' Home in Aippl. y-street has involved the Association in heavy expenditure and on this account a deficit of over £256 has to be met. Several speakers expressed surprise that the various churches and chapels do not lend more aid to such a deserving institution. While it is true that collections have been made in fourteen places of worship during the past year, it cAnnot b 3 said that the amount handed over is at all adequate for a large town like Warring- ton. In some cases, no doubt, the cry is that the church officials are hardly able to raise sufficient money to" make ends meet ", but surely no one can take eAception to a suggestion thrown out at the meeting. The Association would, it was stated, be satisfied if the congregations were afforded an opportunity of contributing on certain date. The amount of the usual offertory could then be deducted for church purposes, and the balance forwarded to the treasurer of the Nursing Association. Such a request cannot be thought unreasonable, and we hope it will be seriously considered and acted upon. The Nursing Association has, in our view, an equal claim with the Infirmary on public generosity.</p>
