        <p>Reference was made at the meeting of the Education Committee on Monday to the communi- cation which had been received from the Board of 'Education respecting higher education. The tenour of the communication is conciliatory but none the less emphatic. Whilst the local Authority is to be given time to frame a comprehensive scheme of higher education, it is clear that the Board intends to insist upor. the Authoriti taxing this work in hand with the least possible delay. We do not think there will be any desire on the part of the Warrington Committee to shirk their responsibility. The danger to be avoided if) providing a scheme of higher education is that of overlapping. It should be possible in any scheme which is provided, for a acholar with brains to pass by easy stages from the., elementary school to the Secondary school art" thence to the Grammar school, where if the stn- dent is desirous he can prepare for a UniversitY. course. Many difficulties require to be overcoie° before such a scheme can be evolved, but the Board of Education have some such idea in vief" Now that the power has been placed in the hail° of one central authority a comprehensive schevr, on the lines suggested should not be beyond til' range of possibility.</p>
