      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Grenadier Guards Band at Warrington.</head>
        <p>Grenadier Guards Band at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Opportunities of hearing the band of the Grenadier Guards are so few and far between that it was not surprising that the concert given in the Parr Hall on Sunday afternoon by that famous body of regimental instrumentalists should be eminently successful. The hall was filled in nearly every part, and the audience thoroughly appreciated the rich musical treat pro- vided through the enterprise of Mr. S. Vickers, of Liverpool. Of the selections by the band, which numbers over fifty performers, it would be difficult to speak too highly. The masterly precision and refinement of expression with which intricate works were interpreted left a last- ing impression on the mind of the listener. The per- formers worked together as one man and responded to the baton of their new conductor, Mr. A. Williams, his. Bac., with an alertness and intelligence which could not fail to produce an artistic success. Rossini's overture to "William Tell" was an excellent opening item and gave the audience a foretaste of the treat that was to follow. The suite from Grieg's incidental music to" Peer Gynt"were given with finish . combined with a thorough grasp of the spirit of the composer,and the wierd finale brought out the full capabilities of the band. A truly majestic render- ing of Tschaikowsky's "Grande Overture Solennelle " called forth cordial applause and the selections from Gonnod's"Faust"concluding with the stirring Soldiers' Chorus, were treated in musicianly style. In Damare's fantasia "Les echoes des bois " there was an excep- tionally meritorious piccolo solo by Corporal F. Gibbs. Notlthe least enjoyable features of the programme were the songs by Madame Laura Smart who infused taste and expression into her interpretations of" The mocking bird" and "Scenes that are brightest." Although the concert was long the interest of the audience never flagged, and this fact alone was a .striking tribute to the ability of the performers.</p>
      </div>
