      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>NOVEMBER 21et 1903.</p>
        <p>DOUBT as to the future prosperity of</p>
        <p>SOUTH AFRICA.</p>
        <p>South Africa is scarcely likely to survive the remarkable report of Mr. HENRY BIRCHENOUGH. Here we have an observer whose mind is in no way warped by prepossessions arising out of the late war. Mr. BIRCHENOUGH is an experienced man of business, who has judicially examined the resources of the South African Colonies. His favourable con- clusions are supported by evidence which leaves no room for misconception. Trade has expanded so much that South Africa bids fair to become the best customer we have. In ten years her total imports have been increased by 250 per cent. "The rapidity with which South Africa has come to the front as a great market for British manufactures is almost startling. Ten years ago Great Britain's exports to South Africa were valued at a little under £9,000,000; last year they almost reached £26,000,000. In 1893 South Africa stood sixth on the list of Great Britain's customers; last year ' she stood second." Ma. BIRCHENOGGIT adds: "It is no rash prediction that this year she will pas India, and stand first on the list as the largest buyer in the world of ,the produce and manufac- tures of the Mother Country." There is no danger of stagnation after the repair of losses caused by the war. "The large pur- chases of last year and this year represent not only the replenishment of stocks, but the beginning of the execution of enterprises planned before the war, aid only postporred by it. All my inquiries lead me to believe the present is no temporary boom, but the beginning of a period of great and sustained expansion." In!a, country lately, torn by civil war,these symptoms are highly satis- factory. Racial dissensions cannot paralyse the energies of the population when these are applied with such signal advantage to the development of commerce. The elections at the Cape indicate that parties are pretty evenly divided, and that there is a marked subsidence of political heat. But more significant still is the evidence that the local struggles of Progressives and Bondsmen do not in the smallest, degree retard the recuperation of the country. The mining industry in the Trans- vaal is seriously embarrassed by the want, of labour; but the chief danger thee is not in any failure of natural resources. It lies in the im- patience of delay. " May not capital grow weary of waiting for a change which seems so long de- layed, and seek other channels ?" On the other hand, the mining capitalists affirm that, with an adequate supply of labour, they will be able to spend £50,000,000 sterling on new equipment in the next fifteen years. But the most interesting feature of Mr. BIRCHENOUGH'S report is his analysis of foreign competition. Are British manufacturers holding their own in this great and growing market ? Are they adapting themselves to new conditions, and eamlating the flexibility which distinguishes the German and American traders ? Mr. BIRCH— ENOUGH notes some improvements in our commer- cial methods. " I speedily became convinced that many of the complaints one hears in South Africa are out of date, and that there are manu- facturers in all trades who are as ready and willing as their foreign competitors to meet and even to anticipate, the wishes of their customers. But in the trades in which com— petition is most keen this spirit is unfortunately not so conspicuous. British manufacturers of machinery, for example, are slow to grasp local conditions. Americans, on the contrary, "are brought up with the idea that whatever is done can be better done, and it is just this attitude of mind which makes them such formidable com- petitors." This attitude of mind, we are afraid, is not among the special characteristics of British enterprise. The Americans in South Africa benefit by" greater alertness and adaptability and less conservatism. Moreover, l..hey enjoy an advantage which calls for an explanation on the part of our shipping trade. American competition has been materially aided by the low freights at:which (competing British steamship lines have carried American cargoes from Now York to South Africa, as compared with the freights from British ports to the same destinations." The result of this arrangement is that "many American manufacturers have been brought into the market which otherwise would not have found their way there for many years to come." Here is a perference in favour of American goods established in one of our own markets by the carrying trade which is believed, justly in the main, to uphold our com- mercial supremacy. Mr. Birchenough declares that there ought to be uniformity of British and foreign freights, and that is a conclusion which nobody is likely to dispute. How this uniformity is to be brought about is a queation which might be usefully pressed upon the attention of ,British shipowners by the Board of Trade.</p>
        <p>The representation of Bewsey Ward on the Town Council is undergoiug rapid changes. Alder- rn an Pierpoint's elevation created the first vacancy and now a second vacancy has occurred from the saddest of all causes. Death has claimed Coun- cillor Brophy,an unassuming but staunch Catholic, Liberal, and Non-Trader. His career municipally had been short and uneventful, when that dread disease, cancer, laid him low. He fought bravely against the fate that encornpasse I him, but in the end Death triumphed.We are sure the event will be deeply regretted and that the sympathy of all who knew the deceased Councillor will go oht to his sorrowing widow and family.</p>
        <p>The last decade has been rendered striking in many ways, and in none more so than in the huge</p>
      </div>
