      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <p>tends that, after all, the English firms who im- ported last year 1,134,000 tons of steel bars, rods, 4c. did not do so to set them on the mantelpiece to look at. They bought them to make a profit out of them. And that they did make a profit the Revenue Returns sufficiently prove.</p>
        <p>Mr. Justice Cnannel's charge to the grand jury at Northampton the other day included a dictum that is almost certain to excite a controversy in the law journals. "The sooner advocates were given to understand that the old formulaq reserve my defence,' was no defence at all the better. A. defence, if real, slicalld be brought forward at the earliest possible moment." To say that the reservation is no defence is a mere truism; but to say that a poor man, placed in a compromising situation, should, as a rule, disclose his defence in petty session or police-court for the batteries of the Treasury to be discharged upon it before the actual trial is quite another matter. It would be interesting to know how many of us there are against whominnocent circumstances might not be turned to suspicion. How many busy people wrongly identified could tell, for instance, where they were and what they were doing at a given hour on a certain day two and a half months ago? And if you add to this—what is so often the case with the accused—some discreditable associations and a •cocksure witness or two for the prosecution, it is easy to see the jeopardy of an innocent person, There was a hint of this in the assurance with which two lady doctors declared that Miss Hick- man's death was due to violence! Given an awkward propinquity of sbme one with a chequered 'career, and the necktie would have seemed near adjusting. It is, of course, true that in regard to capital charges juries are very careful, because they feel that an error is irretrievable, and because Anyone charged with murder is sure of a good defence. But in the smaller cases where the weight of artillery is with the prosecution there has to be economy if a miscarriage is to be avoided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS. •</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS. •</p>
        <p>Conning Events. In connection with the services at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church to-morrow the Rev. W. C. J. Neville will preach in the mor4ing on "The Eternal Word," and the Rev. John Gunnell in the evening on ",The Ideal Life—Pagan and Christian." The Earl of Crewe and Mr. Henry Broadhurst, XP., will speak at Liberal demonstration to be held in the Parr Hall on, Tuesday evening next. Mr. C. E. Parker will preside. Another of the series of University Extension Lectures on " Dante" will be delivered in the Co- -operative Hall, Cairo-street, on Wednesday evening by Mr. Philip H. Wicksteed, M.A. The Mayor and Mayoress will hold an "At Home" at the Town Hall on Thursday next from 3.30 to 6 p.m. A sale of work and bazaar will be held in Hill Cliffe Baptist schools on Thursday, Friday, and Saturday next. Mrs. Baxter, of Dutton;Hall, will open the sale on Thursday and Mrs. Armitage will perform the ceremony on Friday. The second annual Warrington Eisteddfod and Musical Festival will be held in the Parr Hall on Saturday next. There has already been a splendid entry for the various contests and a special attrac- -tion will be the works glee competition. Professor Sadler having promised to give an address on Secondary Education, the meeting of the Literary and Philosophical Society, fixed for Novem- ber 27th, has been postponed. The Rev, W. J. Spriggs-Smith, vicar of Terrington St John's, Wisbeclf, will deliver an address on Chamberlainism "at Stockton Heath, on Tuesday, December Ist.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS NIINERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Personal. The Prime Minister has graciously exchanged fiscal pamphlets with Mr. H. P. Hughes of Stockton Heath, author of the "One-sided Free Trade v. Protection" articles that appeared in these columns. Mr. Balfour's pamphlet contains his "Notes on Insular Free Trade," together with his Sheffiel(.l9eech,</p>
        <p>captain and Brevet Major C. J. Steavenson, who served with distinction in the late Boer war has been elected for a military adjutancy at Warrington. You CANNOT no BETTER! Buy your boots and shoes at ALF. TAYLOR'S. Cheapest and best town. Fall From a Waggon.</p>
        <p>Richard Griffiihs:(36), who resides at 19, Kerfoot- street, was admitted to the Infirmary on Monday suffering from injuries to the hack. It is stated tha he fell from a wagon, aril a barrel rolled on top of him.</p>
        <p>SEE BOOTH &amp; SON'S SMART WINTER BOOTS. 29, BUTTERMARKET-ETREET.—Advt. .Cab Accident. John Taylor, aged 61, of Collins' lodging-house Stanley-street was walking along the gutter in Sankey- street on Monday evening when the driver of an ap- proaching hansom cab belonging to the Patten Arms Hotel,. hailed to Taylor to step on to the parapet.. In- stead of doing so Taylor, who is, rather short-sighted, stepped towards the centre Of the road, and was knocked down. The cab ran over him and broke his lag. The injured man was clnveyed to the Infirm- ary, where he was attended to. . LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER the CLOCK, BRID GE-STREET.—AdsPI- - as a Tragic Poet." This was the subject of an intensely interesting Paper read before the members of th:" Wycliffe Young People's Society On Mimday evening by Miss Corney. The essayist referred to Shakespeare's great genius in Picturing all phases of human life and character and drew special attention to his triumphs as a tragic poet. " Macbeth " and " Hamlet " were taken as two typical examples of Shakespeare's work in tragedy. The most striking passages were read by the essayist, and the motives and fears of the several characters were carefully analysed.—An interesting discussion followed, taken part in by the Chairman (Mr. James Ball), Miss E. Brown, Mr. J. Gauld, Mr. J. Hawthorn, and Mr. J. Critchley.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINER.A WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>A Globe-Trotter. • Oscar RadVaner,.a young Roummian, who is walk- .. ing over the whole world in seven years, for a Fager .of many thousands of pounds, and who has the liberal allowance of a year for illnesses and accidents, -arrived at Warrington on Monday afternoon and 'reported himself to the chief Constable. When he started upon his long journey.Ttadvatier was seven- teen years of 'age and, having been on his travels -three years and four months, be is now twenty-one. -So far, he has covered Siberia, China, Siam, India, Switzerland and other countries in Europe and Asia. On Tuesday he left Warrington for Manchester -en route for Scotland. From Scotland he will make a descent upon Wales, and afterwards proceed to Africa. Then he will go on to America and finish his task about three years hence at Quebec.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER the CLOCK for BES</p>
        <p>130c-r-7-siii-LA—NCASHIRE.—Advt. -Brtck-street Mission Band of Hope Society. The second monthly 'entertainment of the season in connection with the above Society was held on 'Thursday last, 'above 230 Members being presen Mr. Edward Roberts presided and gave a practica and encouraging address to the members. Recita- tions were given by Master Ernest Harding, Master William Smith, Misses Ethel Wainwright and Ethel Bibby. Miss Lettice Warburton sang two solos very creditably and Elsie Barton (a little girl about six Years old) sang very sweetly the solo entitled "Little Ones like me." Mr. Stanley Tomkinson very ably rendered two violin solos and a very good dialogue 'entitled "Whatever we may be " was given by six girls. The reading "All night in a grave" by Mr. Will iarn Wright caused much amusement: The meet- ing concluded with a 'laughable sketch entitled " l'addy's Mistake." The officials responsible for this .!:ptertainment were Messrs. William Wright, Joseph Harrison and James Shaw, jun., the music being in the hands of Mr. William Barton. UNDER the CLOCK or GOOD BOOTS.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <p>1 -</p>
        <p>Liberal Club Stewardship. Mr. and Mrs. Randall, late of the Cobden Club, Broughton, have been selected and appointed steward and stewardess of the Warrington Liberal Club out of 300 applicants. They take up their new duties on Monday.</p>
        <p>H. -AI. AsirroN's LIFE TONIC Is the KING OF TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, WarringtOn.—Advt. "The Yeomen of the Guard."</p>
        <p>- We are pleased to learn that as the result of the recent performances of "The Yeomen of the Guard " the •funds of the Warrington .Musical Society will benefit to the extent of :£134 10s. 3d. The performers and all concerned in the production are to be heartily congratulated upon the signal success which has crowned their efforts.</p>
        <p>"Lady Clancarty." We are informed that at the Parr Hall last week over 3,300 persons were present at the performances given by the St Mary's Dramatic Society. At the final performance some 1,100 persons were in attendance. Figures like these easily go to show what a popular- ity the Society enjoys and how appreciated is the splendid work of the Nursing Association. At the annual meeting of the Association a very cordial vote of thanks was passed. to the Hon Secs. of the Society for the welcome assistance given to the funds from last year's performances. It would be a great assist- ance to the Hon. Secs, if holders of tickets would kindly forward them to the,offices at 6 Mill-street as soon as possible.</p>
        <p>BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER the CLOCK.—Advt. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Theft at Winwick. At Warrington County Sessions on Wednesday, James Terry and James Fielding were charged with seizing a quantity of celery, valued 35., the property of James .Jepson, at Winwick, on October .19th. Terry admitted the theft, but Fielding said he was at home at the time named, and called his wife to give evidence to that effect. Terry also said that he was alone at the time. The bench decided to send Terry to gaol for a month, and Fielding was given the benefit of the doubt and discharged.</p>
        <p>B.A.SSINETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. GARLAND, 'Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Revival of the Mono-rail Scheme. Mr. Alex. Wilson, of Liverpool, proceeded to London on Tuesday in connection with the proposed revival of the Manchester-Liverpool mono-rail scheme, for which he acts as solicitor. An attempt to float the concern some time ago with a cap:tal of £2,100,000 failed. Now, however, the attempt is to be renewed with, it is believed, every prospect of success, owing to the more favourable state of the money market. When the first appeal was made to the public, big financiers fought rather shy of it, not because' they doubted the practicability ,of the scheme, but because they had already over-speculated. On the other hand, the small investor took up the shares eagerly, and there were many subscriptions of £lO, £307 and .£5O, occasionally 1•100, by people in a humbler station of life.</p>
        <p>FIRE GRATES of every description.—J. S. GAR- LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street,Warringion.—Advt. Local Shepherdry.</p>
        <p>A tea and stnokinwg concert were held at the White Bull Inn, Horsemarket-street, ln Saturday evening, in connection with the Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds, to celebrate the jubilee of the order. A large num- ber of members sat down to an excellent tea. A. smoking concert was subsequently held, when a pro- gramme of vocal and instrumental music was sub- mitted, the following taking part : —Messrs. J. Ratcliffe, Peter Knight, William Taylor, J. W. Yatei,a.nd others In responding to the toast Of "The Order," Mr. Joseph Booth, hon. sec„ said that after having paid the sum of ;t1.77 -ss. for sick pay, ±:,31 for, medical attendance, and ;eB9 for district bills, they were in a position to meet all claims, and also to Add 120 Bs. 3d. to the lodge fund,making a total of £2,032 4s. 3d. The number of members was 208. This welcome announce- ment was received with acclamation.</p>
        <p>Will of the Late Mr. T. Fletcher. Mr. Thomas Fletcher,of Grappenhall,near Warrington F.C.S., a managing director of Fletcher, Russell and Co., Limited, of Warrington, Pendleton, Manchester, and London, gas engineer and ironfounder, formerly a dental surgeon in Warrington, who 'died on the 20th September last, aged 63 years, having given two years ago £l,OOO for distribution among his work- people, -left estate valued at :t28,200 Bs. 110. gross, and £27,582 16s. 6d. net. Probate of his willY of the 26th:May, 1902, hasbeen granted to his nephew, Arr. Charles Clare, gas engineer, and to Mr. W. J. For- shaw, of Warrington. The testator bequeathed his scientific and technical books having his name plate therein and his personal belongings marked with his name or monogram to his son Thomas William, and his household effects to his daughter Mary; and he left his residuary estate in trust to . pay the income of £ll,OOO to his son Ernest Lyon Fletcher during his life, and subject to his life interest- in this sum the residuary estate is to be in trust for the testator's daughters, Mary Fletcher and Alice Healey, and his son Thomas William. Death of Councillor Brophy. _ _</p>
        <p>_ It is with regret we announce the death of Conn- cillor John Brophy, who passed away at the Royal In- firmary, Liverpool, on Thursday morning. About a month ago, Mr. Brophy proceeded to the Liverpool Infirmary to undergo a serious operation for internal cancer. The operation was .performed last Friday but the patient _gradually grew weaker and on Wed- nesday he became unconscious. He never fully re- gained consciousness and died shortly after seven o'clock on Thursday morning. Mr. Brophy entered public life only 12 months ago, but by his genial dis• position he had gained many friends. Last November he was • returned as the representative of Bewsey Ward his opponents being Mr. Samuel Gough who obtained 239 votes and Mr. W. J. Shaw who polled 127 votes. Mr. Brophy polled 290 votes. He served on several Corporation Commit- tees, and was also one of the Council's elected representatives on the Education Committee. The deceased gentleman was 50 years of age. For many years he had been the licensee of the Farmers Arms, Dallam-lane—The Mayor (Alderman Bolton) has written to the members of the Council informing them of the death of Councillor Brophy, and has invited them to attend the funeral on Monday next. The interment will take place in the Warrington Cemetery at 3 o'clock.</p>
        <p>THE WELL-KNOWN GENT'S "WA.UKEEZI " AND "G" Brands of Boots can be had from BOOTH &amp; Socr, Buttermarket-steet.—Sole agents.—Advt. The Mayor's Hobby.</p>
        <p>As probably most of our readsrs are aware, the Mayor (Alderman Bolton) takes a great interest and pride in the cultivation of orchids, and on Monday evening his Worship delivered an interesting lecture on the subject to the me,nbers of the Warrington Society and their -friends. Mr. Linnt-eus Gremlin, presided over a large gathering, and con,ratulated the Mayor upon his election to such a high Office. He did not think Alderman Bolton could have commenced his Mayoralty better than by lecturing before the Warrington Society. He Was an authority on his sub- ject and had 100,003 specimens in his orchid houses at Wilderspool..—The Mayor pointed out that orchids attracted so much interest on acconat of the eccentricity of the plants and the great difficulty that was experienced in , growing them. In order to be a successful orchid grower one had flra of all to study the climatic conditions bbtaining in the districts from which the orchids were brought, and then provide similar conditions artificially. Lancashire and Ches- hire had furnished more orchid growers in propor- tion than any other part of England, so that this part of the country had a good reputation so far as orchids were concerned. The plants and the districts peculiar 'to them were fully explained, and some • choic specimen flowers were- exhibited and afterwards , handed over to the ladies present.—A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the lecturer on the proposi- tion of Mr. Woodcock, seconded by Alderman Bennett, and supported by Mr. C. Madeley and Mr. E. J. Hall. A similar compliment was plid to Mr. Greening for pre:skiing on the motion of Mr. .T. Moore Murray.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>_ SCOTCH SHORTBREAD The "Crawford" Quality.</p>
        <p>HIGHLAND. Thick Cakes. LOTHIAN. Thin Sections, sugared on top. AYRSHIRE. Thin oblong Cakes, sugared on top. Sold by Grocers and Bakers everywhere.</p>
