      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday November 21, 1903 THE WARRINGTON BYE-ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday November 21, 1903 THE WARRINGTON BYE-ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The New Street. - A start has been made towards providing the forty- feet street at the back of Bridge-street. The demo- lition of property has already commenced, and tenants of existing premises are under notice to quit. The Macedonian Relief Fund. A list of subscriptions to this fund will be found on the first page. Already nearly A:5O have been subscribed. GIVE THEM A TRrAL.—ALF. TAYLOR'S wonder- ful boots for everybody.. Confirmation Service. In the absence through illness of Bishop Chavasse, a confirmation service was conducted in St. Barnabas' Church on Monday evening by the Right Rev. Dr. Royston, assistant Bishop of Liverpool. Candidates were presented from St. Barnabas', St. Peter's, St. Ann's, St. Paul's, and Orford Churches. There was a large congregation. Temperance Meeting. _ . _</p>
        <p>The first of a series TA meetings in connection with the London and North. Western Railway Temperance Union was held in the Arpley rooms on Sunday even- ing. There was a, good attendance. Mr. Thomas Dale took the chair. and addresses were given by him and Mr. Henry' Taylor, Police Court Missionary, FoR CLASS AND CHINA. of every description try J. S. GARLA.ND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and • 55. Bridge-street, Warrington.--Advt. Board of Guardians.</p>
        <p>An interim meeting of the Board was held on Wednesday m)rning. Mr. A. Brooks presided and. there were also present: Mrs. Clay, Rev. Father Wilson, Messrs. S. Davies, J. Payton, T. H. Ashcroft, A. J. Jolley, G. Pillinq, G. Mather, W. S. Sutton (assistant clerk) J. F. Awbery (workhouse master) and A. Wriglit (:superintendent Padgate schools.)</p>
        <p>A GOOD RESOLUTION.—Wear ALF TAYLOR'S Wonderful Boots.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Oriord and Bewsey Ward Vacancies. IMonday next at five p.m. is the latest hour at which nominations can be handed in for vacancies in the Bewsey and Orford Wards, and the elections will take place on the following Monday. The only nominations received by the Town Clerk up to the present are those on behalf of Mr. James Artnitage Rushworth, licensed victualler, 212, Winwick-road, who will contest Orford Ward as a Conservative in opposition to Mr. Pred Stott, of the Independent Labour Party. The Liberal Committee for Bewsey Ward met on Saturday evening under the presidency of Mr. Joseph Owen when it was unanimously decided to invite Mr. Thomas Pemberton to become the candidate. Mr. Owen Toole who had been mentioned as a probable candidate, was present and announced that under the circumstances he should not saek election. If they were without a candi- date and would care for him to stand, he was prepared to fight for the Liberal causaat any future time. We understand that after care fulconsideration r. Pember- ton, who would have had the support of the Non- Trading Committee, has declined the invitation, and another meeting of the Liberal Ward Committee will be held this (Saturday) evening. Owing to the death of Councillor Brophy another vacancy is created in Bewsey Ward. Messrs. Plinston and S. Gough have also been mentioned as possible candidates. A largely-attended meeting in support of the candi- dature of Mr. Fred Stott in Orford Ward was held in the Co-operative Hall, Winwick-street, on Thursday evening. The chair was occupied by Mr. 3. Podmore, Jun., president of the Trades and Labour Council. and the speakers included Cduncillor Tom Cook, of Man- chester, Mr. John Upson, and the speaker.</p>
        <p>colonies has given us 10 per cent. more and Australia would give us 10 per cent. more of trade if we would only accept it. Now that we saw millions of hands of our own flesh and blood who had fought side by side with us in South Africa, waiting to carry out the policy of Mr. Chamberlain and make one great united Empire —if we failed to respond we would be to blame. Shorttspeeches were also made by Mr. Bindloss, Mr. H. J. Westbrook and Mr. Vere B. Davies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MUSICAL SOCIETY'S CONCERT IN PARR HALL.</head>
        <p>THE MUSICAL SOCIETY'S CONCERT IN PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>The Musical Society would seem to be just now under the spell of Dr. Elgar. The "Coronation Ode" and a part-song of his wound up the last season just before Lent, and the rather large dose of four items in the programme of Wednesday's concert may be fairly said to preserve the 'continuity of the society's present predi- lection for this composer's works. Moreover we are promised "King Olaf" for the next of this series of concerts. Whether in the society's interests the experiment is altogether a wise one of devoting so much attention to the compositions of any one nian,even though he may happen to be just now to the fore in musical circles, remains to be seen. But of the four Elgarian items in Wednesday's programme it cannot be said that all were of striking interest. The unaccompanied part- song "Weary wind of the West" was certainly the least interesting of the vocal composi- tions. Indeed it came dangerously near to being tedious in the monotony of its general effect. The two choral songs from "The Bavarian Highlands" —" The Dance" and "Lullaby "—(the first of which has rather recently been heard before at these concerts) were, on the other hand, full of sparkle and verve and served to instance how captivating Dr. Elgar can at times be in writing for voices as well as for orchestra, The singing of the society's members shewed many points of excellence. In the part-song especially the balance of tone in the various masses of the voices was particularly well preserved, while in the " Lullaby " the collective contraltos had a good opportunity, which they made the most of, in dealing with the broad melody which here, quite exceptionally, falls to their lot. Finishing with the Elgar music, the" Pomp and Circumstance" march presents its composer in his not infrequently capricious mood, and familiarity with it seems only to confirm a certain dissatisfaction felt at the enforced association of the two very strongly con- trasted sections of this composition. The first is surely either too scrappy in. subject-matter and too jerky in its tempo and rhythm. or the second has too much of the cantabile element about it, to make a happy union. The main choral item in the programme of the con- cert was the "Hiawatha's Wedding-feast" section of Coleridge-Taylor's setting of Longfellow's blank verse. Although in the nature of things it struck a more cheer- ful note than "The Death of Minnehaha" heard at these concerts not long ago, we doubt whether the work proved as potent as did that section in interesting the listener arkl rivetting his attention. Sufficient, relief to the dull level of the continuous choral narration is not afforded in the break made by the tenor solo " Onawa,y, awake!" fascinatingly lovely though the melody and harmony may here be. The work therefore stirred little enthusiasm, albeit chorus, orchestra and principal tenor alike did most conscieatious work in its interpretation. Miss Helen Jaxon rendered the seem " Softly sighs " from Der Freischatz with all the commingled alternations of feeling and the brilliance of vocalisation which are indispensable for the due interpretation of so dramatic dn. excerpt. Needless to say she was vociferously reealled. Nor was Miss Jaxon less successful in her singing of Gounod's serenade "When the voice of thy lute,' the voluptuous melody of which is so greatly enhanced by the fine violin obbligato. In addition to "Onaway awake ! " in " Hiawatha," Mr. Gregory Hest sang two bracketted songs later in the evening, and earned for himself such plaudits as could only be appeased by their being responded to as an encore. Mr. William. Henley's violin solos with orchestra were not in them- selves of absorbing interest, but as a means of exhibit- ing his marvellously perfect technique they were first rate. Something more soothing and grateful was cer- tainly necessary to calm the state of excitement into which one's feelings were worked by much of the eXeCUtant gymnastics with which Mr. Henley inflicted us. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. F. 14Crossley was at his accustoMed nost as conductor, and Mr. W. H. Payton and Mr. R. H. Pear- son similarly discharged their usual duties as organist and leader -of the orchestra respectively. Theaudience MIS very disappointing in its sptrcity of numbers and, adverting to the recent huge success scored by a number of the society's members in the domain of light opera, it becomes a serious question whether the purely edlicational object the society has in view might not be wisely modified. A policy which would make it possible and practicable to devote a measure of effort to the providing of a form of enter- tainment which if less classical need not be less elevating, would, we venture to think, win for the society a place of high esteem in the hearts of all music-loving Warringtonians.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>At the " Wedding Feast."</head>
        <p>At the " Wedding Feast."</p>
        <p>Though not personally acquainted with the dusky warrior I was nevertheless one of the guests at Hiawatha's wedding feast on Wednesday. We had a great time, for Hiawatha did the thing well, and the mena would not have disgraced a Mayoral function, though it might have surprised some of the Aldermen and other elected epicures. Old Nokomis, the chef, excelled herself on the occasion and dished-up ahighly- respected sturgeon named Nahma, a friend of his of the pike tribe bearing the classic title Maskenozha, together with peinican, buffalo marrow, and the like. One doesn't get these sort of delicacies every-day. buffalo marrow for instance, is a dainty quite as distinct at one of these gatherings as is haggis at a Scotch banquet. In fact fthink it takes pride of place even over this latter famous dish. It requires a whole buffalo to supply one marrow—you can't get this variety from the market; gardener—whereas, for all I know to the contrary you can get haggis from any old hag. The feast was unmistakably a feather in the cap of Old Nokomis, who earned further encomiums for her self-denial in "tasting not the food before them," but "waiting on the others." In addition to being chef, Old Nokomis was apparently 1V1.C., forafter the feasting she thought- fully filled up the pipes of peace, passed them round, and called on various accomplished guests to take their turn in the subsequent entertainment,. There was "the handsome Pau-Puk-Keewis," whom the people (some- what rudely I thought) called the Storm-Fool, and.the Warriors coward, Shaugodaya, idle Gambler, Yena- dizee, and other opprobrious epithets. Whether it was the buffalo-marrow or the wild rice of the river that caused them to so far forget themselves could not be determined; bit Pan- Puk-Keewis heeded not their insults, and altogether conducted himself in a manner which betokened good breeding and evoked warm approval. As a dancer he took the cake—not a yellow one by the way. 'Tis not everyone who could "twirl the light fantastic "wearing deerikin leggings fringed with hedgehog quills, fox- tails on his heels, a feather-fan in one hand, and a pine in the other. He might have put his pipe down for the nonce, but they are great smokers in the far West. Hardly had the applause died down and Pan-Pnk-Keewis resumed his seat, than Chibiabos, a friend of our host Hiawatha, regaled the party with a song full of poetic feeling, and such as one would hardly have expected from him seeing the hearty nieal he had just made( He was obviously not "too full for utterance," however, Last but not least came Tagoo the great boaster, and a crony of Old Nokomis's. Now in some countries a blunt people would call Tagoo a bit of a liar,butat Hiawatha's party they seemed to rather like embroideTy tacked on to the exploits they were called upon to admire. lagoo was of the genuine lick-creation type. He didn't boggle over telling a whopper, and if anybody from the tune of Rameses to Edward VII. had per.formed any feat that he could not surpass—well,he would like to meet the gentleman that's all! I rather liked Tagoo. He had a fine imagination, and being a scribe myself we had things in common, but when he begun to yarn about some old fossil descending from 'the evening star I thought it time there was a line drawn somewhere. The warriors were getting a bit restive. Apparently there is a limit to what the North-American Indian will believe, and to avoid any tomahawking or bloodshed the proceedings were somewhat summarily wound up. 1 In the word of the official chronicler (one Longfellow) "The wedding guests departed, Leaving Hiawatha happy 1 With the night and Minnehaha." TAGOO TILE SECOND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>CONSERVATIVE MEETING AT STOCKTON HEATH, -- Mr. Chamberlain "the Faster Horse." MR. F. MONKS J.P. AND POLITICAL " SOLOMONS."</head>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE MEETING AT STOCKTON HEATH, -- Mr. Chamberlain "the Faster Horse." MR. F. MONKS J.P. AND POLITICAL " SOLOMONS."</p>
        <p>A well-Attended meeting of Unionists was held in the 'Church room, Stockton Heath, on Thursday evening. 1 Sir Gilbert GreenalL Birt. 'Presided and there were present on the platform the lion. Alan de Tatton Egerton, At. P. Mr. J. H. Welsford (Unionist candidate for the Crewe division) Mr. F. E. Smith (Unionist candidate for the Scotland division of Liverpool) Mr. Charles L. Sampson (Unionist candidate at the last, election for the Northwich division),Alderman F s mb, Alderman Smethurst, Messrs. E. Woods, E. W. t indloss W. B. Roberts, William Richardson. J. Fulton - ..'th, Vero B. Davies (chairman) and Mr. H. J. Westbrook (hon secretary of the Local Committee) E. J. Hall, Lance Bentley, J. H. Clarke, E. Draper, James Hepherd C. D. Parkinson, W. Bainbridge. The ladies on the platform were Lady Greenall, Mrs. Henry Greenall,Mrs Welsford, Mrs. Pigot, Mrs. Vere Davies and Miss Murray.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>LETTERS FROM MR. PARR AND MR. F. MONKS.</head>
        <p>LETTERS FROM MR. PARR AND MR. F. MONKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Charlton Parr, writing from Staunton Park, Staunton-on-Arrow regretted his inability to be present. Ile need not assure them, he continued, how pleased he should have been to have oncelagain assisted in giving a very jhearty welcome to their excellent member for the Knutsford division, Mr. Tatton Egerton and to the three would-be (and he hoped in due time will be) members for other neighbouring divisions. it might surprise their member to notice the growth and de- velopment of Stockton Heath, since'he • last visited it, and he hopedhe would be satisfied by his reception,that all his good friends and supporters were still to the front and with them a large army of recruits from the Primrose League habitation, ready to do battle under the banner of the Primrose League and to score a victory for him and the great constitutional cause. Mr. F. Monks, writing from Walton Old gall, said: —"I find I have an engage neat for the 19th, and I shall not be able to attend your meeting. As regards the question now before the country I think Mr. Balfour is quite right in claiming to be free to negotiate in reference to tariff reform. It is, after all, more a workingman's question. If he is content to see one industry after another destroyed he will wake up some day to find himself dependent upon charity for his daily bread. Our menare working from 4to 5 days a week. If they were working U days and earningfrom ss. to 10s. a week more in wages, they could afford to pay Bd. to 10d. per week more for food, but Mr. Chamberlain says it will cost nothing extra. You may depend upon it he knows more about this question than his critics, and I give him credit for quite as much honesty of purpose as those political Solomons who have ventured to decide this question before they knew what it was.—Yours faithfully, F. MONKS." (Applause.) Tim Chairman said their present member had repre- sented the division for twenty years, and in the last eleven years he had not been opposed. At the next election,however, opnosition was threatened, and in a wide and scattered divisiony'of that description, it was of the utmost importance that over-confidence should not be allowed to get hold of the Unionist party.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>FREE TRADE FOUNDERED ON A ROCK,</head>
        <p>FREE TRADE FOUNDERED ON A ROCK,</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. Welsford moved a vote of confidence in his Majesty's Government, heartily endorsing the view that the time had fully come when the fiscal policy of the United Kingdom must be considered in order to secure reciprocal treatment from foreign nations. Re- cently, he said, he had noticed that a number of Free Traders gathered together in Warrington had claimed that Free Trade was founded on a rock, but he would amend that and say that Free Trade had foundered on the rock of foreign tariffs. (Laughter and applause.) Free Trade in principle was as sound as a bell, but in practice it was as unsound as any bell that ever was cracked. (Laughter.) The backbone of agriculture, under the system of Free Trade, had been broken, and a long list of British industries had been crippled.</p>
        <p>POINTED QUESTION FROM THE BACK OFTHE HILL.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. E. Smith, 13 trristlr-at-law. ieconded the pro-- position. He wxs proc33ding to argue that England was in a prosperous condition at the time the Free Trade policy was adopted, when a man at the back of the hall called his attention to Carlyle's "Past and Present." Mr. Smith replied with n, sneer that the questionerhad not p3rhaps, the intelligence to compre- hend the b3ok. Th 9 suns questioner then impure:l if the speaker doubted the comprehension of Mr. Ritchie? Mr. Smith replied that Mr. Ritchie had recently an- nounced that he agreed with Arr. Balfour. Not to be outdone, the questioner asked where was Mr. Balfour sitting, was he not on, the fence Mr. Smith proceeded to state that Mr. Ritchie sup- ported by his vote in the House of Commons the imposition of the duty on corn which was nothing but a step in the direction which Arr. Chamberlain advocated. I.r. Smith then dismisSed,the subject by advising the Tiestioner who had asked him "two singularly foolish questions" not to interrur, him but to reserve his questions to the close of the meeting.</p>
        <p>TIU MEMBER FOIL TIIE DIVISION AND "TFIE FASTER HoasE."</p>
        <p>The Hon. Alan de TattonEgerton,M.P.,contrasted the policies of Mr. Balfour and Mr. Chamberlain. The policy of the ex-Calonial Secretary. he said, was the much more far-sighted of the two, although, no doubt, it included the smaller pati,ly of retaliation. in his opinion, the great thing to which we had to pay the strictest attention was to bring our Colonies into closer contact with oarselves by being able to supply theni with what they required in the shape of manufactured goods, an-I te parcnas from them wh Lt th3v were in a position to produce and to sell to its. (Hear, hear.) 44.1tjumgh he supported Mr. Balfour thoroughly, he sup- ported Mr. Chamberlain equally so—(hear, hear)— because he was firmly convinced he was going in the right direction, and because he believed, if he might use the term, he was the faster horse of the two.</p>
        <p>THE COST OF LIVING.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chtrles L. Samson also supported the resolution Mr. Chamberlain's policy; he said, if effectutlly ctrried out,-would. mean Free Trade in the truest sense of the word. Mr.Cha n'ierli.in hid rises to the occasion. He had seen what the necessities of the CaS3 were and he had suggested a p3licy whereby this country and her Colonies could be mere closely united—the bond strengthened and not weak.ehed. An,lanse.) If thr/se proposals would in prtctice anything to the cost of living amongst the working classes, he should not for a moment support the7l,.b it he w is content to take Mr. Chamberlain's assurtnce, when he pledged his re- putation—and it WAS a. gr ett reoatation t&amp; toss (v3olanse) —that the cost of living iv. mild not be increased by OEI3 farthing. (Applaase.) A tw3 &amp;tilling tax on corn would not increase the price of brawl. The price of bread was raised by the sante inexorable law which .settled everything, and that was the law of supply and demand. This duty of two,,shillings a qutrter on clrn just furnished a handle of objection for their opponents but when it was carefully examined, that objection was , nothing but a myth. Mr. Chamberlain was. a man with a great policy, a policy which if carried out would un- ;oubtedly give more constant employmept and better wages to the Working men, and what the working men of this country had to decide was whether they would Anpport him or not. .</p>
        <p>lit% Edwin Woods, in mOvino•o a vote of thanks to the sp3akers, said he must coogratalate those gentlemen on being on the winning side, for he b3lieved Mr. Chamber- lain would carry the country. (Hear. hear and applause.) He contended that so-called Free Trade had not done for the country what their opponents said it had done— in short, in his opinion, it was ruining the country. He himself had beau as bad as anyone else foi he was a supposed Free Trader. That was because he had not gone below the surface of the matter and had not thought it out until Mr. Chamberlain had come forward with his proposals. (Applause.) After that he per- ceived that he had been on the wrong track. He could well remember the time when we sent more goods to America than they sent to us. That was not so to-day. In 1902 we sent to America goods to the valneof 23 millions ; they sent to us goods to the value .pf 127 millions--a difference of 10-I millions. Who was .to pay for that? Englmd. He begged of them to divert that trade to their Colonies. (Applause.) It had been said by some great speakers that their Colonies did not, want them. His reply to that was that Canada,had given us a preference of 33.1 per cent. over foreign nations. The Cape had given us 25 per cent. more than to the foreigners. other</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>MAYOR AT CHURCH. Sermon by the Rev. B. Percival.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR AT CHURCH. Sermon by the Rev. B. Percival.</p>
        <p>in accordance with, a well-established custom the newly-elected Mayor (Alderman Bolton) attended Divine service on Sunday morniag and invited the members ofthe Corporation officials and leading towns- men to accompany him to the Parish Church. A large number responded to the invitation and the procession assumed larger dimensions than usual. The proces- sionists assembled at the Town Hall shortly before ten o'clock. Much rain had fallen earlier in the morning. and the weather was of a threatening character, but fortunately the rain kept off during the walk to the Parish Church. The procession,which was marshalled by 1 the Chief Constable, was headed by the Volunteer regi- ' mental and bugle bands. under the conductorship of Bandmaster Richurdson. A fairly-large number of Volunteers Wereon partde, including representatives of the Mounted infantry and the Cyclist section. The men were under the command of Lieut-Colonel Edels- ton, and the other officers on parade included Captain A. F. Bundock,the new adjutant, Major J. D. Fairclough, Major B. Fairclough and Quarter-Master A. F. G. Potter. Following the Volunteers were members of the St. John Ambulance Brigade, fire brigade, police force, and the sword and mtce be trers (Sergeants Bell and Dean.) The Mayor was accompanied by Mr. R. Pierpoint, M.P., and the Town Clerk and the following members of the Corporation and officials: Alderman Fairclough (deputy mayor), Sutton, Francomb, Heaton, Pierpoint, Smethurst, Hutchinson and Bennett; Councillors Dodds, Burton, Davies, Rogers. Barton, Stough, Lord, Tinnion, Wilkinson, Peacock. Ellison, Thornton, Maginn, Ash- croft, John Roberts, Wilson, and Forshaw; Mr. Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk). Mr. J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), Mr. W. H. Woodcock (assistant treasurer and. colleetor),Mr. T. Longdin(surveyor),MrA.M.Ker (assist- ant surveyor), Mr. G. F. F. White (magistrates' clerk), Mr. W. S. Haddock (gas engineer), Mr. F. Taylor (gas works' secretary),Mr. F. V. L. Mathias (chief assistant electrical engineer), Dr. J. Gaest Gornall (medical officer of health), Mr. E. Place (chief inspector of markets), Mr. C. Madeley (curator and librarian), Mr. J. Moore -4.l,nrray (director of education), Mr. C. B. Aylward (aft master), Mr. F. Timperley (assistant collector)._</p>
        <p>The local bench of migi,stiat'es was represented by Messrs. T. Birchall, R. T.l Fairclough, J. W. Hayward, and S. Wright. The members of the Board of Guar- dians present were :—The Chairman (Mr. Brooks), and Messrs. W. Houghton, G. Pilling, A. Jolley, J. Payton, G. Anther, T. Halton, S. Sutton (assistant clerk), Mr. T. J. Garnett and Ntr. F. I-I.Aukland (relieving officers), Amongst the local townsmen we noticed Messrs. G. Clay, T. Birtles, R. G. Flenshall, C. S. Burgess, W. Holbrook, J. Mason Josiah Longland, J.Bibby, Fowler, G. Wallington,J. Bahey,Collin,Gandy, Lunt, andT. May A large number of spectators lined the route of the procession. The processionists were met at the church gttes by the clergy and choir. The clergy present were the Revs. Canon 'Willis (she Miyor's chaplain), E. J. Willcocks (headmaster of the Grammar School), B. Percival (vicar of St. Peter's), A. S. Cameron, A. F. White, and G. W. Dickenson (assistant clergy).</p>
        <p>CY • The seats in the ntve were reserved for ihose who had joined in the procession and when the service commenced the church was completely filled. The lessons were read by the Rev. A. S. Cameron, and the Rev. E. J. Willcocks. The choir sang the anthem "0 for acloaer wtlk with God," the solo being effectively sung by Mr. J. Parkinson. Mr. F. M. Dtrbl, Mus. Ba,c., presided at the organ with marked ability. The sermon was preached by the Rev. B. Percival, who spoke on the words "Beholdall souls are Mine;" (Ezekial 18c, 4 v.) When the Rector asked him, he said, to preach in the Parish Church on the occasion of the Mayor's visit he thought how very kind of him it was to do so, although he felt how unworthy he was of the honour. Still, it seemed a call which he must obey. He remembered during the later stage of our great struggle in South Africa, an eminent statesman said, in the course of a great speech, that he would welcome any effort in the cause of peace from whatever quarter it might come, whether from the Boers themselves or from a gathering of two or three in a wayside inn. Therefore if he could, that morning, utter a word or suggest a thought that would enable men of various opinions and aims to apprehend more fully the Father- hood of God and the brotherhood of man he would feel that he had done something to further the cause for which the Master came. There was a difference between the utterance of a man of science and the utterance of a prophet. In science we asked for reasons; the prophet appealed to that within us. Underlying the words of his text was a principle far-reaching in the exhaustless range of its application to various depart- ments of human life, and it was to that principle he asked their attention. The words of his text formed the principle that "no man liveth to himself." Take the subjects of to-ity that were occupying men's minds whether world-wide, national, or local, and they found very great divergencies of opinion on any given subject. A thousand years hence it might not matter much as to which opinion wits held on most subjects, but it would matter a great deal whether they settled the question by mutual generosity and forbearance. Let them look for a moment at one great question which for many months past had been agitating the hearts and minds of men, namely, the question of religiouseducation. What varied opinions they had upon it! He did not speak of the men who would have no religion taught in, our schools at all, for he believed the vast majority of men would have some form of religion taught, and even the passive resisters, though not willing to have any particular creed taught, would nevertheless prefer to have portions of the Bible taught without any explanation being given on the part of the teacher. Now, whatever their opinions might be—as Churchmen, Nonconformists, or Roman Catholics—the question could not be settled without their recognition of the great underlying principle that parents had a right to have their children trained in the doctrines in which they believed. Whatever may have happened in the past, whatever shadows, sins, backslidings, wanderings away from God, he wished them to remember and cline to the principle he had mentioned in the words "All souls are Mine." The preacher proceeded to elaborate his subject under the three heads (1) that we have a Message of love to humanity, (2) that it is a charter of h um= rights, (3) that it is a statute of service. The National Anthem was sung at the close of the service. A collection was taken on behalf of the choir funds. The Mayor and Corporation returned to the Town Hall in processional order, but the rain which fell caused the processionists to put up their umbrellas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WARRINGTON LIBERAL CLUB. Presentation to the Steward.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON LIBERAL CLUB. Presentation to the Steward.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the members of the Warringtan Liberal Club was held on Thursday evening for the purpose of presenting Mr. and Mrs. Lamb, the manager and manageress, who are about to leave Warrington for Altrincham, with a pair of bronzes as a token of the esteem in which they have been held during the four years they have had charge of the club.—Mr. BIRKHEAD presided, and said he had always found Mr. and. Mrs. Lamb to be very obliging and very civil to everyone. He was sorry they were going from amongst them but wished them every success in their new sphere of life. (Applause). Mr. JAMES STRANGE also spoke in high terms of Mr. and Mrs. Lamb and said he considered they had always done their duty. He was pleased to know that Mr. and Mrs. Lamb were so well respected by the mem- bers of the Liberal Club. (Applause). Mr. Councillor WrLK NsoN- mentioned that he knest nothing of the presentation and was surprised that he had not been approached and given an opportunity of contributing. However, he and Mr. Bibby would endeavour to complete the presentation of the bronzes by adding a time-piece. (Applause). Mr. JAMES WOODS then made the presentation. He said that every penny subscribed was given willingly by the members, and each expressed the hope that Mr. and Mrs. Lamb would do well, after they had left the club and gone into their new business. Mr. and Mrs. I Lamb had striven to do their best in the interests of the club generally and they ware always civil and obliging. He would also like to ask Miss Lamb to accept a gold locket pendant from the same friends, who wished her every success. (Applause). _ Mr. LAMB cordially thanked the members, and said that the fact that the members were perfect strangers four years ago enhanced the value of the bronzes, and sheri,ed the kindly feeling that had existed. He trusted these happy relations would not cease when they left Warringtton. He should be pleased to welcome any one of his friends to his new home, and the door of friend- ship would always be open to them. (Applause). Three cheers were given and the health of Mr. and Mrs. Lamb was drank. Afterwards the company sang "For he's a jolly good fellow." Mr. FORD proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman and those who had arranged the testimonial, and added testimony to the good work of Mr. Lamb.—Mr. LOWE, seconded, and endorsed the remarks of every speaker, as did Mr. J. RUSSELL. who was one of the promoters of the testimonial. He also wished Mr. and Mrs. Lamb ' every success in their new business. .</p>
        <p>After the presentation a very pleasant evening was scent, several members contributing songs &amp;c, Mr. Birkhead gave almmorons song; Mr. E. A. Sanders a recitation; Mr. J. Woods a Immorons song; Mr. James Strange an old English song, and Mr. Lawton and Mr. Stanley, songs.</p>
        <p>ELASTIC STOCKINGS, KNEE CAPS, &amp;C.—For Varicose Veins and all cases of Weakness and Swelling of the I.4egs, Sprains, &amp;c. : they are porous, light in texture, and inexpensive and are drawn on like an ordinary stocking.—Prices Gd., is. 6d. and 10s. Gd. each. Postage free.—ViETE AND WRIGHT, Surgical Instru- ment. and Truss Makers, 98, Renshaw-street, Liverpool. ' —Advt.</p>
      </div>
