      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>FISCAL PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>THE FISCAL PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>Protection in Sweden.</p>
        <p>Sir —I enclose an extract from a letter written by a Swedish gentleman to a friend of mine who has passed the letter on to me. authorising me to quote from it provided I do not publish the writer's name. I have purposely abstained from correcting the blunders that the writer makes in his English.—Yours'obediently, Grays Inn, HAROLD COX. Nov. 12th, 1903. "I follow Mr Chamberlain's propaganda very keenly. He says that our country under the Pro- tectional system has made great progress. Non-: sense! This country was a cheap country to live in and therefore the specialities of this country covld be so cheap produced, so one could sell them abroad and thus bring money back. Swedish iron and steel manufactures found their way anywhere. So did Swedish -s7ood-paper, and many other articles. And now ? First got the farmers protection and it became very dear to live. The industry could not produce cheap enough owing to the increased costs for labour. And afterwards the industry got protection in order to safe the home market for it. They (?This) protection shaped a lot of manufac- tories in the country, and some years after they competed hardly (?keenly) for the Home market and spoiled themselves. To think of export was impossible' because the costs for producing were too high. It was not possible to compete abroad. There is no more smoke out of many big manufac- tories' chimneys. Enormous money is lost and about 50 to 100 thousands young powerful people prefer per annum to try their chance in America. We other unfortunate who remain at home, we have to pay taxes, for every piece of bread or flesh, to our farmers. And how much? We pay twice as much for such provisions as you will, and as one does in, for example, Copenhagen where there is free trade. All in favour of the big farmers. Our country is going ti) be ruined in this system. I hope the Englishmen will have common sense enough not to follow Mr. Chamberlain. Dear friend, excuse that I lay my experience in this matter and receive my best regards to you and your wife."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Mr. Woods and Mr. Cox.</head>
        <p>Mr. Woods and Mr. Cox.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Cox,;11 his reply to.Mr.Edwin Woods,entirely evades the point at issue, and gives details about im- ported iron and steel, and prices, which if they prove anything only sheW that the loss in wages and labour to this country through foreign importation of steel is much larger than Mr. Woods makes out. Mr. Woods I gave 1,134,000 tons as being imported, Mr. Cox 1,170,670 tons, and "The Iron and Coal Trades Review" in its issue of the 6th inst 1,090,000 tons; and I would recom- mend Mr. Cox to read this article, as it fully bears out Mr. Edwin Woods' arguments. It does not matter if the quantity imported be a little more or less for the sake of the argument. I calculate that upon imported steel bars and rods valued at £4 ss. per ton or thereabouts, ' this country loses in labour, on coal, limestone, at the 1 blast furnace, in the rolling mills, and in railway rates,l £3 per ton on every ton imported, for the sake of a gainl of 2s. 6d. to ss. per ton in price to the importer. Mr. Cox seems to imply from his details that this imported steel is of special quality, and cannot be manufactured in this country This is very misleading, and Mr. Cox as a labour_ leader should be the last person to make such a statement. I can point him to furnaces and steel works both on this coast in South Wales and on the east soast, that have closed down and are closing down, that could have manufactured this imported iron and steel or the bulk of it. These industries have been killed by an unfair competition and the warkinen dis- persed. Capital is very sensitive, and will seek the place for investment which gives the greatest security and incentive. When I came to this district thirty years ago, there was practically a free market on the continent for its manufactures, but Prince Bismarck in 1879 proposed and carried in the German Parliament a Bill to protect German industries, and created a high tariff wall amounting to 25s per ton against British steel entering Germany. What has been the result of this policy? Germany's trade has increased enormously and reached in 1902 8,300,000 tons of pig iron and 7,400,000 tons of steel, of which Great Britain, India, and the Colonies took a little over 1,000,000 tons. Mark this important fact, and I wish Mr. Cox to specially take, note of it, that Germany has built up this great indus- try within the last 25 years, since Prince Bismarck carried his Act for the proteetian of German industries, and British ironand steel manufacture during the same period has been stationary, if not on the down grade. Germany is going on from conquering to conquer, and is bound to do so more and more unless we change our policy. With a large home market protected, both Ger- many and the United States can use the profits of their Home markets, to capture our Free markets, the Colonial markets, and Neutral markets, and British manufacturers may struggle and adopt all known improvements, but it can only end one way under so serious a disadvantage. An investor with £lOO,OOO, if wise, before he spends his money upon any enterprise, will look which country will give him the greatest security and incentive for his enterprise, and with pre- sent conditions it will not take him long to decide, it will be better to go inside the German or United States protected market, because he knows his chances will be twofold against an investment in Great Britain. viz., If in Germany a . market without tariff of 56 millions plus 42 millions in Great Britain ; if in United States at market of 80 millions without tariff plus 42 millions in Great Britain. The manu- facturer in Great Britain's free market is always in danger of being ruined by an unfair competition, hence the lack of enterprise here, and the great growth in Germany and the United States within the last 25 years. Mr. Chamberlain has pointed out a way .that by making an arrangement with our Colonies we can not only secure our own market, but also secure a much larger Preferential market than either Germany or the United States. What possibilities this opens up for British trade ! A free and Preferential treatment in favour of British enterprise amongst four hundred millions of people under the British flag, with a soil and climatic conditions suitable to supply all we require. Who doubts an enormous expansion in trade and manufac- turers immediately Mr. Chamberlain carries his policy? British Capital and enterprise directed to develop and realize these immense resources within the Empire. We have plenty to do amongst our own people if we are only alive to our great responsibilities and adopt Ia scientific trade policy against the haphazard policy or no policy now in vogue. Let us be wise in time.— Yours &amp;e.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>JOS. ELLIS, Managing Director, Workington Iron Co. Ltd., 'Workington. November 17th, 1903. THE RECENT LICENSING PROSECUTION.</head>
        <p>JOS. ELLIS, Managing Director, Workington Iron Co. Ltd., 'Workington. November 17th, 1903. THE RECENT LICENSING PROSECU- TION.</p>
        <p>Rev. J. F. Matthews Criticises the Magistrates.</p>
        <p>In the course of the service at the Latchford Baptist Church on Sunday evening last Mr. Matthews said: During the coining winter, I intend, as occasion offers to call your attention, and the general public through you, to matters of local and public importance. On Monday last a prosecution having been brought by the police against the landlord of the Roebuck Inn, the case came on before a full bench of magistrates. The defendants' solicitor admitted, that literally and tech- nically the case against the landlord was proved. The Bench however after consultation dismisged the Case with costs only. •</p>
        <p>Now I venture to think no social reform in this town is content with a decision of this character, a decision quite in keeping of course, with the general attitutelind disposition usually taken by the Warrington magistrates. There were men on the Bench whom we justly esteem alike for their personal character, and public work and one hesitates to speak of them as callous in regard to public morality and hostile to the interests of temper- ance and sobriety. We know some of them are afraid to act against the liquor power, and when any case demanding strong action comes before them they act as cravens and cowards. I should not like to say this concerning the whole Bench. It does however seem strange, that, in the very face of the evidence, and when the liqour traffic is making such enormous, and unscrupulous claims, a decision like that given last Monday should be recorded. I wish the magistrates would make a tour of the town say any Saturday evening. They would see some of the drunken and brutal orgies only too common. They would hear language which for foulness and filthiuess is unsurpassed anywhere else—floating from the lips of people under the influence of drink. If they could but enter a few of the homes and note the pictures of desti- tution and the signs of degradation all springing from' the same source —strong drink—they would become a little more sensitive to the growing demand for the exercise of more stringent methods on their part when publicans are proved to have broken the law. Nor is it fair to the police. The actual , commendation of their behaviour in the present instance by Mr. Monks, the presiding magistrate, offers a singular commentary on the -weak and ineffectual attitude of the whole Bench. What encouragement have police in fulfilling these most disagreeable duties? What encouragement have they in seduring convictions, —ordinarily so difficult in the case of publicans— when their efforts are but coldly approved. Twice within less than 12 months the Chief Constable has acted with conspicuous nobility and judgment. We have not forgottqn his strong opposition to the Lovely- lane licence transfer. We shall not soon forget his action in the case of the Roebuck Inn, and T hope that temperance men of all shades of opinion will narrowly watch a bench not too slow, to rivet still further the fetters of the liquor traffic on the people of this town. My own conviction is that the time has come, when we ought to call into exist- ance a Town's Vigilance Committee, every one of whose members, should be men of tried worth unimpeachable integrity, and fearlessness. This c6mmittee should from time to time institute prosecutions against re calcitmnt landlords and demand the endorsement of the licenses of offending houses. This</p>
        <p>will only be done when a listless public is roused from its shameless letharay to swelling indigation at the criminal apathy of the magistrates and the law- breaking licensed victuallers. Worst of all however, the churches are to blame. So long as men having brewery shares are allowed to hold office in the Chris- tian Church so long as churches themselves are willing to receive handsome gratuities from brewers, so long as Christian ministers talk in a mealy-mouthed way about moderation and Timothy's wine-licence secured for his stomach's sake, so long as liberals,conservatives, and non-traders push publicans forward into municipal life, so long may we expect to see this plotting and thriving trade dominating all phases of civic and muni— cipal life. I hope and pray too, that all the curses born of this terrible traffic in strong drink, all the men and wreckage of human life brought about by it, may be broughthome in the most vivid and vigorous way to the consciences of all those, who now spare no pains to throttle all attempts to put down, the personal, social, and public evils of the liquor traffic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Alderman Bennett and Mr. Chamber-</head>
        <p>Alderman Bennett and Mr. Chamber-</p>
        <p>lain's Policy.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire Conservative Workingmen's Associations in Liverpool on Saturday Alderman Bennett (Warrington) moved— "That this meeting cordially supports Mr. Chamberlain in his policy of preferential trade, believing that it will be beneficial tl agriculture, will not materially enhance the price of fftd, will foster trade between the various portions of the Empire, and will gradually lead to true Imperial Union." He considered the first resolu- tion did not go far enough. Free Trade had been detrimental to agriculture. Since 1881 the amoun e'" of land under agriculture in this country had decreased by 2,000,000 acres. The capital value of agricultural land had decreased £800,000,000. Agriculture suffered by the " dumping" of foreign produce. The speaker proceeded at consider- able length to argue that a duty on imported wheat would not necessarily increase the price of bread, and even if it did, the taxes Mr. Chamberlain would take off other commodities would more than compensate it. The cry of dear food was simply a parrot cry. He described the statement as far-fetched and ridiculous that other countries would retaliate against us, because foreign countries were in the utmost dread of being kept out of our market. As so many of the foreign markets were closed against us, if we could get the whole of the colonial trade, and if we could retain the whole of the homes market, we should be compensated for this ex- clusion. With an Imperial union effected there might be some readjustment with regard to Imperial burdens. If we gave a preference to the colonies the colonies would grow in wealth and population and might well be asked to bear their proper proportion of the burden of Imperial defence. He thougkt Mr. Balfour was mis- taken in thinking that the country was mot ripe for Mr. Chamberlain's policy. Alderman Salvidge, seconded, and the resolution was carried, after considerable discussion by 28 votes to 6.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>WARRINGTON GOLF CLUB. Opening of the New Pavilion. VISIT OF MR. CROSFIELD AND "JOHNNIE" BALL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON GOLF CLUB. Opening of the New Pavilion. VISIT OF MR. CROSFIELD AND "JOHNNIE" BALL.</p>
        <p>The pleasantly-situated links of the Warrington Golf Club at Hill Warren were visited by a goodly number of enthusiasts in the sport on Thursday afternoon, when the new pavilion, which has been erected at a cost of £250, was opened by \Tr. A. H. Crosfield. Although the club has only been in existence about six months, it has a membership of 125 gentlemen and 50 ladies, and a further evidence of its success is that the sum required to build the pavilion has already been raised. The pavilion is designed on much the usual lines, and con- tains every facility for the comfort and convenience of the golfers. There are rooms for both the ladies and the gentlemen, and here tackle can be stored in lockers. In addition there is a well-appoi ntecl ref reshment-room,with the necessary kitchen at the rear. The opening ceremony was not the only source of attraction, as the greater portion of the afternoon was occupied by a golf-match in which Mr. John Ball, the ex-amateur champion and probably the finest amateur in England to-day, gave an exhibition of his skill with the clubs. His partner was Alderman Fairclough, and the opposing players were Mr. A. H. Crosfield and Dr. Peacock. A game of 18 holes was decided upon, and this meant that the course had to be traversed twice. The golfers had quite a large and enthusiastic followingonany of the members no doubt being bent on " picking up points." During the first round Alderman Fairclough showed indifferent form and at the close Mr. Crosfield and his partner were leading by two holes. In the second round Alderman Fairclough improved considerably, but Dr. Peacock showed a falling off. The result of the match was that Mr. Ball and Alderman Fairclough were victorious, bein,, 4 up and 2 to play. The members then assembled outside the pavilion, and Alderman Fairclough called upon Mr. Crosfield to open the club. Mr. Crosfield said he thought that after the dis- tinguished performance they had had from Mr. Ball and Alderman Fairclough. the opening ceremony ought to have been performed by one of those gentlemen,because his (Mr. Crosfteld's) partner and himself were what might be called" smashed, demolished, and pulverised" (Laughter). He hoped Dr. Peacock did not feel very much depressed (The doctor :NO, no). They had had a very pleasant round and he thought that was a most delightful place to have chosen as the site for the golf links. He remembered years ago when Richard Cobden was at Walton Lea he said he liked the situation for the reason that on one side they had the beauties of. nature, looking towards Cheshire, and on the other hand they had the signs of industry. They had the same attractions at the golf links, and when they were enjoying an afternoon-off and looked in thip direc- tion of Warrington, they would see the mills grinding laway, even if they were not looking after the shop. Warrington was noted for its divers industries, and he thought they might say their town was noted for the many branches of sport in which their townsmen had won distinction—in cricket, football, athletics, bowls, and, let them not forget, also in golf—(applause)— because last year a Warringtonia,n won the amateur championship of Great Britain. He dare say some of those present witnessed that performance, and he did not think they would forget it in a hurry. Mr. Ball, Mr. Jack Hutchings, and himself were the first to get hold of old Charles Hutchings and hoist him into the club at Hoylake. They might well be proud that a Warringtonian had won such great distinction, and ne trusted there would be golfers in that club xvho would be worthy to follow in Mr. Hutchings' foot- steps. He hoped the club would be a very great success. The links were a very fine test of golf, but they wanted knowing—(laughter)—and the situation was altogether exceptional. The club would be a very greatboonto Warringtonians, and he hoped to have the privilege of playing many a round at Hill Warren in future. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Crosfield then opened the door of the pavilion amid applause. Mr. E. .1. Hall proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Crosfield for opening the pavilion. Mr. Crosfield he said, had many titles to distinction both in politicsand as a mountaineer, but he (Mr. Hall) imagined that pro- bably the greatest sensational pleasure Mr. Crosfield had received was the first time he beat bogey. (Laugh- ter.) He did not know what the future had in store— whether some constituency would send Mr. Crosfield to Parliament ; or whether in days to come he might lead his Majesty's opposition, for he was afraid Mr. Crosfield belonged to a party that would not enjoy the fruits of office. (Laughter, and Mr. H. Roberts: Question.) He did not know whether or not Mr. Crosfield might have the proud distinction of climbing some mountain that had never been scaled before, but be thought the proud- est distinction he might aspire to would be to win the amateur golf championship of England. He hoped he might achieve that success. (Applause.) Mr. C. D. Parkinson (secretary) seconded the vote. which was heartily carried. Mr. H. Roberts proposed a vote of thanks to Mr Ball for coming down and givinethem an exhibition of what golf was. He thought they had all picked up a few points and they would he the better for the treat they had had and for the exercise they had received in marching round those beautiful links. (Applause.) Alderman Fairclough seconded the motion which was cordially passed. Mr. Crosfield said they had been watching perhaps the finest golfer in the world—a very old friend of his, and the most distinguished amateur who ever held a club. There was one faculty that Mr. Ball had not vet developed as they hoped he would do some day. He did not love speaking. (Laughter.) It was a peculiar thing that there should be that little deficiency on their friend's part, because whatever golf did not do for them, he thought most of those who played the game would agree that it was calculated to extend one's vocabulary. (Laughter.) Although Mr. Ball had played all these years he' was-still a little backward in that re- spect, and so he (Mr. Crosfield) thought he wouldven- ture to respond for him and at the same time to express his own thanks to Mr. Ball for coming down to play for them. They were very proud to see him there (Applause.) This concluded the opening ceremony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Death of Mr. Joseph Fisher.</head>
        <p>Death of Mr. Joseph Fisher.</p>
        <p>Another of Warrington's well-known and respected inhabitants has passed away in the person of Mr. Joseph Fisher, of King•street. Deceased for about 40 years carried on business as metal and. furniture broker, and his business career brought him into con- tact with many of the leading manufacturers of the town by whom he was greatly respected. Deceased was oft very kind-hearted disposition and had been the means of helping those who have been less fortu- nate to tide over their difficulties. He had been suffer- ing for a long time from bronchitis and had been con- fined to his house for about twelve. months. He waw 77 years of age. '</p>
      </div>
