I Mrs. Falshaw wa's counting money in the office when the prisoner, who was an apprentice in the employ of the prosecutor, came in with his dinner basket and can. Mrs. Falshaw. made the money ready for the bank and found it amounted to 1:17 10s. Subsequently she had occasion to leave the office and left the prisoner there cooking his dinner. Afterwards she went to the National Provincial Bank with the cash and found it was 30s. short. Prisoner was apprehended at Stockton Heath by Detective Foster and handed over two half sovereigns and two five-shilling pieces.—Mr. Crompton appealed to the , Bench to deal with the prisoner under the First Offenders' Act, and Mr. Falshaw said he was willing to take him back into his employ.—Prisoner was accordingly bound over to come up for judgment if called upon within the next six months, and ordered to pay the costs. UNHAPPY HOME LlFE.—Patrick Burke, 23, School Brow, was summoned for persistent cruelty to his wife, Mary J ine Burke.—Mr. Crompton appeared for the complainant and said defendant's assaults had been per- sistent from the time the parties were married nine years ago. Separation orders had been granted but they had come together again.—Complainant stated that on one occasion defendant cut her head and she had to have it stitched.—The Chief Constable said prisoner made his 49th appearance.—A separation order was granted with 15s. per week alimony.</p>
