      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A DEFENCE THAT FAILED.-FINE FOR STREET BETTING</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON. William Green, jun.. Wilderspool-road. was charged at the Warrington Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor and other magistrates, with frequenting Winwick- street on November sth, Gth, and 7th, for the purpose of betting. Mr. Rigby Swift, barrister (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) appeared for the defendant who pleaded not guilty.—P.C. Clough stated that defendant received' slips and what witness surmised was money on each day. He was certain that. defendant was paid a silver ' coin on one occasion.—P.C. Stewart corroborated.— P.C. Clough (recalled) said he saw the defendant after- wards and told him he had reported him for betting.— Defendant said he might as well go and ask what his fine was. There were men, he added, making £4O a day by betting and he could only make £l. He said the officer must have been mistaken in supposing that he was betting on the dates named.—Mr. Swift, for the defence, said he thought the case served to illustrate the old proverb "Give a dog a bad name and hang him." When once the magistrates had convicted a man of bookmaking it seemed as if it were impossible for him to walk about the streets without running the risk of having a charge of betting made against him. Defend- ant had been a bookmaker and had paid the penalty. Subsequently he got into difficulties owing to his book- making and had cleared out of the the town owing something like £4O. At the beginning of October his brother offered to find him £4O to enable him to payhis debts and obtain some other employment. The money waslfound on defendant promising not to bet again, and all he had done since had been to pay off his debts. He had had to pay some people in Winwick-street and he (Mr. Swift) suggested that he must have been doing this when the officers saw him and jumped at the conclusion that he was betting. On one of the dates a wedding was taking place from the house of one of defendant's relatives,and defendant was standing outside the door. There was, \therefore, a perfectly good reason why people should come and speak to him—Defendant gave evidence bearing out this statement. In cross-examination by the Chief Constable defendant admitted "welshing "but Mr. Rigby Swift objected to such questions as to character being asked—Thomas Green, defendant's brother, who said he wasa betting man, spoke to giving -defendant £4O to enable him to pay off his 'debts. As far as he knew defendant had not made a book since.— The magistrates considered the case was fully proved and imposed a fine of £5 and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>AS WELL AS EVER.</p>
        <p>IMr. Frank Tombs, of Gwerna Vawr, Maes-y-cwmmer, via Cardiff, Monmouthshire, had Rheumatic Fever five ! times in six months, and tried every remedy he could think of, but all failed to do himany good. He grad- ually grew worse and worse, and at last he got so bad that he had to stop working and was doubled up with pain. He says : " I read of a case of a shoemaker, who was cured of ! Rhematism by Dodd's Kidney Pills, and I thought I would give them a trial. I sent for a box and when it I was finished I certainly felt better. This improvement encouraged me to take a course of Dodd's Kidney Pills, and I am pleased to say that they have cured me cosppletely. I'The Rheumatism has left me and I am back at my work again, as well as ever I was in my life. lam very thankful for what Dodd's Kidney Pills have done : for me." The remedy which cured Mr. Tombs is said to be a sure cure for all forms of Rheumatism, Backache and ; Kidney Trouble. From the number of wonderful cures i which are being published every day, it would seem as if this were absolutely true.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ATTEMPTED. SUICIDE AT WIDNES. George Walker, an elderly man, living in Alforde- street, Widnes, was on Monday brought up on a charge of attempting suicide on Saturday afternoon. Defend- ant had been suffering from bronchitis for some time, as well as an affection of the leg. A bottle of ammonia was sent b) the doctor to rub his leg with. After going for hiv. wages on Saturday he went to bed, and sent his niece for some beer. &amp;fore drinking this he drank half the bottle of ammonia. When spoken to by his wife he said his life was a miserable existence. When . charged defendant said" It's right." On promising not 1 to do anything of a like kind again he was discharged. ABSCONDING FROM THE WORKHOUSE. , At Widnes Sessions, on Thursday morning Kate Tarleton, a young married woman well known in Wid- nes, was charged with being druid( on August 28 and on the 9th inst. also with absconding from the workhouse with 30s. worth of cloths. Police constable Grayson and Parr proved the cases of drunkenness. Defendant MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen pleaded guilty to all charges. The bench inflicted fines Sutton, Francomb,Hutchinson,Heaton,and Smethurst, of lOs and costs or 'fourteen days for each " drunk "and Councillor Thornton, Messrs. Birchall, Wright, Hay- , ordered her to prison for fourteea days for absconding ward, Hepherd, and R. T. Fairclough. j vith the apparel. THE TOY PISTOL DANGER.—John Skelton, iron- 1 ' PETTY THEFT AT WIDNES. monger, Stc., 55, Sankey-street, was summoned for hay- I Frank Price, aged thirteen years, living in Henry- Ing on October 30th sold a pistol to a boy named street, was on Thursday sent to an industrial school Albert Wakefield, under eighteen years of age, contrary until sixteen years of age for stealing 6s. in money and to the Pistols' Act, 1903.—The Chief Constable said two pawntickets. the property of Messrs. Burgess, the prosecution arose out of a case in which the boy i Fisher-street, Widnes. Complainant stated that on the Wakefield was fined, for using a pistol and shooting 7th inst she gave the boy the money to redeem two another lady. It transpired that defendant sold the , bundles from a local pawnshop, and instead of so doing pistol to Wakefield on october3otit and he (Mr. Talbot) ' went to Warrington and spent the money. The boy had no alternative but to lay the information. Mr. had lately got into bad company, and was beyond the Skelton,who was a most respectable tradesman, pleaded control of his mother. Mr. Danby, director of educa- ignorance of the law, which only came into force on tion, asked the bench to withdraw the charge and grant August 1 lth'. Ignorance 'was no excuse but at times it an order so as to enable them to get him into a home. might be a reasonable palliation,and in this case he sub- This course was adopted.' mitted it was a palliation amounting almost to a I complete excuse. —Mr. Arthur Browne remarked that his learned friend Mr. Rigby Swift told him that he did not know of the existence of the Act. Neither did he (Laughter.) Mr. Rigby Swift: I did not think my friend would make our ignorance public. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>The case was dismissed. - ONE OF CIVILISATION'S FAILURES.—JaIia,Ann Shields was charged with disorderly conduct in the Workhouse. Mr. Aubery, workhouse master said prisoner refused to perform a task and used filthy language.—The Chief Constable said the prisoner had been fifty-seven times before the bench for all sorts of offences. She was one of civilisation's failures. She had been brought ip under wretched surroundings, with criminal associations in her girlhood, and her after life had been spent alter-</p>
        <p>Noo</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, November 21, 1903</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, November 21, 1903</p>
        <p>large Via had be3n given up and he should be sorry if -others followed suit.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOLLEY said it might appear to require a certain amount of impudence or moral courage to _reply to a criticism so severe as that which had been launched at the commitee by Mr. Upson. The adjective monstrous</p>
        <p>.. ~ . and the words penalize the children and cheese-paring economy were very effective debating phrases; but they did not seem to very materially touch the real point at issue. m4+ich was one of principle. He knew how _ _ . . . .</p>
        <p>eattest Mr. Upson was Fn the cause of education, but they must not let ithemselves be carriqd away by considerations of that kind. He did not want to retaliate by a sarcastic speech, but he suggested that if the head teachers were full of enthusiasm to encourage the children to take an interest in work outside the ordinary school hours and their enthusiasm burnt with such a flickering flame that it would not sacrifice 9d a week they wanted bellows or something to !bake the flame burn up a little. (Laughter.) If the enthusiasm of the teachers was measured by 9d or 2s. Od. a week and they would not sacrifice it-</p>
        <p>Mr. UPSON : Why should they? Mr. Jou.nv : Then why should this committee sacri- fice a principle and open the door to what I feel would frequently lead to acrimonious discussion in relation to matters connected with various denominations? If they relaxed for one denomination, he continued, they would be asked to do it for others' and it would, there- fore, be better to put down a firm foot at the outset and prevent any possible question arising in future. He hoped the members of the committee would be very careful indeed before they referred the matter back as it had not been pissed Without careful consideration.</p>
        <p>Mr. UPSON said it was not the loss of 9d. a week but of 2s. 9d. In one school the head teacher was a married man with a family, and he had a'salary of £ll5 a year, and he was asked not only to give his labour for two hours a week, but 2s. 9d. out of his pocket. If that was not monstrousfie did not know what was.He(Mr.Upson) had not the ability to give violin lessons. He had to employ a special teacher and pay ss. a week for the two lessons. He was to lose that and 2s. for the hire of the room. He did not know what the committee expe3ted the teathers to do, but it seemed that' they were ex- pected to pay is. a week for the extra tuition of their scholars. Some schools were already preparing for a concert at Christmas, and the practices had to be held after school hours. Would the committee charge the schools for every practice they had after school time? (A Voice: "Certainly.") - The CHAIRMAN suggested that no harm would be done by referring the :natter back for re-consideration. This course was agreed to. _ _ _</p>
        <p>. The Rev. JOHN YONGE said he was sorry to be found on the bench of the malcontents. He expressed his regret, and that of his people at Wycliffeat the decision with regard to the schoolkeeper. It was desirable there should be uniformity in the matter throughout the town, but he ventured to think that they were even yet in a transition stage in regard to their educational arrangements. Here was a case where a man had been caretaker of a church and a school immediately adjoin- ing the church, and to which there was access from the church, and now he was told that if he wished to be the keeper of the school he must relinquish the keeping of the church, which meant he was to lose about £3O of the £l.lO of his income, bringing it down to £BO. That seemed to him to be a hardship and very unfair to a man who had held the position for nine years. Would it not be possible for the committee to arrange that the rule laid down should apply to new appointments and not to the appointments of persons who had already held the positions for a considerable number of years? He moved that the matter be referred back to the committee for further consideration. • Councillor ROBERTS seconded, because, he said, a similar question also affected the church and schools of which he was one of the managers and church- wardens. In his case they had had a caretaker for • upwards of 25 years, and she had been caretaker of the church ever since it was consecrated. It would be a great hardship if a. person had to give up one of those . positions. Councillor ELLISON thought the committee might ascertain how far the system was carried out through- out the town. He was afraid it would be found that some people were already doing double work. It would, thereforejbe well to refer the matter back. Mr. GEORGE WALKER supported the amendment and said that under the new rules three caretakers would suffer a reduction of salary. If a caretaker performed his duties satisfactorily he did not see why an arrange- ment could not be come to by which he could hold the two appointments. Alderman BENNETT said there was a good deal to be said for Mr. Yonge's argument. In this country we always liked to protect any vested' interests, so long HS we could do so without prejudice to anyone concerned It was somewhat hard that a man should be penalized by the adoption of new arrangements if he could per- form dual work satisfactorily. Mr. Jolley might accept the amendment and try to work the matter on the lines which had been indicated, but in the new appoint- ments they could adhere to what had been already .de- cided.</p>
        <p>.Mr. JOLLEY said the question had caused a great deal</p>
        <p>of trouble to the committee, who had quiZe loyally endeavoured to carry out the principles the full educa- tion committee laid down, and which, he understood, were to be adhered t 6. Behind all this was the prim- ciple that they ought not in any way, directly or indirectly, to anything that would look like subsidising any denomination out of the rates. The amendment would open the door to that kind of thing, and if the committee in the full exercise of its responsibility Ilecided to go back upon what they had already agreed upon, the Finance Committee must carry out those in- structions bat they would not be voluntarily carried out by him at any rate. This matter applied in other cases and he could mention three churches where the same argument had been urged. They had adhered in every instance to the principle agreed upon, that it was no part of their duty to mix up church accounts with school accounts.</p>
        <p>Counciller DODDS was entirely with Mr. Jolley al- though he thought the matter should be referred back to the committee.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMANasked if Mr. Yonge could not approach the committee with a view to some modification of the arrangement? The REV. JoHN YONGE replied that he did not see the point of Mr. Jolley's difficulty. He did not see how by allowing his caretaker to continue .the caretaking of the church it would involve the Committee in silbsidi- sing a denomination because they did not ask the com- mittee to pay the caretaker's wages. The committee only paid his wages for the caretaking of the school. He hoped Mr. Jolley would not be offended if he said that this was a case of drawing a red herring across the scent to divert their attention from the particular point The Rev. Father WILSON quite agreed with the prin- ciple that the committee, should do nothing that, savoured of subsidising a denomination. He did not think this was a case, on all fours with the unfortunate music classes. Alderman PIERPOINT said that Mr. Yonge had his ' sympathy, and he hiSped the committee would be able to devise some means out of the difficulty. Mr. UPSON said that this time he was on the side of the gods. (Laughter.) The CHAIRMAN: Who are the gods? (Renewed laughter.) Mr. UPsoN remarked that the caretaking of Wycliffe scho3l would provide sufficient work for one man. According to the duties laid down by that authority there was sufficient work to keep the man employed the whole of the Utile, and if they made him the servant of the committee and the servant of a body of trustees who was to be the master. They were told of old that "no man could serve two masters," and by the amend- ment they were going to try to make people obey two masters. The committee would be ill-advised to alter the arrangements, simply on the question of etfictency, The work was being better done now than ever it had been done in the past. They were paying living wages to their caretakers. and they got an adequate return. If they were made the servants of the various churches also they Would be doing the work of cleaning the church when they ought, to be doing the work of the authority. . Councillor Donns : That is thepoint. The CHAIRMAN asked if Mr. Upson insisted upon a man resigning a position when he could do his work by help that he could employ. Mr. UPSON: If a man has a •sife why not appoint 'her caretaker of the Church. IThe Rev. JOHN YONGE said they would be very glad of such an arrangement, and were quite willing to agree to it on the understanding that such duties as opening the church on Sunday attending to the heating and lightin., were considered within the province of the caretaker, while the wife and daughter had the care of the church. They wanted to avoid making new appoint- ment.; because they would have to engage another man. ; Mr. JOLLEY accepted the amendment in order that Mr. Yonge might approach the committee with a view to obtaining some modification of the arrangements. SCHOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE. THE RETIREMENT OF MR. F. W. MONKS. CLASSES FOR UNQUALIFIED TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee placed on record its high appreciation Aof the services rendered by Mr. F. W. Monks in the catis,e of, education in Warrington. its great regret on his .retirement from the Education Com- mittee, and consequent relinquishment of the chairman- ship of the School Management Committee. It was re- solved that the Rev. Morley Stevenson be elected as chairman of the committee for the ensuing year. The following circular letter from the Board of Education was submitted, viz Board of Education, South Kensington, London, S.W. 22nd October, 1903. • Sir,—ln view of certain applications which have been made to the Board of Education by Local Education Authorities,the Board think it well to state that they apprehend that some considerable time must In most eases elapse before the Local Education Authorities are in a position to frame cogyrehensive schemes for supplying or aßing the supply of Educa- tion other than elementary, under part II of the Education Act, 1902. Some local Education Authorities have addressed to the Board preliminary letters in which they inform the Board that they are initiating an inquiry into the existing educational_ facilities in their area, and they ask that pending the completion of this inquiry and the consideration of the measures which it may be desirable to take when the facts have been sufficiently ascertained, the Board will approve the continuance of work in regard to higher education on existing lines, with such minor changes, or extensions as are</p>
        <p>steps of obvious advantage under the new conditions of organisation. The Board of Education are of opinion that the course adopted in the cases referred to is one which will oe found convenient as a temporary measure by a considerable number of local Education Authorities, and any proposals of this tenour which maybe put _</p>
        <p>before vthi3m will be favourably considered as- an at interim compliance with the requirements of Section 2 of the Act. I have the honour to be, sir, your obedient servant, ROBERT L. MORANT.</p>
        <p>The committee resolved that all uncertificated teach- ers in schools under the committee be required to attend the classes held for acting teachers with a view to their becoming qualified, and that the necessary text books be provided and no fees charged to such teachers; that the letter be acknowledged, and be recorded in the</p>
        <p>minutes, also that the Chairnian'and Director do confer upon the matter, and endeavour to report to this com- mittee upon the general question of secondary education in Warrington. The following resolution passed by the managers of the Latchford National school, viz :—" That the Education Committee be asked to grant facilities to the Vicar of the parish of Christ Church, Latchford, or someone appointed by him to give teaching to the children of those parents who desire it in the new school to be provided in this district on two of the Wye mornings of the week in the time set apart for religious instruction and in consideration of this concession the managers are prepared to allow any uccredited representative of Other denominations to give teligious teaching in this (the National) school on two of the five .mornings of every week during the time allotted for religious instruction," was con- sidered,and it was resolved, that no action be taken</p>
        <p>in the matter. The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON in moAing the con- firmation of the minutes, said he had the opportunity last month of speaking of the loss which the committee sustained by the resignation of Mr. Alderman Monks,as he then wag. Therefore he did not think he need say anything further with regard to the first resolution which placed OD record their high appreciation of his services on the School Management Committee. He would like to ask the members' careful attention to the circular letter from the Board of Education. The committee would see that although the resolution was simply to the effect that it be acknowledged and the Chairman and director asked to report upon it, it was a very important letter. In the first place it showed that the Board of Education had no wish at all to force upon the town any hasty measures with regard to future educational policy,and it recognised the fact that it might be some little time before the committee, with all the work which was before it, was able to carry out all that was necessary for the educational welfare of the Borough At the same time it very clearly intimated that it was the wish of the Board of Education that although the local authority was not to be forced to act it was to be asked to consider carefully what plans were to be adopted for the promotion of secondary education in the future. In a word they were asked to consult and to plan but not necessarily to carry out immedi- ately. He thought they would all be glad of that. They would all feel that the provision of secondary and higher education in the town was a most important matter and one which required very careful and very full consideration. They had to lay the foundation upon which those who came after, would build up the educational building which would be the most important thing for the welfare of the town that could be raised, (Hear, hear.) That was not to be done lightly or hastily and they would be glad, he felt sure, that their hands were not. forced but that they were given time for careful consideration. The School Management Committee felt very strongly that that consideration should take place as soon as possible and that the matter must not be delayed because the amount of concentration and consideration which would be necessary would take a very long time. They would have to consult many interests; they would have to confer with experts; and they would have to consider very carefully before they sub- mitted their plan to the Board of Education. There- fore he hoped every member of the committee would turn the matter over in his mind and think out what should be done in the future. With regard to the classes for acting teachmes, the committee would be aware that there were in the town a certain number of teachers in the elementary schools who had not gained their Government certificate. That was a state of things which they should endeavour gradually to do away with. It was not wise, it was not rigitt,that they should entrust the teaching of the children to those who were not properly qualified for the office. He did not say this in disparagement of these teachers—not for a moment—because he knew very well indeed from the office be held that in almost every case, the failure to obtain the certificate was generally more due to want of means to promote their interests than to want of will. Whilst the committee wished to deal quite fairly and quite justly with all these uncertificated teachers they felt it was their bounden duty to endeav- our to provide such instruction as might enable them to gain their Government certificates. By doing so they hoped not only to promote efficiency but to confer a great benefit upon these acting teachers who would become duly qualified and able to carry out their im- portant work. With regard to the financial question it would be obvious to all the members of the committee that an unqualified, uncertificated teacher could only comfnand a very small salary and it would hardly be fair or just to ask them to pay fees and to buy expen- sive books which would be necessary to help them through their course. The committee had therefore decided to bear the expense, but the teachers who attended would be required to attend regularly and do their work properly. Alluding to the resolution res- pecting the request of the managers of Latchford National school, the Rev. Morley Stevenson said it was legally impossible to comply with therequest. A clause in the Education Act of 1870 made it distinctly clear that no denominational teaching could be provided in a public school provided by the ratepayers, and on the other hand the trust deed relating to Christ Church</p>
        <p>schools laid it down clearly that, o other teaching except that given by the Church of England could be given in those schools. Therefore it was not plssible to invite a Nonconformist teacher into those schools.T.t was a state of things,which he hoped</p>
        <p>- • would not last for ever. (Hear, hear.)TheSchool Manage- ment Couneil felt they had no right to step outside the bounds of the Education Act and decided to take no action in the matter. They came to that decision with very great regret. (Hear, hear.) The Rev. Father IViLsoN seconded the adoption of the minutes.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGH thought a minimum number of attendances ought to be stated for the classes for acting teachers, or the committee might not get a proper return for the money expended. Tae CIL% IRNI 01s tid a record of attendances would be kept. Mr. R. T. F kIRCIMUGEI : A minimum number of attendances ought to be stated. If the teachers only attended one half the time and did not pass our money would be wasted.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : Unless there is good cause, I should think they would attend every one. Mr. UPsoN thought it would .meet Mr. Fair- dough's suggestion if each teacher entered into a similar agreement as that entered into by parents of scholars at the Secondary school. The CIIAIRMAN : That will have to be considered.</p>
        <p>Alderman PIERPOINT took it that unless these ladles attended the classes and passed the examinations they • would be discharged. _ The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON thought the words "be 'required to attend" conveyed all that the members wished. There was no choice in the matter for the acting teachers. Mr. PLINSTON as'ied if there was any specified time allowed to qualify in. ; The CHAIRMAN: It will go according to the Code. ' The DIRECTOR observed that in one class two years was allowed and for the other five years. The CHAIRMAN asked the Rev. E. J. Willcocks if he desired to say anything with respect to the communica- ! tion from the Board of Education. ' The Rev. E. J. WELIC,IOES thought he had better leave the subject alone until after next Monday. I The CHAIRNI AN added that there w likely to be a' " - solution Of the difficulty with regard to higher educa- tion in the town, which would be of advantage to the townand satisfactory to the committee. (Hear, hear. i Mr. A. .1. Jota-Aiv said there had be3n a too wide- spread feeling that what the Education Committee had 'to deal with was elementary education. The Act was perfectly clear that it was equally the duty of the local authority to deal with all forms of education. Without wishing to indicate what might be the result of the con- ference on the Board of Education's letter, he would suggest, in view of the charges. of secrecy which had been made about various sub-committees, that if any. member had any ideas in his mind in regard to higher education it would be well to carefully meditate upon them and- put them in a definite form so that all possible points of view might be in the possession of the Chairman and the Director of Education. It was a matter of great importance. They were going to plan out future lines of action,which,if once started upon,would be difficultto turn away from, if found unsatisfactory to local needs It was very desirable that every point should be well in the minds of those who would have to deal with it. The CHAIRMAN: There is no question of secrecy. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY : I know that, bat the charge has been made. Alderman BENNETT did not know whether the com- mittee would be exceeding its duty if a resolution were passed suggesting that the law might be amended to allow religions instruction to be given on the lines suggested by the Managers of Christ Church schools. Couneillor Dorins: "Let sleeping dogs lie." (Hear, bear.) The minutes were then passed. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE AND ACCOMMODATION. MORE ATTENDANCE INSPECTORS REQUIRED. The School Attendance Committee resolved at its last meeting: "That the resignation of Mr. H. T. Kane as assistant school attendance inspector, be accepted as from the 30th November, 1903, and that the committee, whilst regretting the loss of his services, congratulate him on his new appointment." It was also decided that an advertisement should be issued in- viting applications for the position of School Attend- ance Inspector thus rendered vacant, at a commencing salary of £6.5, rising to a maximum of £9l per annum. Coancillor Emasom moved the adoption of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>THE QUALIFICATIONS OF TEACHERS. School Accommodation Question.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Education Committee was held in the Counkll Chamber at the Town Hall, on Monday evening. Mr. F. Monks presided and there were also present: Rev. Morley Stevenson, Rev. E. J. Willcocks,- Rev John Yonge, Rev. Father Wilson, the Mayor ,(Alderman Bolton), Aldermen Pierpoint and Smethurst, Councillors Ellison, James Evans, Dodds, Brough, Davies and John Roberts, Messrs, A. J. Jolley, J. Bibby, G. Walker, J. Upson, E. J. Guest, J. Fulton Smith, R. T. Faircloughand J. Moore Murray (director .of education).</p>
        <p>CHANGES ON THE COMMITTEE. letter was read from the Town Clerk stating that Alderman Sutton and Councillor Maginn had been elected members of the committee in place of Mr. F. NV. Monks and Mr. NV. Roberts. Mr. W. Madden had tendered his resignation as a co-optative member, and the association which nominated him had been asked to submit a recommendation for the filling of the vacancy.</p>
        <p>The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON moved that Alderman Pierpoint and Mr. Fulton Smith be appointed members of the School Management Committee.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. YO.-GE seconded and the motion was agreed to. Alderman PIERPOINT moved that Councillor Maginn be placed on the School Attendance Committee in succession to Mr. W. Roberts. Coancillor DODDS seconded.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNBTT pointed out that Alderman Sutton who was the father of the Coundil, was not a member of a single committee. Alderman PIERPoiNT : That is a position which sometimes occurs in the Warrington Town Council. (Laughter.) Alderman I3ENNETT : It is not a desirable position.</p>
        <p>Alderman PIERPOINT : Itis not desirable and I have often spoken against it. The proposition was carried.</p>
        <p>THE PROVISION OF TRAINING COLLEGES. The DIRECTOR read a letter from the Clerk to the Lancashire County Council stating that a conference of representatives of education committees of Lanca- shire and Cheshire and the county boroughs of the two counties would be held at the Town Hall, Manchester, on November 23rd, to discuss generally the provision of training colleges for teachers. The Committee was asked to appoint two representatives. . .</p>
        <p>Councilco-r DODDS moved that the Chairman and Mr. A...1...J011ey should attend the conference.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT seconded. Alderman PIERPOINT said it was desirable that Mr. Murray should attend.</p>
        <p>Mr. MURRAY pointed out that only two representa- tives were invited. The CHAIRMAN witkdrew his name in favour of Mr. Murray, and it was unanimously decided that Mr. Jolley and Mr. Murray should attend the conference.</p>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE'S BUSINESS. CHARGE FOR THE USE OF SCHOOLROOMS. THE VIOLIN CLASSES. THE DUTIES OF SCHOOL-KEEPERS.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee considered applications for the use of rooms at the Fairfield, St. Barnabas', and St. Mary's (R.C.) schools after school hours for violin classes, and it was resolved, that the managers be in- formed that, under the existing arrangements, the com- mittee regard this use of the rooms as being for purposes other than that of an elementary school, and will, therefore, come under clause 5 of the scheme governing the cleaning, Sm., of schools. • The following.appointments of school keepers were sanctioned. Warrington British, Mr. William John Spriggs, £BO 46.; Latchford St. James', , Mr. Frederick Burrows, £7l 195.• St. Peter's, Mrs. Hayes. £52; Sankey Bridges Wesleyan, Miss Elizabeth Gough, £52; Trinity N. (Mixed), Mr. Thomas Baskerville, £52; Trinity N. (Infants'), 'Mrs. Clara Shaw, £52 per annum. The managers of the ‘Vycliffe B. school having re- newed their request that the schoolkeeper may be al- lowed to retain his office as keeper of the Church,it was resolvedfthat the committee cannot accede to the re- quest. The tender of the Warrinaton Corporation Gas department for the supply of coke to the various schools during the period ending the 31st March f9o4,and after- wards renewable for a further period of, twelve months fromlthat date,at a cost of 14s per ton wasaccepted,as was also the tender.of Messrs T.and H.Beddard,for thesupply of firewood and sawdust to the various schools, durfng the period ending the 31st March,l9o4,andlafterwards re- newable for a further period of twelve months from that date, at a cost falls and 5d per bag, respectively. The tender of Mr. S.Fernley,for the winding and care , of the clocks in the various schools, at a cost of ss-per , annum, for each clock, was accepted.—The Chairman having reported that the following resolutions had been passed by the Special Joint Sub-committee appointed to inquire into the office accommodation, viz.:—(1) That, in consequence of the rack of accommodation in the Technical Institute, it is necessary that the Educa- tion Office, including the School Attendance depart- • ment, should be transferred to some more convenient plane; (2) that the Education 0 {ice be transferred from the Technical school to the old Vicarage Sankey- street ; and (3) that those premises be altered in accordance with, the plans prepared by the Borough Surveyor, and the work carried out at a dest not ex- ceeding £2OO, it was resolved that the resolutions of the Special Joint Sub-committee be approved and adopted. It was decided that a youth be. appointed to assist the stationery and stores clerk at a weekly wage not exceeding twelve shillings. On the recommendation of the School Management Committee, it was resolved that, in view of the additional cleaning required consequent upon the open- ing of the Secondary school, the wages of the two pre- sent cleaners be increased from 7s. 6d. to 10s. per week, and that two additional cleaners be engaged at a weekly wage of 7s. 6d. each. In moving the adoption of the minutes Mr. Jolley referred to the violin class at St. Mary's school. After making further inquiries, he said, it had been found . . .</p>
        <p>that tire children atlending it had to pay a small fee for the purpose, it was stated, of providing the music. The Finance Committee felt very strongly that when any fee whatever was charged, something should be' paid for the use of the rooms. Unless that policy was strictly adhered to, they might easily be, opening the door to possible abuses. Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGH seconded.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Father Wltsos regretted the decision of the committee with reference to the music classes, which resulted in the case of St. Mary's School in the class being given up. He did not know how they ex- pected a teacher to give an extra two hours a week for nothing. It was no advantage to him, and it was no advantage to the managers. The children paid 3d. a week each and there were eleven pupils, making the total receipts 2s. 9d. 1-16 did not see how they could expect a teacher to.be more zealous in the cause of education than the committee itself was. He -quite understood the grounds on which the decision of the committee had been come to, but he much regretted it in the cause of education. Mr. UPSON moved an amendment to the effect that the committee be directed to grant the free use of the rooms to head teachers for educational purposes after school how s. In the school to which Father Wilson had referred the teacher was of some local standing as a musiciao, and he was able to give instruction to the scholars. He was the only teacher who could do so: Other schools had to employ special teachers who had to be paid out of the fees from the children. As Father Wilson said, the fee was 3d. per week, for which the children received two lessons of an hour each. The teacher received 2s. 9d. : he gave skilled instruction and bought the music. He did not do it for what he got out of it—(laughtee)—seeing that he had to pay 2s. for the rooms. That authority ought to encourage teachers in every possible way to take an interest in , their children outside the ordinary school curriculum. Some teachers were intereste I in swimming, others'had ' musical gifts and liked to instruct theirchildren beyond ! the ordinary requirements of the school and the educa- tion code. Those classes had been approved by H. M's inspector; when he visited his (the speaker's) school. An outside body could hire a school, and have the full use of the building, burn gas through 90 burners for two hours at a cost of is and yet the authority wanted to charge the same fee for a small class that could be put ' into one of the class rooms where they would simply burn gas through 'two burners for only three quarters of an hour. The thing was monstrous. They penalized the children and teachers and granted undue facilities to those not connected with the schools at all. This was one of the cases in which a hard and fast rule was drawn, which rendered the matter really ridiculous. It was one of those , cheese-paring economies where they got the minimum of economy and the maximum of irritation. No one did the work for what he could get out of it. A music teacher in two of the schools took what the classes made, but in every case the head teacher himself was responsible for the financing of the class. Personally, he drew is. 9d. from a class last week, and he paid the teacher 2s. 6d. (Laughter.) If he undertook any more financial speculations of that sort he would soon be able to retire from the teaching, profession to the workhouse. (Laughter.) The Finance Committee would be well advised to grant facilities to the schools that children might receive instruction that they would not otherwise get. At present they re- ceived the tuition cheaply, but if the classes were dis- continued they would have to .p-ty prices which were absolutely prohibitive for their lessons. The CHAIRMAN suggested that the amendment he altered to read " that the matter be referred back for re-consideration." Mr. UPSON agreed. Father WILSON seconded, and Said he bad intended to propose an amendment himself, although he preferred someone else doing so. He appealed to the Committee to use discretion in matters of this kind. Alderman SmETturnsTsupported, and appealed to the committee to make a concession to the banks connected with the day schools and to the bands of hope. One</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <p>Perfect Oats.</p>
        <p>Perfect Porridge.</p>
        <p>Formerly Oats were simpv ground down, and the result was called Oatmeal. But in the production of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>' Provost ' Oats</head>
        <p>' Provost ' Oats</p>
        <p>a very different system is adopted. Only the finest quality Scotch grain is used, which is put through special patented machinery, the latest improve- ment being to pass the Oats over strong magnets which draw out all impurities, leaving for con- sumption the perfect grain, and that alone. Thus scientifically treated, and partly cooked, Provost ' Oats make the most nutritive and most digestible breakfast and supper food 01,- tainable.</p>
        <p>R. ROBINSON &amp; SONS, ANNAN, N. B.</p>
        <p>Try our "Blue Bell" Flaked Rice, it makes dainty puddings in five minutes. Simply drench the flakes in boiling water and cook in an oven for five minutes.</p>
        <p>3d. one pound packet.</p>
        <p>minutes and wished every success to Mr. Kane.</p>
        <p>Mr. P L INSTON secondea.</p>
        <p>Rev. Father WILSON contended that when the new officer had been appointed the school attendance officers' staff was still undermanned. • He understood that there wore four visiting officers and if so that gave them 3,500 children each to look after. That was far too many for any one officer. If there were five visiting officers the staff would not be overmanned as they would then have 2.800 children under their supervision, and that was considered rather more than one officer could manage. He had had sufficient experience to say that the attendance at the schools was suffering, and he did not think the epidemic of sickness accounted by any means for the whole of the absenteeism. There was one class of children very much neglected and that was the newcomers. Children in their fifth year and young persons who came to the town had not their names on the books and consequently they were not visited. He quite con- ceived that with the present staff it was almost impos- sible for the officers to devote much time to that particular branch of work: He therefore • moved a resolution calling the attention of the committee to the matter. It was false economy to under-stiff the attendance office, because the laxity of attendance very soon ran away with a considerably greater income than it would take to pay an extra officer. Mr. I3PsoN said he had intended moving a similar resolution to that of Father Wilson. He found that Ithey had 500 children more on the books this month than in the corresponding month of last year, and the attendance was actually 300 lower. Of course, sickness did account for some of it, but it was a very serious state of things. In his school the ratepayers were losing £lO a we3k through absenteeism, but that, of course, was a very small thing compared with the few shillings he had been referring to. (Laughter.) A portion of the loss was inevitable, as the epidemics accounted for some of it, but very much of it ought not to be. The loss he had mentioned was the case in one school, but all the schools weee suffering more or less, and Father Wilsonhad laid his finger on the weak- ness. They had not sufficient attendance officers. It was a very serious question as they were losing Some thousands of pounds per annum through the bad attend- ance. They ought to make provision for two additional officers, as it would repay them immensely. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said he would like to point out that if the school attendance work was carried out with perfect efficiency they would not have accommodation for the children. He thought the Committee ought to have the questionof the provision of additional accom- modation well in their minds as undoubtedly the, schools were suffering by reason of the overcrowding of the existing premises. Mr. PLINSTON, speaking as a member of the School Attendance ' Committee, said they would have to seriously consider the question of providing further accommodation, and especially so if they were to make their officers visit from house to house. If they found the children who were not attending school, where were they to find accommodation for them? The CHAIRMAN : You will have to build. Mr. UPSON : There were 2,000 empty places in the schools last month.</p>
        <p>Councillor ELLISON remarkesi that the question of school accommodation was receiving the attention of the committee. In dealing with the question of attend- ame they had the difficulty of accommodation facing them. They had often cases before them where there was a difficulty in finding children schools to place them in, hut he hoped that state of affairs would soon be remedied. (Hear, hear.) Recently they had had two of their officers on the sick list, and that had been a great drawback. The minutes were carried, and the proceedings termi- nated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The Boarding-out of Children.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The Boarding-out of Children.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Board was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday afternoon under the presidency of Mr. J. Stokes. There were also present : —The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. A. R. Norman (vice- chairman), T. Baxter, C. E. Linaker, T. Wright, S. Taylor, T. Lea, T.Wood,T.Antlvis,,J. Moseley Williams, G. Pilling, S. Woodward, T. Darlington, J. Whitlow, H. Mawdsley, S. Horton, T. Lewis, J. Mount, 4d, J. L. Jones, J. T. Collier, E. Clarke, C. Roylance, A. G.Guest, J. Mclntyre, J. Douglas, R. Billington, E. Bretherton, J. Edwards, J. \V. Baguley, J. Bolshaw, A. Eaton, W. Turner, G. F. Ashton (clerk), NV. E. Hough •(assistant clerk), T. Cooke, J. 'Riley, and W. de Prez k relieving officers), and J. Cunnah (master).</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. KNOWLES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Peter Knowles wrote from the Old Farm at I Appleton to the clerk regretting to inform him that his father (Mr. Joseph Knowles) passed away on Sun- day afternoon and adding that the funeral would take place at Hill Cliffs on Wednesday. The Chairman said he felt there was a cloud hanging over the meeting that day,the like of which wa3seldom experienced. As far as his recollection went death had never visited the council to the same extent in any twelve months as it had done during the past year. Mr. Knowles was a man whom they all honoured and respected and he had rendered public services which deserved the hearty I,nd sincere recognition of all. He moved that a letter of deep cemlolence be sent to the widow and family apd he hoped as many of the mem- bers who &amp;mild would attend the funeral. • Mr. Woodward seconded and said the news of the death of Mr. Knowles came as a great blow to him.. He had learned to respect Mr. Knowles most highly for his. geniality and uniform kindness. The District Council</p>
        <p>and Board of Guardians had lost a most valuable mein her and not the least sad feature of his death was the fact, that they lost one of the oldest members of the authorities.</p>
        <p>Air. Baxter endorsed all that had been said and the resolution was carried by the whole of the members standing.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED _LABOUR COLONIES</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Scorer, Clerk of the 1-3;;Tior the quarter Sessions of parts of Lindsey, Lincolnshire. forwarded a copy of a long and important report of a Special Com- mittee of that Sessions Court referring to the present. methods of dealing with offences against the VagrancY Acts, and proposals to amend the law by the establish- ment of labour colonies upon the lines found to be so successful in Belgium. I</p>
        <p>OFFICERS' SECURITIES. The Local Government,Board wrote stating that they had had under consideration the question of the guaran- tee policy which might be taken by Boards of Guar- dians as security for the good conduct of any officer employed by them, and required to give such security. Under existing regulations it had been open to a. Poor Law authority to accept the policy of any company or association which undertook to guarantee the good conduct of poor law officers but that ,CraS subject to the condition that the form of the security should be one approved by the Board or their predeces- sors. The Board thought it unnecessary to retain this condition and had issued an order amending the exist- ing regulations in that respect.</p>
        <p>A NEW NURSE. The Northern Workhouse Nursing Association recom- mended Mrs. Mary Muir to fill the vacancy for a nu,rge,„ at the Workhouse at a salary of £3O per year Iv"' uniform:</p>
        <p>• It was decided that Mrs. Muir should be appointed on these terms.</p>
        <p>WHO SHOULD PA l' THE CAB FARES? The of Chorlton Board of Guardians sent copies f minutes in which the Clerk reported that`the opinioeie' Counsel wasthat members of the AssessruentCommitLee were not legally entitled to incurfthe expense of cab farv; when inspecting properties under appeal. It was decide". "that steps be taken to circularise the .varioes the unions throughout the country with view of asking the Government to introdel into Parliament at an early date a short Bill which W_Aiji remove the restrictions now placed by certain Le"' Government Board auditors upon any reasonable esk- penditure of the Guardians, such expenditure theugu not strictly legal having for its object the benefit of the community generally." The matter was referred to the Assessment Con- m ittee.</p>
        <p>THE COUNTY CALL. The call for the County Rate contribution for the Union for the current half year was forwarded by Reginald Potts on behalf of the County Council. total call amounted to £5,197 ss. Bd. and was a ultf' decrease of £ll5 9s. 10d. on the call for the last 11!14 year, although the amount for elementary educalen was an increase of £466 9s, 11d. THE BOARDING OUT OF WORKHOUSE CHILDREN. , In accordance with notice handed tq the Clerk Nr: Spruce moved that a resolution passdd at the monthly meeting deciding that the Workhouse children should be boarded-out, be rescinded. He contended that the resolution was not a clear expression of thf, opinion of the whole Board, as only twelve member' voted for it. It was not anticipated by some of the Guardians that the question would be finally settled °II that occasion.</p>
        <p>Mr. Baxter seconded and said he did not think allj, good reasons were giveq.at the last meeting for nlak°l such a great change. Tffe expenditure of the 13°"r,„ would, he considered, be increased about £6O per anon." by the adoption of the boarding-out system. This sl capitalized would represent an aggregate expenditure ° about £3,000 and he did!rtot think the time was opportuPe for embarking upon such an expensive scheme. Large er penditure was already being incurred on the isolation hospital,and the Education rate was pressing heavilY 11 the ratepayers at a time when they were not•very able to bear the increased expense:(Hear, hear). Re "led not think that the sentinzent which had been introdue into the discussion with regard to the '" poor children was particularly called for because the children were better off than most of the working-people's childrelif outside the House. (Hear, bear). More moneY !Id week was being spent upon them than labourers afford to spend on their children. Mr. Baxter denied that the children who were brought up jell:re- Workhouse generally returned in later years. „ „11,1,—e was only one such case in the house at present, anti-- lad had lost his arm. Mr. Wood pointed out that other Boards had 100, since taken the course which the members adopted ti'd the last meeting, and before very long that Board n'°_e be compelled by the Local Government Board to do O._ same. Most large Unions had already erected holn7 for the children and if that Board were compelled to carry out such a scheme the cost would far exceed per week. At the present time they could only, in .17 labourers and drivers of canal boat hems of the Cl'', dren. (Cries of "No "). His experience on the 1101'; Committee during the past two years showed that '"-ri majority of the boys had come back to the House Tell, the complaint generally was that the work to wii,"ee they had been put was uncongenial. The eounalit'ry immediately dispatched them to the North Sets ilsuL or to training ships, and the cost of maintenaPet,need training ship was 75. 6d per week. Mr. Wood ills—Aians a case where one family alone had cost the Guar7 ed nearly £l,OOO and said that amount would have deft' Y the cost of sending all the children to homes. ro- Mr. Norman urged the members to look at the P.O, posals as a national and not a purely local clues? be The additional cost of boarding out children woulo, very small and the benefits would he very great; and lw felt he dare not vote against the scheme • d h°'lld Mr. Mclntyre, whilst agreeing that the Boar s re, do their best for the children committed to their hOd said labourerswho earned about 18s. per week tuld six or eight children of their own would be called veer to contribute to ,the• rates in order to raise 13°111) children above the level of their own. do 0° Mr. Woodward said it was their sacred duty to .ise best they could in the intere3ts of the Workh°ll` se children, and they would not be doing it at the esPewbo of men who were earning 18s. per week. A 1°3'1,4d paid a rental representing a ratable value of £l6 w'',tro. only contribute one penny per annum towards the e,--not expenditure entailed. Hard and fast business ough‘who to throw out sentiment in such a matter. Children fee, were brought up in the Workhouse,he contended, o'l' reverted to pauperism. t the The Chairman supported the resolution carried a last meeting. etb The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi made a powerful appeal to members not to reject the scheme. r Mr. Linaker was of the opinion that if the numb:tight</p>
        <p>inmates in the Workhouse 'Were reduced,the Board Ots'Aiot not to go on increasing or even maintaining the be staff. On the understanding that this matter w9tl,,.tait taken into consideration he supported the boardleb proposals. After further discussion Mr. Spruce's resolutioe carried by 17 votes to 13. FINANCE, 1,009 Mr. Woodward reported that the balance in the A' of the treasurer amounted to £993 9s. 3d. This concluded the business of the meeting.</p>
        <p>Should Capital Punishment Be Abolished2jer The subject of " Capital punishment " was in discussion at the secotid meeting of the sessietla connection with the Fairfield Literary and Peb'ev• Society on Monday evening. The President (the "ad A. S. (2ameron) presided. Mr. I. C. Pharaoh °/-:01 the question, by reading a very carefully preParfwe49 argumentative paper, showing the unjustifiar wa4 of capital punishment. He contended that 148, sacred, and it was beyond the p)wer of any alltir 1-00," to take it ; also that lesser punishments, if trie; tof he equally as effective as death. It fell to the,oto- Mr. F.. flaigh to commence the arguments .laa wal4 of capital punishment. He argued' that haugltit to not merely a punishment for .critne committed, deter others from committing crimes ; that it ,F. 419 br dangerous to the public at large to trifle with jet! punishments for so serious an offence ; and 00°1104 that until a better method could be found, should not not be done away with. After a very it'll' tiOiti ing debate a vote was taken, when it was founteto the majority were in favour of the present ?-4P't capital punishment. .</p>
      </div>
