      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>LORD ROBERTS AND A ST. HELENS VETERAN. Patrick Gaffey, aged seventy-four, of Stone-street, St. Helens, has, by the kindness ,of the Commander-in- Chief, Lord Roberts,had his pension increased fromls.to is. 6d. per day to prevent his going into the workhouse. Gaffey joined the army in 1856, and has the remarkable record of serving eighteen years in India out of nine- teen years' service. Gaffey was in the Black Watch at the Relief of Lucknow, and served under Lord Roberts when he won the Victoria Cross at the storming of Delhi. Latterly Gaffey has "earned a living" by break- ing stones for the St. Helens Corporation, but for some months past he has been unable to work. A Sr. HELENS BLIND MAN'S STRUGGLES. Mr. A. Lancaster, the St. Helens librarian, related an interesting Incident that had come under his notice in connection with the gift of Miss Hornby, of Liverpool, of a number of books for the blind. -Mr. Lancaster said that a few days ago he met a blind man with a dog in the Central Library, and the man told him that he had for some time been making use of the books in the library, and he knew the way there perfectly, but he had a blind friend living at Blackbrook who was very anxious to get the books, but he did not know his way. He had, therefore, got a dog, and was teaching it the way to the library, and hoped that very soon his Mend would be able by means of the dog to safely cover the mile and a half between his home and the Central Library.</p>
        <p>COTTAG E GARDENING AT HavnooK. An interesting gathering took place on Saturday night at the Haydock Colliery, Schools,, when Mrs. Charles Pilkington presented. ambulance certificates and also prizes which had been given by Messrs. Evans and Co. for a cottage garden competition amongst their tenants. Colonel R. Pilkington, M.P., spoke of the value of ambulance work in a colliery district like that, and presentedto Dr. Hayward a gold medallion, and thanked him most heartily for the care and kindness be had shown in instructing the members of the class. An enjoyable concert and tea formed part of the pro- ceedings.</p>
        <p>PRACTICAL ADVERTISING.—NIeiSrS. Mather and Crowther, of New Bridge-street, E.C., have just issued the new edition of their annual Press Directory and Advertisers' Guide, "Practical Advertising." As heretofore, the work contains a preface of articles on subjects of interest to advertisers. The newspaper lists and indices have been subjected to complete revi- sion, and the publishers have endeavoured to enhance the value of the book to 'advertisers by including a complete list of Bill Posters in the United Kingdom/. In short, they have spared no trouble to make the book ndispensable to those for whom it is designed. A girl in a village near Bremen awoke on Sunday out of a trance which has lasted fifteen years.</p>
        <p>The Mayor's Magisterial Duties. • ! The Mayor (Alderman Bolton) presided for the first time over the business of the Police Court on Monday. On behalf of the members of the legal profesSion Mr. Browne congratulated Alderman Bolton on his appoint- ment to the high office, and said they were sure he would carry out the duties as satisfactorily as his predecessors had done. They wished him every success in his mayoralty,</p>
      </div>
