        <head>, . ~. 4 '1111" :- – – ' ".----._ -- • r ts. ~.. , -_ . '---47,11,....... •AlliAliF___. •A' ~.x. -•• ---,--',...—_-- _. • 1 . t . – _ . • _ „„..,,_,......„..,,,,....,,,..„......,,,..-..--,......,............--',.....A.....,...-.... . __,_ . _ – ..... • _ . – • . . • . .-- .. . -- – • • –</head>
        <p>, . ~. 4 '1111" :- - - ' ".----._ -- • r ts. ~.. , -_ . '---47,11,....... •AlliAliF___. •A' ~.x. -•• ---,--',...—_-- _. • 1 . t . - _ . • _ „„..,,_,......„..,,,,....,,,..„......,,,..-..--,......,............--',.....A.....,...-.... . __,_ . _ - ..... • _ . - • . . • . .-- .. . -- - • • -</p>
        <p>DEBATE ON COMPULSORY MILITARY</p>
        <p>SERVICE.</p>
        <p>I Vote by the Latchford &amp; St. John's Societies Oa Monday evening, the Latchford Dabatinc,' Society and St. John's Literary Society held a joint debate at Wash-lane, the subject being "-Should Military Service be made compulsory for all young men in this Country ?" _</p>
        <p>The—fte.v. J. F. Matthews occupied the chair, and Welcomed the members of St. John's Society to Latch- brd.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. H. Brown, of St. John's, opened for the affirma- tiVe vvitha lengthy and carefnlly prepared paper. He dealt at some length with the present unsatisfactory State of the country's military organisation, referring to the unprepAredness ;n which the outbreak of the Boer War found them, and showing how, in holding their own in South Africa. they had taxed their military resources to their utmost capacity. The experi- e„sce of that war and the present .condition of the world's navies, which were rapidly growing snowed their position to be insecure. They could not 'get sufficient men by voluntary enlistment, there being frowing difficulty in obtaining recruits in spite of icnvering of standard of height, etc. He 'did not ad- vocate conscription, or any lengthened period of service but held that every young man should give a portion ?f his time to military training on something like the lines suggested by the National Service League,a. branch Of which has recently been fotmed in the district. By sending untrained men to the front in the late war a great sacrifice of life had been. entailed. Compulsory military service and drill would be highly beneficial ,t° the youth of the nation, and would go far to solve 'tie social problem, and to check the physical degenera- tion and the ravages of consumption of which they heard so much.</p>
        <p>Ajr. bre-TjetT, of Latchford Society, seconded. He Pointed out how necessary it was that this country ahould be prepared to hold its own against all comers. h was needful for them to be on the alert if they would defend and hold their possessions. He also dealt with the advantages to be derived from military training and discipline, referring to the low condition of .the youth of their slums. If these were taken in liand, placed under control and trained to obedience and order, they might be made good and useful citizens. Mr. Wilkinson, of Latchford Society, led for the lie.glitive, and at once disputed the assertion that military training would lead to any great physical im- provement, pointing out that in order to raise the physical standard, training and development must begin ic:mg before military age is reached. He held that we did not require a large army to defend ourselves, having less need for Conscription that any other nation on account of our insular position. We stood in no great "tiger of invasion, and an invader's troubles would only °egin when he landed on our shores. Mr. Wilkinson dwelt upon the difficulty in which an invading army Would be placed in having to operate without a base of supplies,and instanced—referring to Napoleon's -i*treat from Moscow,—how the first reverse would be disastrous. Again, he held that for present-day warfare Men did not require to be elaborately drilled and trained, 'is with modern weapons and methods it was possible !or small numbers of men to operate against large attack- ing Conscription hindered trade and 'interfered With young men when starting in life. It was unwise on diplomatic grounds, with the eyes of other nations Upon them, to increase their military strength. He also strongly depreciated militarism as a hindrance to intel- lectual proaress. Mr. W. E. Rees, of St. John's, seconded the negative. saying that conscription was necessary to continental nations because they had extensive land frontiers to defend; in this country they had no such frontiers and 80 did not need a lame army. Their soldiers, being volunteers, were far superior to men serving cOmpul- sorily, and if, instead of having their army officered by one certain social class, the way was left open, as in Other walks of life, for men to rise from the ranks by merit and ability, they would soon have an abundance of recruits of the very best class. Several members of each society took part in the sub- sequent discussion, most of them being strongly against any form of compulsory service. On the vote being taken 12 voted for the affirmative and 18 for the nega- tive, several declining to vote. The negative was declared carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Liberal Meeting at Preston Brook.</head>
        <p>Liberal Meeting at Preston Brook.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at a meeting held in the Council Schoolroom, Preston Brook, on Tuesday evening under the auspices of the Knutsford Division Liberal Association. Mr. C. Boylance presided in the temporary absence. of Mr. J. Longland, and Mr. Ziba Armitage wrote regretting hi inability inability to attend. The Chairman said he had'been a Liberal from his younger days and had always thought the Liberal party was the best. The men who had done the most for the benefit of the working classes and for the country were on the Liberal side. (Hear, hear.) They saw what the Conservative party had now brought them to, and as for Mr. Chamberlain he would tell them anything. (Laughter.) A long time ago he promised the workingmen old-age pensions, but he had not seen his way to do anything in the matter yet. (Applause 4 Mr. A. J. King, prospective Liberal candidate for the Division, moved the following resolution : " That this meeting condemns the past and present policy of ,his Majesty's Government, and declares that the revelations of the War Commission, the Education Act, and above all, the fiscal policy foreshadowed by the Premier and the ex-Colonial Secretary have destroyed any claim they may have had to the confid- ence of the country." In the course of his address Mr. King condemned the Ed cation Act and the pro- Posed fiscal change and sakT We had prospered and Would continue to prosper under Free Trade. Mr. Lloyd Jones seconded the resolution, which was carried with six dissentients. Seeing that there Were dissentients the Chairman invited anyone who was prepared to speak for the other side to do so. Remarks in support of Mr. Chamberlain's policy were made by Mr. Alfred Broome, and Mr. John Whitlo-v. Eventually a resolution pledging the meeting to do their utmost to return a good sound Liberal at the next election was pas4d. Mr. J. Longland delivered a short speech in support and said the* was every Probability that Mr. Kin!, would be the candid'hte.</p>
        <p>• .PIC.</p>
      </div>
