      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <p>COLONIAL PREFERENCE</p>
        <p>FROM THE COLONIAL POINT OF VIEW.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>NOT WANTED.</head>
        <p>NOT WANTED.</p>
        <p>Business Bargains.</p>
        <p>THE British Colonist is generally supposed to be as keen a man at making a bargain as the proverbial Yankee himself. It is some- what strange, therefore, that he seems very indifferent about the great scheme which Mr. Chamberlain has launched for his special benefit. From the 13ritish point of view, Mr. Chamberlain has admitted that some sacrifice will be necessary in order to give a Preference to the Colonies. It would hardly be worth while for us to make sacrifices for something the Colonies do not particularly care about; so let us look into the Chamberlain scheme, from the Colonial point of view, and see what it all means. We know what we are to give; we are to make a sacrifice by paying higher prices for everything we buy, and to risk the greater half of our trade and com- merce into the bargain. Let us see what the Colonies will get by all this giving on our part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Colonial Imports.</head>
        <p>Colonial Imports.</p>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN proposes that we should put a tax upon all the food we get from the foreigner, but let in Colonial food free. Therefore the Colonial producer will be able to increase his profits by the amount o the taxes, which are to be as follows:</p>
        <p>... 2s. per quarter. 5 per cent. ... 5 per cent. Now it is a very simple matter to work out the Colonial profits on the above basis. We know how much corn, meat, and dairy produce they sent us last year, and by adding the taxes to the total we get the amount of the extra profit.</p>
        <p>Corn (except maize) Meat (except bacon) Dairy produce ...</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Colonial Profits.</head>
        <p>Colonial Profits.</p>
        <p>DIRECTLY we begin to consider the figures, we see what a comparatively small amount of food we receive from the Colonies. Here are the figures for last year, and the extra profit which Mr. Chamberlain's scheme would give the Colonial grower at the expense of the British consumer: Article. Amount Imported. Extra Profit. Corn 35.000, 000 cwt. £875,000 Meat ... Z 7,500,000 £375,000 Dairy produce 0,000,000 ... £350,000</p>
        <p>Thus we see that the total extra profit under Mr. Chamberlain's scheme would be a com- paratively paltry sum of £1,600,000. This small amount would have to be divided between Canada, Australia, New Zealand, and South Africa. Supposing they had equal shares, each would receive £400,000 apiece! Mr. Chamberlain tells us that unless we are prepared to give this extra profit the Empire will fall into disjointed atoms! According to the late Colonial Secretary, Colonial patriotism can be bought ,very cheaply. As the popula- tion of the Colonies including the native races, is just over 16400,006, the extra profits of Mr. Chamberlain's Empire-saving scheme work out at 2s. per Colonist per year, which, strangely enough, is nearly the exact sum of Colonial contributions towards the Army and Navy for the defence of the Empire.</p>
        <p>Sharing the Plunder. THE most unfortunate part of Mr. Chamber- lain's scheme is that while it only provides extra profits at the absurd rate of 2s. per Colonist, it does not provide for all the Colonies, or all the Colonists, getting their fair share. In the first place, only a propor- tion of the Colonists in eaoh Colony are interested in producing foods. In the second place, while some Colonies produce a fair quantity of food, others produce very little or none at all. Here are the, figures of the value of foods imported last year into this country from each Colony:. Canada... ... £13,381,000</p>
        <p>Australia .- £3,011000 New Zealand... Z 4, 839,000 South Africa ... nil. Thus we shall see, when we work out the extra profits of Mr. Chamberlain's scheme that the two-shilling pieces will not go round equally. If we were to give an equal prefer- ence to each Colony and each Colonist they would get the following amounts: Canada and Newfoundland ... £582,000 Australia 390,000 New Zealand ..........£86,000 South Af ric a ... Z 545, 000 As it is, under Mr. Chamberlain's scheme, the extra profits arising from the preference will be distributed as follows: Canada ... ... £969,050 Australia £255,550 New Zealand ... £291.990 South Africa ... nil. Thus we see that not only is the preference to be given very small in amount, but it is unequal in its distribution. No wonder the Colonies are indifferent. But the most absurd part of the whole scheme is that while it gives the Colonies next to nothing it will cost the people of the United Kingdom £16,000,000 alear !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Colonial Manufactures.</head>
        <p>Colonial Manufactures.</p>
        <p>-- - NOT only will Mr. Chamberlain's scheme do very little for the Colonial food producer, but it would be an absolute injury to Colonial manufacturers. In order to fill up the £16,000,000 hole in John Bull's pocket caused by the food tax, Mr. Chamberlain proposes to put a tax of 10 per cent. upon ALL imported manufactures. "All" apparently includes the Colonies, who sent us £20,000,000 worth of manufactures last year. The object of the tax upon manufactures is two-fold; first, to raise revenue; and, secondly, to protect the British manufacturer against his foreign and Colonial rival. Such a tax must be Injurious to the Colonies. But that is not all. Mr. Chamber- lain at Glasgow actually threw out the sugges- tion that in return for his paltry Preference the Colonies Will REFRAIN FROM PROTECTING NEW MANUFACTURES, so as to encourage the import of British goods. This suggestion had judiciously dropped be explained away, and was dropped in the revised edition of Mr. Chamberlain's speech.</p>
        <p>Balance of Trade. Now let us see what the Chamberlain scheme means to the Colonies: Cr. Dr. 2s. per Colonist a- 10 per cent. tax oil year. manufactures. The probable loss of their Fiscal independence. As we said in the beginning, the British Colonist is as keen a-man at making a bargain as any. Is it surprising, therefore, that he is indifferent about Mr. Chamberlain's scheme</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <p>• . . . _</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday November 21, 1903.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>GOLBORNE CASE.</head>
        <p>THE GOLBORNE CASE.</p>
        <p>PERSONATION OF A DEAD DOCTOR.</p>
        <p>Committal to Liverpool Assizes.</p>
        <p>At Manchester Police Court, on Tuesday, the man who was originally thought to be the late Dr. Herbert Part, of Bank House, Golborne, but who subsequently stated that he was Dr. Hubert Part, a native of Ade- laide, Australia, was brought up on remand before tho stipendiary magistrate (Mr. Edgar Brierley). Last week, on the assumption that he was Dr. Herbert Part, not dead as certified, prisoner was charged with having bigamously married Alice Ford while his real wife, Mrs. Ellen Part, nee Ellen Kershaw, was alive. As it transpired that prisoner was not Dr. Herbert Part, and that he had not married Ellen Kershaw, the charge of bigamy was withdrawn, and he was now charged with an offence against Mrs. Ellen Part by impersonatingher dead husband, whom she believed to be alive. The I prisoner was further charged, under the Medical Act of 1858, with having falsely pretended to be a physician and surgeon, and having illegally granted death certifi- cates; but Detective-Superintendent , M'Kenzie stated that as the Medical Council did not seem inclined to take proceedings, those charges would not be pro- ceeded with.</p>
        <p>MRS. ELLEN PART'S EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>The first Witness called was</p>
        <p>Ellen Part, who stated she was married to Herbert Part, a medical practitioner, on January 13, 1900, at the Leigh Union Registry Office. She lived with him for ten days,three of which were spent at Liverpool. Then he got an appointment on a steamer, the Grangense and sailed for Brazil. She received letters from him. The vessel returned on June 2, and witness met him at Liverpool. Her husband, however, went to stay with his mother at Earlestown and they parted. She never heard of him again until she heard of his death on August 11. On October 21 Mrs. Alice Part called at her sister's house, and in consequence of that visit she went to see her on the 23rd at Prestwich. They had a talk, and then went to see prisoner at 5, St. Leonard- street, Chorlton-on-Medlock. They arrived there abcut 11 p.m. and prisoner was in bed. When Mrs. Alice Part told him there was a lady who wanted to see him he asked them to come</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>STRA&amp;GE INTERVIEW.</head>
        <p>A STRA&amp;GE INTERVIEW.</p>
        <p>The Magistrate: What happened then? Witness: Mrs. Alice Part asked 'me if he was the man who married me.</p>
        <p>What did you say? I said: He has altered, and he said he did not know who I was.</p>
        <p>What did you say: I asked him to own to the truth whether he was the man who married us both or not. What followed: He said; "It is quite right, I married you first; you are my lawful wife." Did you think he was your husband? Witness: Well, everything pointed to that. Were you satisfied?</p>
        <p>Witness: I asked him a few questions about our marriage, and he answered them all right. He satisfied me that he was the right man. Witness, continuing, said she and Mrs. Alice Part then went downstairs, and prisoner, when he had dressed, joined them. He said, "You can do your worst." Witness said she did not come so do anything, but to see if he was the same man. HIS ALTERED APPEARANCE.</p>
        <p>Did you say anything about his appearance having altered ?—Yes, but he said he had had certain things ttikea out of his head and his eyesight was better. My husband used to wear glasses, but prisoner said he did not wear them now. He also said he had lost his left leg. I also pointed out that while at Golborne he was clean shaved, and he said he had started to grow a beard a year ago. The three of us sat up all night talking the matter over to see if we could avoid legal proceed- ings. Witness added that when she left that night she had a doubt about prisoner being her husband, but when she got a letter from him she became convinced that he was. The Magistrate: You had not seen your husband for four years. Had you known him long before . you married him? , ,</p>
        <p>Witness: Yes, about eight years—fram "bc;lrig a girl. His voice and appearance had altered, but in size he was about the same. A LETTER FROM PRISONER.</p>
        <p>On i Monday, October 29, witness receiv,d a letter (produced) from prisoner by post. Had you given him your address? Witness,: He asked if I still lived at 4, Harvey-lane, Golborne, and I replied "Yes." In consequence of the letter, witness again went to prisoner's lodgings in Manchester, and waited till he came in at ten at night. Prisoner asked if she had brought detectives with her, and she replied in the negative. He then said: "I have done you a great wrong. lam sorry for it. I have been punished, and am still being punished."/</p>
        <p>HIS LAWFUL WIFE</p>
        <p>Did you think he was your husband then?</p>
        <p>Witness : Yes; I theri felt quite -co-nftdent he was my husband. Afterwards he asked me to stay the 'night, saying I had a right to do so, as I was his lawful wife. I said I would sit up in the chair' but he said, "We will go to bed together. You are my lawful wife." He seemed to know so many details about Dr. Part's life at Golborne that I believed him, and I stayed with him that night. They breakfasted together in the morn- ing, and went out together. He left her with Alice Part, saying that he was going to Leigh about some money due to Dr. Herbert Part., and he would share it with both of them. To Alice Part he said: "You see the girl has us in her power." Witness, proceeding, said that an a later occasion prisoner told her and Alice Part to do their Worst as he was willing to faceany punishment. He also asked them to pay for his lodgings, or else he would have to sleep out. Afterwards witness and Alice Part went to the Town Hall. Alice Part lodged information, and a warrant was issued against prisoner. The Magistrate : Only on this occasion you slept with prisoner ?-7Yes. _ _ - -</p>
        <p>Whit do you think of the matter now? Do you think he is Herbert Part or not ?—No, I don't think he is.</p>
        <p>HARD TO CONVINCE. Superintendent M'li4nzie : She was very hard to be convinced, sir. On Friday night, afterthe prisoner con- fessed to me, she still persisted in saying that he was the man.</p>
        <p>• Witness : There were things he told Alice Part be- fore I saw him.</p>
        <p>The Magistrate: You mean there were many things combined to lead you to the belief? Yes,things he told Alice Part and things he told me. rho Magistrate: However you have a doubt now?— Yes.</p>
        <p>Have you a doubt whether he is your husband or not, or are you sure he is'not ? I dont feel sure he is not, I feel a little doubt about it.</p>
        <p>Was it your belief that he was your husband, and in ecinsequenee of his representing himself as such,that you slept with him ?—Yes. CROSS-EXAMINED BY THE PRISONER.</p>
        <p>The prisoner then cross-examined witness. "Supposing," he said." my sister had met you, and said she was your sister, would you hate acknowledged her ?—No, I don't suppose I would. As far as nay features, build, and vocabulary are con- cerned, are•they like those of your husband ?—Yes, I said the build was, Ant I like your husband now ?—Yes; you are the same height and build, only smaller in features. Is my forehead 6hesame ?—No ; but I explained that to you.</p>
        <p>• If anyone else rams and said he was your husband, would you recognise him as such ?—No ; but you said you had married me. Did you say at our first meeting, in the presence of my wife, that you didn't come to dome any harm ? Yes.</p>
        <p>Did you say that since I had lived with my wife longer than I had with you, I could still live with her, and you would pursue your own course ?—No. Why did you want to claim me so badly ?—I didn't want to claim you so badly. Why did you come and seek me? Was it because I said I was your husband ?—Well, I could not do other- wise than come.</p>
        <p>Do..you remember saying that you did not believe me to be the man, and why did I not free mysel.f, as you would be all right, and I could live with my wife ? No. I don't.</p>
        <p>You did not believe your husband to be dead ?—No.</p>
        <p>Were you engaged to another man ?—Yes. -- Prisoner: Were you engaged to be married until this bother arose, believing your husband not to be dead? IVitness : Yes. Since your husband died have you had a baby ? Witness : Yes. The Magistrate: When? .Witness: Tl-e 10th October, 1902. DOUBTED DEATH CERTIFICATE.</p>
        <p>Further answering the magistrate, witness sa id that she received a certificate of husband's death, but people kept telling her that they bad since seen him at Manchester and Liverpool,se that she was not convinced of his death. She heard that he was in Wigan Infirmary nine months ago.</p>
        <p>The Magistritte (to the police-superintendent): Have you anything to ask her? Superintendent M`lienzie: No; I think she has told her story pretty straight, sir. La'er on Mr. M`Kenzie said there was absolutely no similarity in the handwriting of prisoner and that of Dr. Part. Dr. Part addressed witness as "My dear</p>
      </div>
