      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <p>' LETTERS TO THE EDITOR of which I presume arranged the contract with t h ' Liverpool agent of Pearson and Knowles; I have never 0- ...---------- -- - - - -- --- -- - ------ spoken to a nwmber of the Council or to an official of the Authors of letters intended for publica.tion must in all Corporation or to anyone else on the subject of any , , cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a such contract; on the other hand, I spoke and voted in guarantee of good faith. April last against the increase of the Gas Manager's Matter intended for publication must be written salary, surely a most unwise thing to do if I wanted to on one side only of the paper and sent in, if possible,n.ot influence orders for Pearson and Knowles! Further a later than Thursday. year ago' took counsel's opinion upon the subject putting -- the whole circumstances before him, and he advised that Miss Robinson and Conscription, neither legally nor morally could my position be assailed. Finally the whole case drawn up by an oppo- Sir,—The Secretary of the National Service League nent was fully put before every elector of the Town in his letter published in your last issue, remarks Hall Ward the. da before m election. I never aske of my address to the Warrington Women's Liberal a single votehr toy vote for me, yetl with all th Association that it shows "a complete ignorance of facts before t em, my constituents elected me by an facts." He fails however, except in one instance, to point out wherein this ignorance has manifested itself overwhelming majority. I trust I have now laid this year at any rate.—' amther for another y Scc., and he contents himself with that "vague denunciation" ghost ' of which he also accuses me. The error referred to. is JAMES DODDS the misuse of the word Conscription, where I should November 20th, have said " universal military service." I regret having made it, but a slip of this nature is not uncommon in public speaking. Is Mr. Tighe quite sure that his own facts are right ? He states, that universal military service has everywhere been reduced to two years. I venture to think that he is much mistaken, but I have Small-pox Cases.—A High Death-rate. no authority at home to consult at,the moment. Of the seventy-six great towns of England and Wales, Mr. Tighe's charge of ignorance appears to arise from Hornsey, with 6 per 1,000. had the lowest annual the fact, that I differ from him fundamentally, on death-rate last week, and Warrington, with 311' the question of ak/ compulsory military service. He had the highest. The deaths in Warrington included ascribes to it, the self-control, methodical work, sobriety ;12 from measles and two from whooping cough. and cleanliness of the German nation. Why then do The total number of scarlet fever patients in hospital not all thenations, where the system prevails, manifest . during the week was 52,but fortunately the disease is these qualities? He asserts that because an advance in of a mild type. It is a rather unpleasant fact that the civil and religious liberty synchronises with the intro- ! deathrate should continue so high, it having reached a duction of universal service. therefore the latter is the figure at which it stood in the old days prior to the cause and the former the effect. My contention is, that appointment of a medical officer of health. Owing to where compulsory military service exists, there can be , theprevalence of infectious diseases it has been found aato true religions or civil liberty. Inecessary to close two additional schools. It is in the nature of all military ser- Since spring the town has enjoyed an imminity from vice, that men's right to the exercise of smallpox, but four cases have been brought to light their reason and conscience, should be abro- 1 during the past week. The patients were promptly re- gated. Obedience is the supreme virtue of the moved to the sanatorium at Great Sankey They were soldier, to it he must sacrifice his religious and intel- all residents in Orford Ward and it is surmised that the lecetual convictions. It is one thing, when men for disease was introduced into the town by a tramp who glory, pay or patriotism voluntarily place themselves in was not detected. this servile position; it is altogether another when men are forced into it, against their will. Again, mili- tary training is training in the art of human destruc- tion; from such work many naturally revolt, and no man has a right to insist that they should engage in it. Weall owe a duty to Society and to our State but . there are other and higher duties than those rendered by the soldier. Construction is nobler than destruction, and the safety of the State in the long run depends far more on the moral character of its citizens than on its military forces. There is an alternative to this universal militiism. Why not substitute the law of amity, for the law of enmity. All nations are interdependent, all are brethren, all have one Creator, one Father. The law of doing unto others as we would be done by, is as appli- cable to nations as to individuals. Treaties of arbitra- tion with the other countries of Europe and with the United States,suchas that, which we have just con- cluded with France, would set us free front this night- mare of the dread of invasion, and would do away with the sup Posed necessity for increased armaments. They would on the contrary enable Europe to lessen her gigantic armies and to rolieveher oppressedpopulations from the terrible taxation for military purposes, which has led one writer to say, that" all Europe would soon be sitting bankrupt in front of a barracks." If only the earnestness, energy and self sacrifice of ' the younger members of the "National Service League" were turned in the direction of fulfilling the higher law of nations,how much they might accomplish for a world weary of this old barbaric yoke of militarism! As it is, they are unconsciously fostering the spirit of enmity. I am quite aware, that their proposals are very moderate compared with Continental compulsory service, yet from their Secretary's open admiration of the Continental system, it is evident that their desires go further than their proposals, and these may be only first steps on a downward path.—Yours etc., ELLEN ROBINSON.</p>
        <p>Newsham Drive, Liverpool. [Correspondence on this• subject must now cease.— En. "Ex."]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Councillor Dodds and Mr. A. H. Crosfield.</head>
        <p>Councillor Dodds and Mr. A. H. Crosfield.</p>
        <p>Sir,—At last a critic has ventured to give his name ! Such a correspondent I have undertaken to notice ; therefore reply to Mr. A. H. Crosfield. Not to his perspnal attack of course ; I have no per- sonal quarrel with any man, and I leave the public to decide whether Mr. A. H. Crosfield's personal refer- ences are or are not entirely unworthy of a man who aspires to represent Narrington in Parliament. Yet there is "method" in Mr. Crosfteld's " madness," his object being to draw your readers off the scent. On Saturday week I impeached the local leaders of the Liberal party in regard to the Orford Ward election of the 2nd instant. I asserted that there was not a single circumstance of thatcontest that was creditablci to the Liberal party; that the opposition to a genuine pro- gressive and representative Labour Candidate, in the person of Mr. F. Stott; and the more than questionable methods and means employed to compass his defeat, were such as no true Liberal could look back upon with- out shame and remorse. The impotency and degradation into which the Liberals of Warrington, led by Mr. Crosfield and his satellites, have allowed themselves to gradually fall during the past few years, are the terrible conse- quences of the blindness and the perversity with which these leaders have backslidden from those principles of probity and - purity in all public concerns which have ever been among the most prized traditions of the Liberal party. The complicity of its representatives in the Town Council in the dis- reputable methods by which an unpopular Water Bill was promoted and carried, and which methods were publicly censured by the Chairman of a Parliamentary Committee ; the gradual decline of scrupulousness among its members in the Council resulting from and festered by the political compact,and followed byever widening moral laxity and the alarming development of immoral trading, all rendering easy such deplorable consequences as the Latchford election of last year, in connection with which Temperance leaders of Liberal views, brought contempt upon the Temperance Cause by siding with and supporting men who draw their living from the Liquor</p>
        <p>The result has been, in short, that men who give sober consideration to these things have come to despise and to hold in contempt those electors of both parties who in order to defeat the cause of pure, honest local government in Warrington have agreed to swallow their own convictions and to work hand-in-glove with men whose views they both dislike and detest. Not only in Latchford last year, but in Howley, St. John's and Orford this year there has been the same pitiable exhibition. Warrington has, in fact, earned for itself owing to the shallowness and hollowness of its political leaders such a character for demoralization and corrup- tion as will stick to it for a generation. And yet Mr. A. H. Crosfield has nothing but insult to offer to those who in two short years, and at immense sacrifices of time and money,have killed the compact,driven out of public life some of the most pro lific of the traders and created such a healthy public sentiment as will if kept alive and alert, prevent the recurrence of the illegal and immoral doings and the contract and other scandals which have shocked the town, and indeed the whole country. On the other hand after claiming to have been neutral,tin other words to have been on the fence, in'regard to the Non-trading cuuse, he now dares to flap his wings and crow on the strength only of the retention of oue Liberal seat in Orford Ward, and that by a victory which (owing to the means that secured it) was worse than a score of defeats. Let him not,however,be too cocksure; let me tell him that all the three candidates for the two seats at present vitcantare thorough Non-traders. And let him also remember that the cause of municipal purity in Warrington will never be deserted until every trader and every sympathiser with the trader is driven out of public life. Till that hour it will be war to the knife anti the knife to the hilt. I must free the rank and file of the Liberal party and also indeed the rank and file of the Conservative party from any complicity whatever in wrong doing. The working men of Warrington to a man I believe desire only pure and honest government, and they have shewn a grasp of the grave issues involved and an appreciation of the position taken up by the leaders of the purity cause and of the motives which have inspired them which contrast vividly with the suspicion and theimpu- tation of so many of those above them in the social scale. And in one personal word let me assure them of my eternal gratitude. They know I want nothing for myself, that I intend, as soon as the purification of the Council is completed, and this is now insight, to return to that privacy which for 23 out of the 25 years. I have been associated with Watrington, I have preferred and still desire. Of course some capital must be made by Mr. Cros- field out of the hung-up inquiry. lam not disposed to flatter or depreciate any Commissioner. I have striven to bring immoral trading to an end; and that has been my chief aim rather than to pillory guilty parties. At the same time I have, in deference to a public demand, done more perhaps than any other man to bring about an inquiry. The members of the Roberts family in the Council did all in their power to defeat me; they threatened with legal proceedings the Non-traders who supported the ultimately arranged inquiry, and they alone voted against it. How it comes that they have changed their attitude; why Mr. H. Roberts is now clamouring for the prosecution of the inquiry; and why thetneutral (?) Mr. A. H. Crosfield is so jubilant. I think I could guess. Is it because they surmise Non- traders want to shirk the issue? If so they will be Frievously disappointed. The Non-traders intend the anqu iry to proceed; the health of their most important Commissioner is not yet such as to enable his medical attendant to allow him to return to his place among the Commissioners. So soon as it is the proceedings will be resumed, a compromise I hope arranged as to reporting, and the work of the Commission carried to completion. One word more, T see that the question of my" in terest "in a Pearson and Knowlescontract is once more raised. Need I say that I have no interest whatever in any such contract. I sold all my shares in Pearson and. Knowles before I entered the Council; my remunera- tion does not depend upon the !business done by the Cfmpany, I am not on the Gas Committee, the members</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist." "Friendlies" in local football are fortunately scarce nowadays, for there is precious little interest taken in a game unless the contest is for points. Perhaps I am mistaken in attributing the lack of interest to the fact that the match is merely a " friendly." If the visiting club were Leicester, Newport, or Blackheath there would doubtless be a crowd of more than ordinary dimensions, but a " friendly " usually means a game with some more or less insignificant club, which has been arranged as a stop-gap on some vacant date. Such was the case at Warrington on Saturday, and the spectators turned up in very small numbers to witness the conquest of Seaton. The stay-at-homes were good judges too, for there have not been many poorer or less interesting games at Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>I suppose you can't expect a prominent League team like Warrington to exert itself very much in a contest of this description, but the men might make an effort to play football. That is what they failed to do on Saturday, and Seaton were only defeated by the margin of 5 -points to 3 points. To scramble home and secure full pay was about all the home lot cared about, and there were more than a few people watching the sorry exlfibition who prayed earnestly that the Cumberland men might win. They came very near doing so when a goal-kick was all that divided the teams.</p>
        <p>The Seaton men entered the enclosure promptly to time, and despite the long railway journey from Cumberland, they appeared very fit and spry. Their football outfit was something to look and wonder at and certainly suggested a fondness for colour on the part of those responsible for it. There was a chocolate-coloured jersey with a yellow band falling obliquely from the right shoulder to the left side of the waist. Then there were scarlet stockings of the br:ghtest hue, and pants varying in colour from light to dark blue. The players received a hearty, recep- tion, and the home captain winning the toss, played with the advantage of a slight breeze in the first half.</p>
        <p>The Seatonians went off with a rush, and it was soon evident that the visitors were determined to give the home team a shaking. Hallam was repeat- edly called upon to defend. The Warrington halves were given numerous chances behind the scrimmage, but the passing was often inaccurate and ill-timed and many good open- ings were lost. It was not until near the close of the first half that Davies wriggled his way through the opposing backs and serving Harris with the ball near the line that player scored a try near the corner. On the change of ends it looked as though Warring- ton would materially increase their score, but the keen tackling of the Cumberland men and smart following up kept Warrington out. Hallam then made his only mistake in the match. In misfielding the ball he let in the visitors, who were on the ball in quick time and scored a meritorious try. .The kick at goal, like that of Warrington's, failed, and the teams were once more on level terms. The home team tried desperately hard to break through the visitors defence, but they were handicapped by losing one of the forwards. the visitors on the other hand played five three-quarters and spoiled the combination of the Warrington backs. Hallam endeavoured to kick a goal from a penalty but failed, but later on, Cook, to the joy of the spectators,placed one over the bar and won the match.</p>
        <p>The League table underwent some alteration on Saturday when itull Kingston Rovers regained the leadership by a win over Keighley, and Oldham lost a point by drawing at Batley: Runcorn receded further after meeting Wigan, the Coal heavers secur- ing a five points to two points win. On Monday Leeds vanquished Wigan 6-3 at Leeds in a postponed match.</p>
        <p>The league table up-to-date is as follows : For. Against. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. Pts Hull Wen R 11... 8... 2... 1...12 22 90... 4 5-23...17 Oldham 10... 8... 1... 1...10 16— 68... 5 7-31...17 Salford 10... 7... 2... 1...20 28-124... 5 9-37...15 Hunslet 11... 7... 3... 1...14 17— 79...10 11-53...15 Bradford Leeds 10... 6... 3... 1... 7 16— 62... 7 12-50...13 Br'ghton R 10... 6... 4... 0... 9 23 87...13 10-56...12 Warrington 10... 5... 3... 2... 9,11-51... 8 7-37...12 Halif a Runcorn Hull Leigh 10... 3... 6... 1... 7 11— 47...13 22-92... 7 Wigan Huddersfild 11... 3... 8... 0... 9 17— 69...13 24-98... 6 Swinton Keighley Widnes 11... 2... 7... 2... 9 8— 42...14 26-106.. 6 Batley</p>
        <p>'rhe South-west Lancashire. League Table up-to-date is as follows : • Pts.</p>
        <p>'P. w. L. D. for ugst Pts Warrington-- 2 -. 1 .- 0 _. 1 8-3 .- 3 Wigan - 1 5-- 2 ._ 3 St. Helens - 0 .- 0 5-- 2 -. 2 Runcorn - 2 Leigh 3 -. 1 -. 2 ... 0 15-18 -. 2 Widnes 4 -. 0 _. 2 ... 2 12-23 -. 2</p>
        <p>Warrington have a popular engagement to-day, Huddersfield being the visitors. They always play open football and generally give Warrington a good game. They may be relied upon to do so this time, seeing that they vanquished Leigh 25-2 on Saturday. L. I. Deere scored three tries, so he will require close attention from Hockenhall, who is filling Fish's place. Warrington have a strong team picked and they ought to capture the points.</p>
        <p>The side is : Hallam, full back; Hockenhall, Isher- wood, Dickenson, and Harris, threec.tuarters ; Bate, Mahoney, or Davies, half-backs • Morrison,Boardman, Thomas, Cook, Jolley, Lunt, Edmondson and Naylor forwards.</p>
        <p>A good deal will depend on the work of Dickenson and Harris, and lam still in hope of seeing the for- mer play a true centre game and provide his wing man with proper openings. Harris can score tries if he has the opportunities and I am strongly of opinion that so far, he has not been judged on his merits. if Dickenson cannot get him in, why not let Isherwood have a try ?</p>
        <p>The" A" team have a stiff engagement at Roch- dale, and will be represented by: Knight, fullback ; Kenyon, Fillingham and Short, three-quarters ;Jordan and Edmunds, halfbacks • Harmer, Swift, Fairhurst, Forster Warburton and Coleman, forwards.</p>
        <p>A Klerksdorp telegram, dated Thursday, says: Mrs. Cronje, wife of the famous Boer general, died hare to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday November 21, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday November 21, 1903.</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR, J. SHAW GREEN, JP.</p>
        <p>TRIBUTES FROM ALDERMAN BENNETT &amp; WESLEYAN MINISTERS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. Wilkins Rees conducted an impressive memorial service in memory of the late Mr. J. Shaw Green, J.P., at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church on Sun- day evening. In the course of an able discourse, in which he paid a very fitting and touching tribute to the memory of the late Mr. J. Shaw Green; J.P., Mr. Rees said he would not attempt to trace Mr. Shaw Green's career. Many of you know what it was. And those who were never acquainted with him may find his career traced with accuracy and sympathy in the local papers of recent date. Still, I cannot refrain from certain references to his work. Surely a reference to his work in the Town Council is not out of place in this pulpit. Any man who serves the bet interests of your town on its Council may thus undoubtedly serve God. My friend</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>MR. ALDERMAII BENNETT,</head>
        <p>MR. ALDERMAII BENNETT,</p>
        <p>who was associated with Mr. Shaw Green in the Town Council for nearly ten years, and had many opportuni- ties of observing his public career, has kindly furnished me with information, of which I now gladly avail myself. He says: "As Chairman of the Finance Co- mmittee, he displayed a grip, an acumen, and an enthusiasm beyond all praise. He obviously revelled in the work, and his annual 4 Budget speeches ' were models of lucidity, sound sense, and public spirit. His assistance in regard to the purchase of the Water Works, the extension of the Borough, the negotiations with the County Council, and all the numerous progressive move- ments of the last few years, werequite invaluable. As Overseer of the Poor, he manifested equal knowledge; tact and judgment. I was also brought into fre- quent centaet with him as a member of the old Museum, Library, Art Gallery,.. and Technical Instruction Committee, the work of which was specially congenial to him, and was performed with the utmost diligence and care. I was not privi- leged to work with him upon -the Water or the School Attendance Committees, but, as Chairman of the latter he took the deepest interest in the education and the highest welfare of the poorer classes. As amagistrate, he won the public confidence, and, as one of the first presidents of the Society of Accountants and Auditors, he added lustre to a profession of which he was pardon- ably proud. ,'His resolute adherence to the view which he believed to be correct, and his ability to hold his own in any argument, Were softened by geniality and courtesy which won for him the warm regard of some of his most strenuous opponents. An abler, or a more disinterested representative the town has never had." Of Mr. Shaw Green's connection with this. church I will allow ministers to speak who were acquainted withhim for a longer period than I, a comparatively new-comer to this town, .could a necessity be. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>REV. WILLIAM BROOKES,</head>
        <p>REV. WILLIAM BROOKES,</p>
        <p>who came to Warrington 16 years ago, says, when writ- ing to me in view of this service :—" Among the many active members of the Bewsey-road Church in those days, none was more active that Mr. Shaw Green. He was always in his place in the sanctuary, both morning and evening, and was ever a good hearer of the Word, attentiveand discriminating, thus drawing out the best that was in the preacher. We sometimes differed in opinions, but he could respect another's fidelity to con- viction, whilst he was true to his own, so that such differences never severed friendship. As I knew him then, he was a faithful follower of the Christ, modest, unassuming, persevering, and ready for every good word and work." The REV. WILLIAM POTTS, _ _ _</p>
        <p>who was associated with this circuitnt a much later date than Mr. Brookes, writes of Mr. Shaw Green's interesting and fascinating personality; his gentleness, iis geniality, his delicate thoughtand consideration for thers ; and how he " abounded in knowledge and inci- ent as to almost every variety of human life and character." "My most emphatic conviction with re- gard to him, however," adds Mr, Potts, " is that he was a good.man, with a very deep andsimple trust in Christ' and love to Him." The .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>REV. THEODORE BISHOP •</head>
        <p>REV. THEODORE BISHOP •</p>
        <p>also warmly testifies to Mr. Shaw Green's unfailing geniality and, further, his command of temper when, as he says, "most men would have boiled over." Mr. Bishop also refers to his singular attractiveness as a companion and adds : "I shall long cherish his memory and always have before me the presentment of an honest man, fearless, true, manly, and withal of a wondrously kind heart." The</p>
        <p>REV. C. H. liocKEs, my immediate predecessor as minister of this church, supplies me with this most welcome appreciation : "When I came to Bewsey-road Mr. J. Shaw Green's best work in connection with the church was done, but one could not but be struck with his commanding influ- ence. The secret of it was confidence and affection won during many years of devoted personal service. The gooe Work he did at Silver-street will not be known before the Judgment Day. He knew all the young people, called them by their Christian names and was their personal friend. He often struck me as exhibiting in his character a rare combination. On the one side he was an advanced man in his views, a convinced Noncon- formist and well versed in the issues involved in separation from the State Church. On the other hand he was something fof a mystic, an antiquary, with an old world courtesy, and a rare reverence for the symbols of religion. 11!s company was delightful. He knew men and the affairs of the world, and he knew how to give and take in conversation, which made conversation a delightful recreation. But there was no subject to which he responded so readily as to the deepor things, He was, I believe, a deeply religious man, and one who lived in closo personal relations with his Saviour. And now he abides with Christ,which is better ! far better ! " The</p>
        <p>REV. JOHN GUNNELL, who knew Mr. Shaw Green for nearly 15 years, says : "He always impressed me as a man of wide intelli- gence, kindly disposition, courteous manner, devout spirit, and faithful in the discharge of all his civic and church duties—a Christian gentleman indeed. His chief sphere of church work was the Sunday school. There he rendered valuable service for many years as teacher of the Young Men's Class and as superintendent. He only retired front the latter office when failing health obliged him to do so. He won the confidence of teachers and officers and the warm regard of the chil- dren." Already from this pulpit, continued the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>REV. R. WILKINS REES.</head>
        <p>REV. R. WILKINS REES.</p>
        <p>have I paid a tribute to Mr. Shaw Green, his work, his private and public life, and his personal character. In spirit I would repeat that tribute to-night, and add to it the foregoing testimonies which have been readily sent me. Happy indeed are they to whose loved ones such heartfelt testimonies can be given! A fortnight since I referred to our friend's love for Nature. He was indeed a child of the sunlight, a child of the open air. He keenly appreciated the beauties of nature; he studied her ways; he loved her with an ardent love. The flower in the crannied wall, the insect only to be trodden under foot by the heedless passer-by, the grow- ing grass, the opening flowers, the birds and the animals, with some of which he lived on terms which might almost remind us of the sympathy said to exist between animal life and that gracious saint of God, Francis of Assisi—all were known by him, familiar to him; it seemed indeed at times as though each had for him a character the very peculiarities of which he understood. He cherished also the adoring mind. He was one of those to whom "Earth's crammed with heaven and every common bush a fire with God," I hope it will not be tonsidered that I tread forbidden ground when turn to his home life—so attractive, so beautiful in mat,y of its aspects The relations between himself, his wife, his 'children, were altogether delightful to behold and are ever good to remember. The married daughter far away was to the end his " little girl," his " baby." The daughter at home was his true friend, his stay, so perfectly trusted that, as he said, he gave his wife into her keeping with- out the shadow of a doubt. For over 30 years his wife and he were lovers. The charm and tenderness of that home life escape words, but T cannot refrain from lay- ing this poor wreath on its grave. But no—not its grave. Such love was too fair and good to die. Still One family they dwell in Christ. I should have been glad to have referred further to his love for the children and the love of children for him, his influence over young men and women, his reverence for the Lord's Day, his staunclitemperance principles his appre- ciation of the work done in this pulpit, his favourite passages of Scripture—almost an education in themselves, and his wide and accurate knowledge of the Theseand other points that might have been dwelt upon with fitness must now be left. I have be- fore spoken of his splendid struggle with the last enemy. He fought a magnificent battle for at least four long years. At last, however, he recognized, as Miss Florence Nightingale once said, that shattered health is the blessed sign both of finished and accepted service; he saw death as no monster, but a kind- hearted though dark-faced messengersent out to bring him, theheavenly Father's child, to the great home. A day or two before the last he said: " The eternal day is beginning to dawn." In answer also to his dear wife's words of encouragement—" What a friend we have in Jesus," he testified to his unshaken trust in the one only Redeemer. Once and again he asked for that ever fresh and lovely Psalm—that tender Shepherd , Psalm—the 23rd, asked for it even when his failing memory did not permit him to recall the number of it. Soon after the Good Shepherd quietly led this tiredl sheep for whom He died to the open gateway of the , fold of heaven.</p>
        <p>At the close of the service a favourite hymn of Mr. Shaw Green's, "Jest', the very thought of Thee," was sung. Earlier in the service Mrs. Hannan sane with taste and feeling "0 rest in the Lord." Mr. Wadsworth presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>The Admiralty officially announce that a system of short service for chaplains is to be adopted in the Navy. Under this a chaplain will be able to retire from the service after completing six, eight, ten, twelve, or six- teen years receiving a gratuity of from £5OO. to £ll.OO, according to length of servioe. This regulaion applies to chaplains now serving.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>LIBERAL AND LABOUR.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL AND LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Osborne and the Trades Council</p>
        <p>"That is a free country where the laws rule, and where the people are parties to the making of the kaws."</p>
        <p>I have been attacked as having been guilty of mis- representation in stating that the loeal Trades Council had prevented a certain society of workingmen from becoming allied with them on account of " the revolu- tionary character of the debates " at its meetings. It is incunibent upon me, therefore, to refer to the charge made against me by the Chairman of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council at their recent meeting as reported in the" Examiner. "I have already stated that my desire is not to misrepresent the Trades Council nor am I anxious to state " hearsay facts," as Mr. Podmore so facetiously terms them,without having some just reason and explanation for so doing. It would have been better,therefore,if, before denouncing me in , terms more strong than polite, Mr. Podinore had asked me for proof of ins- assertion. U persons in sympathy with the main objects of the Council are to be treitted with. such scant courtesy as has been meted out to me then the progressive .spirit amongst its well-wishers will be solnewhat discour- aged. I hope,. however, that Mr. Podmore will be good enough, if I prove what I have stated to be a fact, to withdraw, ot• at any rate make amends for his uricalled for and unfair chastisement. I did obtain my facts at the "fountain head," and that they are not savoury is no fault of mine.</p>
        <p>A REPLY TO MR. PODMORE</p>
        <p>It appears to be fashionable in some quarters just now to hit below the belt.l can assure the local Trades Council, hpwever, that so far as I am concerned, I shall be a sportsman and fight square,and I hope they will reciprocate that sentiment. Without wasting any more of "Mr. Podtnore's time," I will place the par- ticulars,which caused me to make my assertion,before him. Last year, in April I believe, a meeting under the auspices of the Iron and Steel Workers' Associa- tion was held in the Parr Hall, and Mr. Henry Broadhurst, M.P., was the principal speaker. At that meeting:the representatives of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council were conspicuous by their absence. Mr. Fred Stott wrote a lengthy letter to the press explaining that, on account of the political flavour attached to the meeting he" deemed it advisable not to associate the Trades Council with the meeting without having received in- structions from the delegates." That, and certain other reflections upon the character of the meeting, drew a reply from Mr. Joseph Elwell:jun. secretary of the Ironworkers' Association. In that letter Mr. Elwell stated, "In his (Mr. Stott's) letter I notice that he attributes his absence from our meeting to the fact that he could not associate with us without obtaining instructions from his Council. He can,however, rush into print and condemn us for having on our plat- form gentlemen—who in politics happened to hold. Liberal views—without either ascertaining their mo- tives for being present or obtaining the consent of the delegates of the Trades Council, whose wishes he is apparently so anxious to comply with. We do not, however,hold ourselves responsible either to Mr. Stott or the Trades Council for our actions, and repudiate their right to interfere with an organization not in any way connected with theirs. Some years ago our members wisely withdrew from the Trades Council" (Mr. Podmore will please note why they withdrew) oti account of the revolutionary character of its debates" (the italics are mine) "and because the principles advocated were contrary to the ,1 rules of our association. Our rules are based upon and uphold conciliation and arbitration, whilst the principles at that time adopted and advocated by them were quite different." That reply was not refuted.</p>
        <p>THE BUTTONS AND THE BRAID.</p>
        <p>The selection of a Labour candidate for Warring- ton during the past few days, and his arrival in our midst, has caused us to wonder whatever has possessed the leaders of the local Trades Council or the Labour Representation Committee or whoever is responsible for such action. ,We do not question the right they have of pleasing themselves but. before bringing a man all the way from Newcastle to repre- sent the Trades and Industries of Warrington and the public generally, they should first have explained the objections they have to the gentleman who has up to now fought their battles. It is surprising how bigoted of our own friends we in Warrington really are. The fact of the matter is that Warrington is not yet self-contained. We cannot do anything ourselves, we must invite others, —outsiders ifyou like,—to do it for us. For instance, and I hope this is not a useless digression, when we. want a new Chief Constable we can't find ens in War- rington; when occasion • arises for us to fill the positions of Secretary of Education and his Assistant, the Electrical Engineer and his Assistant, there is no one in Warrington competent to undertake the duties so we are compelled to look elsewhere. These are not the only instances I could quote, but are sufficient to show you the predicament we are in here in Warrington. lam not insinuatinehat they are not good men fo,r the work and the best that could be got, Nit what I would like to point out is the fact that whilst we in Warrington are capable of doing the "donkey work outsiders must be brought in to wear the buttons and the braid. And now we begin to quibble and wonder whether an outsider who is not connected in any way with the town would serve us hetter than one of our own friends, who has all along shewn a disposition to help us.</p>
        <p>THE LABOUR CANDIDATE</p>
        <p>And so Mr. Alexander Wilkie, of Newcastle-cn Tyne,general secretary of the Associated Shipwrights Society has been heartily and unanimously invited by the Trade Unionists to become the Labour candi- date for Warrington. Are the Warrington electors anxious to be represented in Parliament by the General Secretary of the Associated Shipwrights' Society ? If lam not out of order, I should like to ask who is going to pay his election expenses and his salary whilst he is up in London? If the local com- mittees are going to do that, then they are more than generous. On the other hand, if his Society are spending money on him as an investment br their funds, how are they going to get a return on their capital expenditure ? Let us be open and straight- forward with him, and we too shall expect htm to ex- plain to the electorate all these things when he comes to live amongst us. Somebody asked me the other day what shipwrights had to do with Warrington. But that is a little, mean way of looking at it. Perhaps he will shew us how we can get docks and will teach us to build ships? Very likely!</p>
        <p>A CAPITAUST,</p>
        <p>We. presume--and we hope Mr.Podmore will not be annoyed at the 'presumption—that the Labour Party cannot accept the services of Mr. A. H. Crosfield because he is what is termed by Socialists, a Capitalist. There is a curse on the heads of Capital- ists, surely. But, after all, my Socialist friends—and, I, too, am a Socialist in some measure—is it not a pity that we have not instinctively grasped the maxim that we shall best serve the cause of progress—and, I trust, that is the goal we have in view—whether political or social, by striving not to displace the actual possessors of power, but to teach them to use their power for the interests of Society? A man may be a good stoker or a good platelayer, but whether the efficiency of our railway management would be improved by making him a director has yet to be proved. Let us look at the position fairly and not altogether blindly. .‘</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>NEW NAME.</head>
        <p>A NEW NAME.</p>
        <p>Mr. Podmore has been „pleased to call me a new ,name—a "Liberal apologist," what- ever that means. If Liberals need apologists then I am not a Liberal. Perhaps he will tell uS exactly, what he calls himself. It would enlighten some of his friends just. now. But whether a man calls himself—or others call him—a Liberal or a Labour candidate (or even a despised Capitalist) if be has the interests of the people at heart he ought to receive the support of the workingman. And, surely, the preference ought to be given to a local man. One is almost tempted to ask even his friends for"the spirit of fair-play" to be more freely exercised. That trait in an Englishman's character so fondly cherished by all of us, rich and poor alike, has ruled the des- tiny of this Empire Individuals have abused it and ' we condemn them. There are, however,still goad men amongst us ready to fight for the common good—and some ofthem are probably Capitalists. L.,A us judge hem even them, fairly.</p>
        <p>RE PR ESENT.tT 10 OF I NTF.R ES TS ,</p>
        <p>I Let us consider very briefly, what is right and what is wrong, not so much where he faultt lies or does not lie, in this question of selecting a [representative of the people for the people. It is needless to prove that a man who has his expenses paid directly or indirectly by any particular person or persons is of necessity'unde,r some obligation to him or them and is bound to some extent to serve the interests of those people collectively, or individually or else to forfeit the claim to future favours. A. master pays his servant's wages for services rendered. The question Maw arises as to what interest or inter-</p>
        <p>ests the man is paid to represent. Hitherto, the repre- sentation of interests has tended very largely to the present unsatisfactory state of affairs and unless the position is looked at fairly and free from bias,and the credentials of each aspirant for office closely examined wet shall in the future be in no better a position. We shall only be shifting the responsibility without securing any ben.e,ficial results. This question of representation of the people is a •difficult phe. We know what we want—the jot of the poor, brighter and happier, the burdens of the land laws removed, the taxation of ground values on an equitable basis, old- age pensions, &amp;c, &amp;c, &amp;c. Who is going to help u 5 to get them? Finally, it is no use the frades Coun cils complaining of the slow rate of progress which is being made towards an improved industrial state unless they first of all reflect whether they are not themselves hindering it by their own selfish conduct. And the sooner they rid themselves of ignoble jealousies and personal rivalries,—to say nothing of the egoism that possesses at least one Chairman—and work with others for the common good, the better it will be for themselves and for those they are supposed to represent.</p>
        <p>OSBORNE</p>
        <p>..,...,“...sm..•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>LIVERPOOL CATHEDRAL SCHEME.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL CATHEDRAL SCHEME.</p>
        <p>A Good Start Made at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Churchmen and Churchwomen of Warrington gave an evidence of their desire to support the Liverpool Cathedral scheme on Tuesday evening, when a mass meeting was hold in the Parr Hall to hear details of the movement. The meeting was held under the auspices of the Warrington Church Society, and the energetic secratary, Rev. H. A. Lester, had not spared himself in his efforts to make the meeting a success. The hall was filled in every part, and the vast audience showed an enthusiasm for the new cathedral which augurs well for its success so far as Warrington is concerned. In the unavoidable absence of the Bishop of the diocese, through illness, the chair was occupied by the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis). He was accompanied, on. the platform by the Mayor (Alderman Bolton), the Rev. Canon Willink (St. Helens), the Rev. Canon Penrhyn (Winwick). Rev. Charles Harris, M.A., and the Revs. Morley Stevenson, E. C. E. Carleton, B. Percival, W. Bmcecamp, H. A.Lester (seoretary),W. Pollock-Hill Councillor Dodd (Liverpool), Mr. John White, J.P., and Mr. C. Sawkins Burgess. Before the meeting commenced selections of music were played on the organ by Mr. Randall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>LETTER FROM THE BISHOP.</head>
        <p>LETTER FROM THE BISHOP.</p>
        <p>At the outset the Rector read the followinu letter from the Bishop :—" My dear Mr. Lester.—Will you express to the meeting on Tuesday evening, my pro- found regret at my inability to be present? I had hoped until the last moment that I should be able to Mlle, but my doctor peremptorily refuses to permit me to leave the house until the middle of, the week, as he assures ma that the results may be serious if I disobey. I have no course open but to submit. I have such recollections of former meetings in the Parr Hall, Warrington, that my disappointment is all the greater. Will you, in my name, tell those present that the two points I desired to impress upon them were these ?—First, that our new Cathedral, though built at Liverpool, is intended not for this city only but for the whole of the diocese.Warrington' as well as every other town and village and hamlet be- longing to the see of Liverpool, must regard it as their mother church, and use it as belonging to them. (Applause.) Second, that it is my earnest desire that every Church man, woman and child in the diocese should contribute to its building. We receive most thankfully the great gifts of the rich, but we believe that in the sight of God the smallest offerings of the poor are at least as acceptable. I trust that very poor may from the first regard the new Cathedral as belonging to them, and may seek to place one stone, however small, in its walls. Wishing you a hearty God speed,' believe 1113 yours ever faithfully, F. J. LIVERPOOL. P.S.—Will you thank the Mayor of Warrington most heartily from me for his great kindness an consenting to be present ? " (Applause.) The RECTOR. (continuing) observed that he was sure it had alwAy.s.glven the Bishop the greatest possible pleasure to see large meetings in Warrington, and that meeting would have done so if he had been able to be present. The Bishop had spoken of past meetings in Parr Hall, and if anything could have made him better it would have been to see the crowded attendance that evening. Dealing with the two points mentioned in the Bishop's letter, the Rector said someone told him the other day that the Bishop loved Warrington very much for he sgemed to be always here, but if he was here often, he was also often in other places. He was due there that evening, and they expected him at the Parish Church on Saturday and at St. Clement's on Monday. His lordship set them the best example in being not only the Bishop of Liverpool, but the Bishop of the diocese, and his lordship wanted them to remem- ber that the new Cathedral was to be the mother church of the diocese as well as for Liverpool. (Applause). In the second place he hoped they would be able to obtain assistance from the poor as well as from the rich—from every man,' woman and child. They wanted £200,000 before the foundation stone was laid, and a special effort was needed to raise another £25,000 to complete that sum. That ought not to be difficult in such a rich diocese as this. (-Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>SCHEME APPROVED.</head>
        <p>THE SCHEME APPROVED.</p>
        <p>A resolution, expressing the opinion that the scheme deserved the united and loyal support of the whole diocese, was unanimously carried, on the proposition of the Rev. Canon WILLINK, of St. Helens, seconded by Councillor DODD, of Liverpool. Mr. JOHN WHITE then proposed "That, in view of the need of large donations to the cathedral funds before the laying of the foundation stone, a special organiza- tion for the town and district of Warrington be formed and that the Rural Dean be requested to call a meeting of the clergy and laity to appoint a committee for this purpose." (Applause.) He observed that it was often stated when a large undertaking was in progress, whether of a religious or secular character, "What a pity to spend such an immense sum when so much money is wanted for other church purposes, for the clergy, the schools and the building of churches ! " If that argument had been made use of by their forefathers they would never have had such beautiful cathedrals as they had in England now, and of which they were so justly proud, and also of which their American cousins were so pardonably envious. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Further, he did not know that they had any guarantee if the money had been diverted, as some wished, to other sources, that it would have been spent on churches and schools. (Hear, hear.) They had only built two cathedrals in 500 years and they could not, therefore, be said to have been extravagant. (Applause.) But there was another argument, and that was that the cathedral would only be a glorified , parish church for Liverpool. But that idea had been I pretty well dissipated that evening by the various speakers, and every one in the hall would, he hoped, go away convinced that he or she had a part in the build- ing and that when it was built all would be welcome to worship in it. (Hear, hear and applause.) The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON seconded, and said the speeches to which they had had the pleasure of listening had been both stimulating and inspiring, and had aroused in them strong feelings. But they wished to put those feelings into action and to do all they could to help forward the scheme. There was nothingc more dangerous than feeling if they allowed it to evaporate before putting- it into action, and it was to prevent that disaster it was pro- posed to carry the resolution into effect. The cost of the use of the hall for the evening had been paid for by the charge made for the reserved seats, and every penny given in the collection would go absolutely to- wards the building of the cathedral. Owing largely to the energy of the Secretary a sum of about £4O had been collected outside those walls. (Applause.) Could they not make that sum into .£lOO. (Hear, hear.) If they could do so it would be a message of comfort and courage to their Bishop. (Applause.) The resolution was carried with enthusiasm and the collection was immediately taken tip. 'ILLUSTRATED LECTURE ON CATHEDRALS. _ _</p>
        <p>An illustrated lecture was then delivered la;v; the Rev. Charles Harris, M.A„ on our Englishrathedrals. The slides were shown on thescreen by Mr. H. N.lioughton Chief interest was centred in a view of the proposed new Cathedral the first which had been shown in public. Mr. Harris graphically described the new Cathedral, comparing it to that at Lincoln, as standing onan eminence in the centre of the city and forming the most striking building in the district. it, would be in every way worthy of the diocese and take a very high place amongst the cathedrals of the land. The best features of the past would be introduced, and therejtvould also be evidences of the genius of the pre- sent which was able to invent and conceive new ideas. It would stand as a witness for God to future generations. (Applause) The Rev. H., A. LESTER said that the collection had realised £25. A lady who was present had promised £25 a year for three years. They still wanted £lO more to complete £lOO and he shoitld like to get it that even- inc. (Applause.) The Lecturer's and speakers services were acknow- ledged on the proposition of the MA.YoR seconded by the Rev. Canon PENRITYN.</p>
        <p>'lt may be added that the promises received together with the collection, bring the amount subscribed and promised in Warrington already to £142 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Chapel.—Foreign</head>
        <p>Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Chapel.—Foreign</p>
        <p>Missions.</p>
        <p>The annual Foreign Missionary services were held in this chapel on Sunday. At the morning service Mr. P. H. Sutton occupied the pulpit, and in the evening the Rev. John Gunnell preached. In the afternoon the Rev. R. Wilkins Rees gave an address to the children on India. There were large congregations at each of the services. On Monday evening a public meeting was hold, when addresses were given by Mr. Charles E.. Parker, Rev. G. H. Carnson, and the Rev. John Gunnell Mr. D. I). Dunn sang two solos. The total collections at all the services anlounted to £4 19s. id.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>SHIP CANAL PROPOSALS.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL PROPOSALS.</p>
        <p>As previously stated, the M;4l-tcheste: Ship Canal Company have approached the Warrington Corporation with the view of their allowing the Manchester Shiro Canal Bill, containing the river proposals of the Com- pany at Warrington, to proceed concurrently with the appeal against Mr. Justice Byrne's decision in the dredging action. The company has asked the Corpora- tion to assist them in the promotion of their Bill, but up to the present no asso=nt by the Corporation has been given and probably no agreement will be come to on this matter until the appeal has been disposed of. The . appeal is down for hearing, and will probably be reached , within the next three weeks.</p>
        <p>The Company, in their Bill which has been deposited in Parliament propose to provide all the funds for the works to be carried out in Warrington,. although they at first asked the Corporation to advance them the money—a request which was declined. With regard to the alteration of the sewers necessitated by the scheme it is imperative that the four outfalls, now dis- charging into the river should be diverted and carried into the tidal portion of the river below Messrs. Monks, Hall and Company's works. This will necessitate the construction of a rising main which will deposit the sewage in the tidal portion of the river outsith the proposed dam. The works will be carried out by the Ship Canal Company, and the only expense which the Corporation is asked to bear is a proportion of the cost of diverting the sewers. The apportionment will be submitted to an arbitratos of the Local Government Board for settlement.</p>
        <p>The construction of the proposed dam will have the effect of providing a dock within the Borough, with a depth of lift. Gin. Under their existing obligations, the Company are bound to dredge to a depth of eight feet, but the Company hope that the works when carried out, will relieve them of the necessity of dredging.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. JOSEPH KNOWLES,</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. JOSEPH KNOWLES,</p>
        <p>OF APPLETON.</p>
        <p>it is with refit-et that we have to announce the death of Mr. Joseph Knowles, who passed away at his resid- ence, Old Farm, Appleton on Sunday afternoon. The deceased gentleman, who was 62 years of age, was well known and highly respected, particularly in- agricultural circles. For many years he had been a member of the Runcorn Rural District Council and Board of Guardians and was most assiduous in his attention to the duties devolving upon him. He had been honoured with election to the chairmanship of the Rural Council, which, of course, carries with it the privilege of sitting on the County Bench of magistrates. He also held a seat on the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee.</p>
        <p>Evidence of the esteem in which the deceased gentle- man was held was forthcoming at the funeral which took place in Hill Cliffe graveyard on Wednesday afternoon. There was a very large assembly to witness the last rites which were performed by the Rev. G. H. Carnson. The chief mourners were Messrs. John Knowles, Peter , Knowles, and Joseph Knowles (sons), Mr. Arthur Pimlott (son-in-law), Messrs. Peter Tickle, Joseph Tickle and Harry Whitlow (brothers-in-law), Messrs. James Knowles; Joseph Tickle and Ralph Tickle (nephews). Representing the Runcorn Rural District Council and Board of Guardians were Messrs. S. Wood- ward (chairman of the Council), J. Stokes (chairman of the Board of Guardians). G. Pilling, T. Lea, T. Wood, J. Douglas, J. Bolshaw, T. Baxter, T. Lewis, W. •Bradford. T. Wright, J. L. Jones, J. Spruce' J. W.Baguley, E. Clarke, E. Bretherton,W, Turner, A. Eaton, T.Antwis, G. F. Ashton (clerk), Dr. Adams (medical officer of health), J. Cunnah (workhouse master), W. R. DePre, (relieving officer), and J. Swinton (surveyor). The Warrington and District Farmers' Auction Company, of which the deceased was treasurer, was represented by Alderman Bennett,Messrs. A. Barber, W. Fairclough, R. Tickl6, Britch, A. Broome, S. Houghton,and T. Jones. There were present on behalf of the Warrington and District Agricultural Society of which the deceased was treasurer, Mr. H. B. Melville (secretary) and Mr. H. F. Greenough, and Mr. D. Parkinson and Mr. B. Shearer represented the Farmers Exchange Club of which Mr. Knowles was a prominent member. Amongst others I present were :—Alderman Hutchinson,Dr.Ferguson,Dr. Bower,lVressrsT. Hall,Part- ington,W. Houghton,B. Bra.dford,G. W. Paul, W. Fletcher, Arthur Smith, A. Browne, A. Jolley, J. Sampson, H. Nield, W. Long, J.P.. J. Tinsley, J.P., R. Shacklady, R. Gordon, H. Singleton, John Whitlow, A. Hayes, W. Hough, 1 John Darbyshire, Thomas Ford, Thomas Boardman, James Boardman, C. Bower, S. Simpson, Lewis, Hindley, Hardy, Leigh, Lear, and Nicholson. '</p>
        <p>Floral tributes were forwarded by Mr. Frederick and Miss Lewis, Mr. John Highfleld, Mr. W. Houghton, Mr. and Mrs. John Wormll (New Brighton), Mr. and Mrs. Ralph Tickle, Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Percival, Mrs. Owen and Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Owen, Mrs. Barrow and family' Mr. A. Sankey, Dr. and Mrs. Bower, Warrington and District Farmers' Auction Company, Mr. William Long, Mr.; tit t ;. eLa aia;, an 4 Mr. and Mrs. T. H' Pretty'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>MRS. LE BLOND AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>MRS. LE BLOND AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Interesting Illustrated Lectures in the</p>
        <p>Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur H. Crosfield once more provided a pleasant evening's entertainment for a large assembly of townspeople in the Parr Hall on Thursday. Invita- tions had been sent to the members of the Literary and Philosophical Society, Field Club, Licensed Victuallers' Association, Cemetery Hotel Bowling Club and other organizations, in addition to local Liberals, clergy, and numerous friends, and there was a willing response. An interesting programme was submitted and was highly appreciated. The services of Mrs Le Blond, an accomplished lecturer who has previously visited Warrington at the invitation of Mr. Crosfield,had again been secured and she delighted the audience with two illustrated accounts of her travels in Spain and Lapland. At the outAet Mr. Percy Randall gave an organ recital with taste and skill, and this was followed by a well-executed pianoforte solo by Mr. Dicks, of Liverpool. Miss Marchant, of Liverpool, who pos- sesses a beautifully sweet voice of fine range, contri- buted several songs in a 'manner which gained high encomiums. Mr. Dicks supplied the accompaniments. In introducing Mrs. Le Blond, Mr. CROSFIELD said he was sure many of those present would remember the very delightful account she gave them last year of climbing in Switzerland. Whether Mrs. Le Blond had made any converts by that lecture he did not know. It might be that there were some in that gathering who had sought to win their spurs among the peaks, passes and glaciers that were so beautifully illustrated for them last year. Others were probably content to win their laurels in a humbler way. They might ramble up to the top of Hill Cliffe—(laughter)—as he had done that afternoon, attracted by the opportunity one got there of hitting "that little ball' round the very delightful course. Others might be content to take their mountaineering in a very leisurely fashion as they would have an opportunity of doing that night, just in the same way that he had known cricketers who were uncommonly good at playing the game from their com- fortable seats down at Arpley or Old Trafford. (Laugh- ter.) Mrs. Le Blond was going to give them an oppor- tunity of playing pavilion cricket, if they liked to call it so. Last year the scenes that were placed before them were to him soniewhat familiar ground, but that evening they were going into "fresh fields and pastures new." He was sure the lectures would be delightful from a literary point of view and the illustrations from an artistic point of view, and that they would •find what Mrs. Le Blond had to tell them very interesting and instructive.</p>
        <p>Mr. CROSFIELD'S prediction as to the character of the lectures was fully realised. Mrs. Le Blond held the interest of the audience throughout and was fre- quently rewarded with applause. The title of the first lecture was " Cities and sights of Spain "and with the aid of beautiful lantern views the onlookers could almost imagine themselves transported to that sunny land. All the slides were made from photographs taken by the lecturer and her husband, and many of them were triumphs of the photographic art. The -pictures of Moorish architecture, including the famous Alhambra, were particularly good. In her second lecture Mrs. Le Blond described "Exploration in the Alps of Lapland" and conveyed to the minds of the audience a vivid impression of the perils ana fascina- tion of mountaineering. Some splendid photographs sho Ring the wonderful effects of the "midnight sun" evoked general admiration. At the close a hearty vote of thanks vrts accorded to Mr. Crosfleld on the proposition of the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) seconded by Alderman. Fairclough.</p>
        <p>In responding Mr. CRosET MD paid a compliment to Mrs. Le Blond and all who had contribnted to the evening's enjoyment. Mr.ARTHUR BROWNE was an efficient director of cere monies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>MRACROSFIELD'S ALPINE ADVENTURES,</head>
        <p>MRACROSFIELD'S ALPINE ADVENTURES,</p>
        <p>Last evening a second entertainment was given before members of the various friendly society branches, and the hall was well filled. Mrs. Le Blond delivered highly interesting lecture on "Climbing in Switzer- land" and her stories of thrilling adventures held the audience spell-bound. Some mafi°nincent views were thrown on the screen. Mr. Percy Randall again gave an organ recital, and Miss Marchant rendered songs. Mr. Alf. Grounds created groat merriment with his humorous sketches. During the evening Mr. A. H. Crosfield repeated his graphic description of "An adventure in the Alps t (illustrated with lantern slides, prepared by Mrs. Le Blond) and which we published verbatim in December last. _ _</p>
        <p>Hearty votes of thanks to Mrs. Le Blond and Mr. Crosfleld concluded the proceedings.</p>
        <p>Speaking at Worcester onthur.sday night, at a co ference on the question of licensing compensation, thn Bishop of Worcester expressed alarm that politician were placing the fiscal question in the position o supreme importance. He deprecated as immoral th common tendency of temperance reformers to shoulde he blame on to publicans, brewers, and distillers. Th tuft belonged to them all as common citizens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-11-21_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>PROTECTION AND THE UNITED STATES. BY GEORGE P. GOOCH. M.A.</head>
        <p>PROTECTION AND THE UNITED STATES. BY GEORGE P. GOOCH. M.A.</p>
        <p>The flourishing economic condition of the United States is sometimes attributed tO their high tariff. A little reflection will shew that this prosperity must be explained by other causes. Any comparison between Protectionist America and Free Trade England is vitiated by the fact that they are incommensurable. The United States are about thirty times the size of Great Britain; the one is a country, the other a continent. The United States possesses every kind of soil and Climate, and their mineral resources are unequalled in extent and variety. With the Civil War began a period of expansion unparalleled in history. The American Union is the greatest Free Trade are in the world, for there are no commercial barriers between the component parts; and the rapid extension of railways, culminating in the great trans- continental lines, have opened up the grain States west of the Mississippi. At the same time, a series of inventions gave a great impetus to the industries of the Eastern States.</p>
        <p>To recall these facts is to say that the demand for labour in the United States during the last genera- tion has been unprecedented. An immense estate had to be developed. The foreign immigrants were for I the most part unskilled, and the Ameriean working man has been able to demand and obtain a h7gh wage, both industry and agriculture competing for his labour. Besides, he has shewn himself a very efficient workman. It is unquestionable that he possesses and expends more nervous energy than any other. We know, too, that drunkenness is far less Oommon than with us, and that more thought and money are spent on education than in Great Britain. Do not these considerations of themselves explainthe prosperity of the United States and its workers? Is it necessary, or even'possible, to account fur it by a tariff in face of these stupendous natural advantages The most recent, and one of the most authoritative, writers on the economic development of the country declares that the power of the tariff, whether for good or evil, has. been vastly overrated. High duties were imposed during the Civil War to raise money, and the war tariff was continued in time of peace. No such customs sysi em could have been built] up except under the pressing necessity of revenue. Many industries,partially if not wholly dependent on the tariff, grew up, and the responsibility of causing their downfall was too great to be lightly undertaken.</p>
        <p>The iffeci of the high tariff has naturally been exceed- ingly favourable to the shareholders in the protected industries. Protection means the artificial narrowing of the market ; and the narrower the market the easier it is for trusts and monopolies to arise. The dominant feature of the industrial life of the United States dur- ing the period of the high tariff has been the Trust. Protection is the mother of Trusts, and Trusts are, as a rule, only another name for monopoly. When the influx of foreign goods is stopped, the home market is left at the mercy of men who find it wiser to raise prices by agreement than to reduce them by competition. In this way have arisen • the giant fortunes which are the wonder of the world. It is often claimed that the high tariff has allowed certain industries to come into existence; and Mr. Chamberlain has recently quoted the creation of the tinplate manufacture by the McKinley Tariff. But will it be maintained that the American people benefited thereby? The exclusion of English tin-plates allowed the directors of the new- born industry to fix their own price ; and they fixed it at a sum only a trifle less that the price of the Eng- lish article plus the enormous duty. In other . words the tin-plate manufacturers take advantage of the tariff to sell their goods at a monopoly price. In whatever direction we turn, we find that Protection thus means benefit of the felw at the expense of the many, the secur- ing of enormous profits and the raisin,' of prices to the consumer. An amusing instance of the working of the system is seen in the nickel trade. A high tariff—prac- tically excluding foreign nickel—has allowed a certain individual to establish a monopoly in the home market, out of the proceeds of which he has endowed a profes- sorship at Philadqlphia, the holder of which is bound tp inculcate the virtues of Protection. We frankly admit, then, that Protection is advantage- ous to those Who are lucky enough to have invested their capital in protected industries. But what of the workers? We know that wages in England are the highest in the world, except in America ; but if we are to know whether the American workman is better off than the Englishman we must know not only what he receives in wages but what the purchasing power of his wage is. In England money wages have largely increased; and we learn from the Board of Trade Blue- book that-1100s. will now buy what cost 1405. a generation ago. In the United States, on the other hand, prices have gone up rapidly, and in the last decade more rapidly than wages. The report of the recent Mosell Commission helps us to determine the relative position of the English and American working- man. In certain trades, wages exceed those paid in England by about 20 per cent. ; but what is their purchasing power? The three chief items of a workman's budget are food, rent, and clothes. Looking down the list of answers given by the Mosely Commissioners, we find that the price of food is con- sidered to be almost exactly the same as with us. No more striking vindication of the policy of free imports is needed than that bread and meat are to be bought in England for much the same sum as in the country from which such a vast quantity of these articles come. But when we pass to rent, the report informs us that the American worker has to :pay from 50 to 100 per cent, more for house-room than the Englishman; and that lwhile it is possible to buy inferior shoddy clothing as cheaply as in England, better, warmer, and moie durable clothes can only be obtained at a price twice, or even more than twice, that which we pay in England. For these reasons the boasted economic superiority of the American workman, when closely examined, tends to disappear. Those in receipt of high wages suffer from the inflation of prices caused in the first place by the tariff, and in the second by the monopolies which grow up behind it; while for those who do not earn high wages the struggle for life is terrible. Strikes are as frequent as with us, and irregularity of employment no less. When it is said that America is in favour of a high tariff, it is forgotten that one of the two great parties of State is its sworn enemy. The Democrats have re- cently issued tables to shew that the working man is mulcted of 10 per cent, of his4earnings by the tariff. The Republican party, which has been in power for the greater part of the period since the close of the Civil War, is the slave of the great capitalists. The unholy alliance between politics and finance is the plague- spot of American life; and it is the corruption, bred of Protection, which has led men of high character to, leave politics to those of less squeamish temper.</p>
