        <p>Nell," with a small "d " for "dear," while prisoner ad-, dressed her as "My Dearest Wife," with a capital "D.' PRISONER AND THE DETECTIVES. Detective-sergeant Williams deposed that be arrested the prisoner on October 31. In reply to the charge of bigamy he said, "Let them do their worst." On Tues- day last witnesa preferred the present charge against him, and he replied, "At first I said I was not her husband, and afterwards I said I was." Detective-superintendent M'Kenzie deposed that 'while the prisoner was under remand he was taken to , the Town Hall, Manchester, and confroated with and I interviewed by witness and a number of men andl women from Golborne, Warrington and Liverpool. Afterwards witness told him that he did not believe that he was Dr. Part at all, and that in order to prevent miscarriage of just ice be would ask for a week-to-week re- mand in order to trace his whereabout since January 1900. Thereupon he made a statement, already pub- lished in which he said that he was Dr. Hubert Part, a native of Adelaide; that in Liverpool he had met Dr. Part, ' who told him all about his wife from whom he wanted to escape; that, though not qualified in this country, he had practised as a doctor' first at New York and also at Salford and Hulme; that he had not married Ellen Kershaw (Mrs. Ellen Part), and that Alice Ford (now described as Mrs. Alice Part) was his lawful wife. Witness afterwards told prisoner of the present charge, and he said, "I will say I did." Prisoner now denied having made that admission. , Superintendent M'Kenzie : You did, and you ' apologised to the girl for what you had done. You wanted to shake hands with her and she refused. The Magistrate asked if there was any evidence as to the prisoner's identity. Superintendent M`Kenzie said he had such evidence if it was required. No further evidence was given. PRISONER'S WIFE GIVES EVIDENCE. The prisoner, in answer to the charge pleaded not guilty, and called his_wife, who said that her name was Alice Part, of 28, Clifton-road,Prestwich. She deposed that she was married to prisoner on December 18, 1902, at the Parish Church Prestwich. rte was married under the name of Herbert Part. Mrs. Ellen Part had told her on several occasions that she thought her husband was not dead, and that prisener was her husband. On the night of October 23rd, Mrs: Ellen Part and witness lay down in their clothes on prisoner's bed, while he stayed downstairs. Prisoner : Did Mrs. Ellen Part suggest that three of us should sleep together. Witness: lam indeed grieved to say such is the truth. She said there was no harm whatever in it, see- ing that prisoner had married both of us. Witness added that on that occasion she wanted to go home to her mother, but prisoner prevented her. Mrs. Ellen Part told her that nothing had taken place between prisoner and her. On the 23rd Mrs. Ellen Part suggested that as witness had lived with prisoner the longest she should keep him. Witness, however, refased, and said she wouldlleave him and resume her maiden name. One day Mrs. Ellen Part would say prisoner was her husband,and the next that she did not think he was. The Magistrate: This is a case which I must send for trial. Prisoner: Do you think there issufficient evidence? I can prove I am innocent. The Magistrate: The case must be tried by a jury COMMITTAL TO THE LIVERPOOL ASSIZES. The prisoner was then committed for trial to the Liverpool Assizes. Prisonerapplied for bail, but was refused. He was allowed to see his wife, provided she desired an inter- view. AUSTRALIAN MARSH-DWELLERS EXTERMINATED. The Melbourne correspondent of the "Daily Chronicle" telegraphs that a despatch received from the Government Administrator of Papua (British New Guinea) announces the extermination of all the males save one belonging to the recently discovered tribe of dwarf marsh-dwellers. They were attacked and killed by another tribe of Papuan cannibals. The Adminis- trator has found a marvellously perfect native Albino at a place in the Trobriand Islands, off the coast of New Guinea. He is a boy, and his skiA is quite white but freckled; his hair and eyebrows and eyelashes are per- fectly white, and he has blue eyes. The British New Guinea Scientific Expedition will investigate the cir- cumstances and history of the Albino and the marsh. dwellers. , In the Chancery Division Mr: Younger, K.C., applied to Mr. Justice Joyce for an injunction to restrain a Liverpool licensed victualler named McPhail from sell- ing as Bovril a meat extract not made by the firm of Bovril, Ltd. Evidence proved three sales of another substance in response to orders for Bovril.—Defendant submitted to a perpetual injundtion and to pay cost.s.--. An order was made on these terms. CLARKB'S B 41 PILL IS are warrith7.,-. to cure, in sex, all acquired or constitutional Discharges from the Urinary Organs, Gravel and Pains in the back. Free from Mercury. Established upwards of 30 years. In boxes 4s. 6d. each, of all Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors through- out the world, or sent for sixty stamps by the makers. The Lince!n and Midland Counties Drug Company. FLORILINE i—roN 71111 TEETH AND BREATH.— Moroughy cleanses the teetli from all parasites or impurities, harden the gums, prevs.nts tartar, stops decay, and gives to the teeth a peculiar pearly whiteness and a delightful fragrance to the breath. Price 9s. 6d. for the liquid, or is. per jar for ILe Floriline " Pov, der." of all Chemists and Putumers.- 4dst VALUABLE DISCOVERY FOR THE ziAIH-11 your hair is truing grey al* white or falling off Use the MEXICAN HAIR Ri.NEWEIi, for it will positively restore, in every ease, grey or white hair to its original colour. It makes the hair charmingly beautiful, as we.l as promoting the growth. Price 3s 6d rer bottle.— al' To NIOTIIER.4.—Mrs. Wino low's Soothing'Syrup has been used for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their :hildren while teething, with perfect success. It will relieve the roors